- **The line again (59:189–247).** Oryn reads him standing, asked; nothing of his turns under her hands (the twelfth is spent); her current parts on the place as before. He gives them the count and that nothing went, of his. **r1:** "Twenty-nine tomorrow," is Vastin's, from the box; "Thirty's the month," is Oryn's.
- **Vastin's night (59:251–345).** He stays on the box where he was posted as a mark. The lines of his service (a sill, three walls, eleven floors, the rings). The second time the thing beside his face went out ("since he was fourteen" stands: it is when any sigil comes), he looked at the place where it was not and found "a man there … not an officer of anything". The stream-bed held as an inquiry: no officer would have found fault with the wall's holder; "the man whom they cleared went on sitting in the stream-bed". **What he is at dawn:** on nobody's sheet; he will take up the tarred case if they go and set it down if told. "He did not mean to ask them anything … He would not look at the six of them so." He does not guess at what is six people's; he does not finish the old sentence. Brom brings a blanket: "You're relieved."
