(the reserve's, the lightest). Vastin takes up the tarred case unasked and stands a stride behind Brom; **r1:** "He had asked for no place among them, that morning or on any other, and nobody had offered him one" (60:281).
- **Roles (all unnamed):** the nine of the pass (three bowmen, the maul-man, five with staves); Teague's tall man, bow (the broad man), boy and lean old woman; the woman and the old man at Oryn's holds (spoken of).

### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
*(Refreshed after repair r1, 2026-10-12. No fact of the calendar, the bodies, the purse, the loads, the fragments or the deployment changed in repair. What changed: the eleven restored units are now on the page with their speakers; Karis's palm "will leave a line"; and Vastin's standing is said as "asked for no place … and nobody had offered him one".)*

This is the BOOK'S exit state.

**Calendar.** The morning of the **three hundred and eighth day past the Line**, at the western rim of the fifth place, seven miles east of the gap. Days on the page: 306 (the shale camp at first light; the walk; the pass; the burial; the ridge; the open rock below the eastern mouth at dusk and through the night), 307 (the plain; the court by a little before noon; the round, about four hours; the cuts; the council; the line at dusk; the centre; the rim at night), 308 (dawn: two departures and one setting out). No season, month-name or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling; "four hundred years" only in the closing prose.

**Where everyone is, and which road each takes.**
- **Six go west from the rim, on foot, with no mule:** Seln (ahead, hands empty), Lira, Brom, Karis, Cael, and Vastin a stride behind Brom. Where "west" leads is not said.
- **Teague, the tall man, the broad man and the lean old woman** left the rim at first light, going west toward the gap and the cairn at its eastern mouth, the tall man in front and Teague last; they are bound for Lowmarch, and Teague carries Cael's letter for the bag. His term: "After the next thaw." … "Whatever's on the next line. My crew comes."
- **Oryn and the brown mule** went north along the rim and out onto the plain, toward a salt road two days up that crosses the ridge, and three holds beyond it. She did not look back. Her term: "After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say."
- **Teague's boy** lies under a cairn as high as a man's hip, on a flat of rock at the pass's eastern mouth. His name was said once, by the broad man, and is written nowhere.
- **The nine of the pass** went up to the ridge and east along it on the 306th; their marks leave the foot of their path and go south. Not seen since.
- Cleon is under Treswick; Pike at Lowmarch; Hesk at Denvash (not on the page).

**Cael.** Nineteen. **Thirteen fragments. Deployment since the Line thirty-five** (33; the frame of the second heap, 34; the twelfth turned on the court's floor on purpose, 35; the inward self-reading uncounted — ruled). Force has been used once, on timber and stone, along a shelf, at no person. **Body:** two of the old three ribs cracked along the old line and the third sore, eased and taped by Oryn ("They'll carry. They'll hurt,"); they carry a box; a fading welt on the left collar-bone; eyes paid twice in two nights and recovering. **The still place:** read inward by the twelfth on purpose (*still*; *set*; *bones*; a thing that bears with nothing built on it; a lean with no mark; "The same kind of thing, to that instrument, as the sites were to Cleon's.") and the same reading taken from the court's floor; at the centre he could find no edge to it. He has said aloud, alone, the three lines, and entered "The same kind of made." in the ledger. **He has told nobody the twelfth's reading and nobody what the centre was like.** It stands in the log of the 306th, which nobody else has read. Oryn's finding after the centre is the same as at the mill. **The Arbiter:** dark, unchanged. **The instruments in his hand:** forty-one in forty-two by the kit's cord; the Ternhall a hair and a half short; Hesk's two hairs long; ruling *hold*; no line drawn; no cause offered by anybody. He did not touch the cord at the court. He has not put the date on any page. He has not said, written or thought through the sentence that lies past the two he has. At the far mouth of the pass he understood that the line "was not ink".
- **Debts:** in money, none. Otherwise two, entered side by side again on the night of the 307th: **to Vastin (the eleventh)** and **to Teague (the thirteenth)**; neither told; he has ruled that the use of the thirteenth over Teague's head "does not pay the debt". Owed to Lira: the telling "on the road", for which no place or day is set.

**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst in the movement. Whole. She was posted at the old man's shoulder in the pass, twenty strides behind Teague's boy, and carries that ("He watched my boots"); Seln has told her that it is the entry. She was handed the log of the 306th and gave it back unread: "I don't read ahead." At the cut she said, "Every other one, yours worked and ours died," and "You think this one's different."; she was told "Wait here," and waited. She asked "Well?" once and has accepted the road. She carries the second box on her back. She has not heard the twelfth's reading.

**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. Grey, silently, in the pass after the first flight. **Left forearm bolted through the flesh on the 306th** (he was lowering Vastin and was not grey); the feathers broken off under the roof, the bolt drawn and the wound shut by Oryn afterward; in linen from wrist to elbow, to be kept out of the wet. Two older bruises "coming on nicely". He carries the heaviest box on the right shoulder, with Oryn's empty bowl under its lashing. **The purse: a hundred and twenty-three.** He asked Teague's leave for the six to carry stone; he laid the far side's bowls on the 306th.

**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. Left palm in linen; **Oryn: "It'll leave a line."** She measured the fifth place (the round; the across; the trueness), read three courses from outside the cut, found the copied slip and said, "The others are copies,"; inked the fifth ring, laid both edges, and wrote *No sixth found.* under it, leaving her hand's breadth of pencil. She crossed one stride ("It's the first one."). She has entered the instruments' figures of the 306th as larger than on the 168th, with no inference. She has joined nothing to anything. She sat with Vastin in the stream-bed and held him up at the burial. She has not been told the twelfth's reading and has not asked for a second glance.

**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He went up the swept slab and over the lip at the seventh flight; bound one bowman's arms with his cord; struck nobody; chose not to lift a cloth. He holds a finding about a changed order and gives it as no more. He expects a fourth reach. The case is on his chest, **shut**; nothing about it is altered; no decision.

**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day twenty-nine of thirty on the 308th** ("Twenty-nine tomorrow," his; "Thirty's the month," Oryn's, on the night of the 307th), read on the 306th (held; "it never took a pound") and at dawn on the 308th ("a day from done"); stick and laths for one day more, then he is to take them off himself. He raised an Iron Wall on the 306th, **sitting, as a slant, by Oryn's amended leave**, and was spent to the stones; he could not lift a bowl that night; he walked seven miles on the 307th at the broad man's pace and sat as the round's mark. He watched a boy killed a stride outside what he was holding and could not move. His sigil has gone out and come back twice in his life (the Span; the court). At the cut: "I stood at a line for forty years … I never once asked who drew it." / "This is where they drew it." **He has asked for no place among the six and nobody has offered him one; there has been no vote, and he does not mean to ask.** He is the sixth of Seln's count on the morning of the 308th and carries the tarred case (cord, mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven). He does not know the mechanism, the eleventh, the twelfth, the thirteenth, the two words or anything after them, the date, or what Cael met at the centre; he knows that he is not told. He knows both seals and has named neither.

**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. **Eight days past her cairn on the 308th.** **A boy died under her hands on the 306th: she asked, could not be answered, went in without, and her hands came off with nothing done.** She shut Brom's forearm and the broad man's shoulder, each asked first. **She knows the thirteenth** (the captain's; met as a line through the ribs: "That's a line … That's not yours." / "It's mine now.") and that Teague is not told. She read Cael twice in the movement, each asked: the ribs (306th) and at the cut after the centre (307th: "The same finding,"). She stood at the fifth line with her hands over it, went one stride in and back, and said: "Nothing … Everything, round it." and, to Cael, "Same as you."; nothing on the page explains it and nobody answered. **She has not heard the leaf**; asked at dawn when she would: "The day I have to." She does not know the twelfth's reading. She has gone north on the mule; three holds are owed, with word sent ("a week, or more").

**Teague and his three.** Teague: right arm on its slat, the hand not shutting, the splint warning standing; he went up a stair left-handed and drew no weapon; he let nine people go past him to a ridge; he knelt by his own and said, "A wall keeps everyone behind it, and one of mine wasn't."; he went one stride over the fifth line and back and put none of his three across ("My crew's stood at four of these. It's stood at enough." / to Cael, "Field's mine. This isn't field."); he has said that he would keep Cael's three sentences again. **He does not know that anything came to Cael at the timbers, or what moved the second heap; he did not see it and did not ask** (he read "the look of a bill"). He knows from Seln that an order had changed between the fence and the gap. He carries the letter and will tell Pike that the bearing was read right, that it ends in a floor, and that he is one short. He has said "Noted" twice in the book (the crest; the Span's shelf); at the parting the "Noted" was Cael's. The broad man: right shin knit and boarded; **right shoulder bolted on the 306th**, shut by Oryn and bound across his chest; a pine stick; he has said "Aye" to the six and nothing else. The tall man: unhurt; has spoken to the six once ("And then?"). The lean old woman: has never spoken to them; she keeps the boy's bowl inside her coat. None of the four knows the mechanism, any fragment, the leaf, the lamps page, the slips, the date or who Vastin was.

**The faction.** Three reaches (the stepped cut; the Span's watch; the pass), each broken; unnamed, unlocated, faceless. At the pass it saw: an old man raise a roof sitting down; a big man take bolts on silent Iron; a small man come up a bare slab; its second heap go along a shelf with a boy's palm on the frame (the maul-man was a yard off); a captain and a spear come up a stair and let nine people pass. It killed one. It took nobody and lost nobody.

**Counts.** Fragments: **thirteen**. Deployment since the Line: **thirty-five** (33 + 1 + 1). **Purse: 123 marks at first light on the 306th; nothing was bought, paid or received on the 306th, the 307th or the 308th; 123 marks at the book's end.** The reserve is sealed in the third box, which is on Cael's back. People: twelve and a mule into the pass; eleven and a mule out; at dawn on the 308th four west, one north with the mule, six west.

**Objects, and who holds each page.**
- **Karis:** the courier's flat (six rows; the technician's sheet at the foot of the second; the two slips re-pinned under the knot of the oldest row, seals unnamed), on her back; the hide with its pinned sheet (five rings, the fifth inked; the pencil line a hand's breadth past it; *No sixth found.* beneath), the Ternhall brass, her notebook (the torn lamps leaf in its back, with "*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*"; the table of the instruments on the 306th; the round's figures), Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes, Vastin's copied page.
- **Cael:** Hesk's book (the logs of the 306th and 307th and the ledger), Hesk's brass edge, the dividers shut in the book (opened only ever by Karis), the folded fair copy of the chamber leaf (not opened in the movement; quoted by nobody; unheard by Oryn), Hesk's two notes in the satchel; the third box.
- **Vastin:** the tarred kit case: the cord (now laid round the fifth place), the slip of ash, the mallet, stake thirty-two, never driven.
- **Brom:** the first box; Oryn's bowl; the purse. **Lira:** the middle box (fifty-two first copies and the nine of the piers).
- **Teague:** the letter to Hesk. **The lean old woman:** the boy's bowl. **In the cairn:** the cloth.
- **Left in the pass:** a heap and a dead pine in the western mouth; the second heap in the team's hollow, over nine heaps of bedding, a kettle, two bundles of unthreaded bolts and a second maul.
- **Nothing is laid across anything.** The leaf and the lamps finding are joined in no mouth and no head in these chapters; Cael has added a reading to his own log and laid it beside neither.

**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before (Oryn among them); Cleon how one fragment came; not Vastin, Teague, Pike or any of Teague's. The thirteenth: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln, and now Oryn; not Teague, not Vastin. *Still* / *set* / *bones* and the shape, and that the court's floor reads the same: **Cael alone.** That he turned the twelfth inward on purpose, and that it is written: Cael and Lira. That there was no edge at the centre: Cael alone. Oryn's finding at the cut: the six at the rim. The round's figures, the copied slip, both edges and *no sixth found*: the six, Vastin among them; Teague in outline. Seln's finding about the changed order: all eleven. The date: Cael and Seln, each alone, unchanged. The two seals: Vastin alone.

**Threads left open on purpose.** A sentence past two others, unwritten. A reading in a log that one girl has handed back unread, and a telling owed to her on a road. A healer's three pieces at a cut, unexplained, and her "Same" finding beside a reading that she has not been told. A leaf that she will hear "the day I have to". Two debts that cannot be paid without telling what is six people's. An old man on day twenty-nine with a case that nobody has taken back, who has asked for no place. Instruments that read wronger in one hand, with two readings and no line. A box ruled for a mark that no sheet carries. A hand's breadth of pencil past a ring, and *no sixth found*. Eleven stumps, as on the Fallow. An order that changed between a fence and a gap; nine people gone south; a fourth reach expected. A crack under two seals. A case, shut. A captain's term for after the next thaw, and a cairn that he walks home past. A bowl inside an old woman's coat. A letter with a sentence about Lira's family that nothing in this book explains.


