    24	
    25	The hillside rose behind the byre, steeply, for four hundred yards, to a band of grey rock that ran across it like the coping of a wall. The fence left the yard at the gate, and climbed that hill aslant, and then ran east along the foot of the rock. Teague's posts stood out of the snow the whole way up from the gate, six strides apart, as straight as a ruled line, for as far as he had got. Beyond the last of them a row of flat stones went on across the slope in the same line, each the size of a hearth-loaf, with a wisp of straw showing at its edge.
    26	
    27	"He dug every hole before the ground shut," said the factor. "Forty-one of them. Stoned them over, and stuffed them, so the frost shouldn't get down. I never asked him to. He'd have had the posts in as well, only he went off south after somebody."
