    78	
    79	Karis wrote for some time.
    80	
    81	"The read, three times, for a breath apiece. No answer at three hundred and fifty yards, at two hundred, or at sixty." She read it out as she had read the other. "It is one fragment and one herd and one morning. I shall not write that it is safe. I shall write that three things did not happen."
    82	
    83	"That'll do me," said Cael. "The read, and nothing else. If I want any other fragment on that hill, I'll have to want it and go without."
    84	
    85	Lira had got down off the sledge by then, and she was not looking at the page.
    86	
    87	"They're not asleep," she said. "And they're not eating. I've read feet for longer than I've had a rank, and those aren't the feet of anything that's settled. Look at the snow." She pointed with her chin. "It's off the rock for exactly as far as they lie. That isn't the wind. They've scraped it. They've had every scrap that grows under that lee, and they're lying on an empty plate."
    88	
    89	"So they'll move," said Brom.
    90	
    91	"Where? It's a foot of snow in every direction. The old one at the far end has turned her head east three times since I sat down, and there's nothing east of her to look at." She waited a breath for somebody to see it. "She wants what's under it. She can't get at it."
    92	
    93	Cael saw it then, and he saw that she had had it first.
    94	
    95	"We uncover it for her."
    96	
    97	"A strip. East, away from the holes, and up, toward that grass tongue at the end of the rock, where they came down. You shovel from the top side, between them and the scarp. Nobody goes underneath."
    98	
    99	"They mow a shelf at forty yards in the hour," said Karis. "I timed it on the mountain."
