   174	
   175	"Draft nothing. Just look."
   176	
   177	Cael had known that voice for a long while. He had heard it give an inventory, and a warning, and a count of days; he had heard it refuse a good many things. He went back over all of that, standing by a rock on a moor, and he could not find one occasion on which it had told a person how a thing was done.
   178	
   179	Seln kept doors. He had seen two posts a hand out of true, and had told one man, in eight words, and let him mend them. He had stood with his back to a shed through a whole cold term so that he could never be asked what was in it. He knew more of how to be overlooked than any of them; and when an Archmarshal had answered a hand aloud on a road, and stood like a gatepost at a ditch, it had been a girl who walked backward in front of him and beat it out. Seln had watched. He had not said a word.
   180	
   181	He had said seven now. It was the first thing that he had ever taught anybody, in Cael's hearing. He had chosen for it the one man there who had once had the power to file him.
   182	
   183	"The tell," said Vastin.
   184	
   185	"A man who reaches for a pen that is not there has told the room what he was. So has one who classes a paper before he has read it, or says *it should be directed*." Seln's mild voice did not alter. "I knew you in a doorway for what you were before you had opened your mouth, and I was not the only person on this side of the Line who could have. There is a town at the end of that road. There is a gate in it, and a man in the gate who has been reading people for his living since before I entered the service. You will not need to tell him your name. You will only need to stand at his counter and begin to arrange it."
   186	
   187	"And if I look?"
   188	
   189	"Then you will see what is in front of you. It is all that the rest of us have ever had."
   190	
   191	Vastin was silent. Cael watched him do the thing that he had been told, and it was a strange sight; the man had read rooms for forty years, in an order, giving each object its due, and he now had to look at one without doing anything to it. His right hand came down, and found the stick. His eyes went to the slab.
   192	
   193	"What's there?" said Lira. She said it without any mischief.
   194	
   195	"A courier's flat, strapped. A calfskin, with four stones on it." He was a long time over each. "A book that belonged to a dead man. An iron stake that has never been driven. Some pebbles, which that young woman has laid out by size." He stopped. "And a wind. I had not noticed until now that there was a wind."
   196	
   197	"Good," said Seln.
   198	
   199	He went to see to the mule's girth, which did not want seeing to. It was Brom who had the last of it, from the basin's rim, with his boot half on.
   200	
   201	"He told you that for nothing," he said. "I'd keep it. He's not given any of us so much in a year."
   202	
   203	---
   204	
   205	Karis had not got up from her stone.
   206	
   207	She sat with the flat across her knees and her two gloved hands laid on it. Cael had seen her so on the hundred and thirty-third night, with a strip of brass and a calfskin, going past the edge of what she had. She did not look tired. She looked like a person who has added a long column and is afraid to read the foot of it.
   208	
   209	"Six years," Karis said. "They measured all of it. They graded it — the sites are ranked by something, and it's the same something in the kit, and I can't read either one."
   210	
   211	She turned her head, and looked westward, where the road went over the next swell of the moor.
   212	
   213	"Everything I can't read points at the same place."
   214	
   215	"Treswick," Vastin said.
   216	
   217	He had said it once before, at a board, as a man reads a place off a waybill. He did not say it so now.
   218	
   219	Karis said, "The archive under it. Cleon."
   220	
   221	"Who's Cleon?" Cael said.
   222	
   223	"The only historical-Path practitioner registered west of the Line. If the language is anywhere, it's under his hands. A day and a half."
   224	
   225	She took one glove off, a finger at a time, and looked at the hand inside it.
   226	
   227	"And then I find out whether I've been right for five years or wrong for five years, and I don't know which I'm more afraid of."
   228	
   229	Nobody answered her at once.
   230	
   231	It was the first time that the town had been named aloud since a page was opened on a table, and the man who had carried the page was the one to do it. It lay on the moor now among the seven of them, like a thing set down. Cael turned the other name over. It was a short one, and he had never heard it. A man sat under that town with his hands on a language, and had a name, and had been waiting there, it seemed, for somebody to bring him three floors.
   232	
   233	At the basin a square woman had stopped wringing linen. She stood with a wet cloth in her two fists and her head a little turned. She had heard a station spoken of twice at a board, and had shut her mouth on something each time; she had heard an archive spoken of today. He saw her put the cloth down on the stones, very deliberately, as she put down a thing that she meant to come back to.
   234	
   235	"If you're wrong," said Brom, "we've had a walk."
   236	
   237	"If I am wrong, I have asked a person to go into the place that he fears most on the strength of fifty-two sheets and a feeling."
   238	
   239	"He's not been asked," said Lira. "He took a slip." She had come round the slab, and she stood over the small pale woman with her left hand out of her pocket. "You said *ours* at that plank before any of us. I never heard you want a thing before. I'd sooner walk a day and a half behind somebody who wants something than sit at Lowmarch with somebody who's being careful."
   240	
   241	"It is not a reason."
   242	
   243	"No. It's a boot. Put the glove on; your hand's cold."
   244	
   245	Karis put the glove on.
   246	
   247	He went and got the mule himself. Nobody had asked for hands, and nobody did. There was a road, and it went one way. A job had put them on it, and what lay at its end had been named now by the two people with the best right to name it. He found that he had no ruling left to give. He had only to pick up a box.
   248	
   249	"The fork by dark," he said. "Oryn, how far is it?"
   250	
   251	"Six miles," said the healer. She was drying her hands a finger at a time, and she did not look at him. "There's a fold there with a hearth in its corner, where a fire has walls round it. I'll have something to say when we're sat at it. I've put it off since a board at Lowmarch, and I've run out of road to put it off along."

