   176	
   177	He went down toward the middle.
   178	
   179	He did not go at it. He had gone at it three times, and it had cost him an order. He chose one current, the hiss of Wind, and gave the going-along to it as a leaf is given to a stream. He did not steer.
   180	
   181	A long way short of the place, the Wind began to lean.
   182	
   183	It was farther out than he had ever marked, since on every other night he had been looking ahead, at the place itself. There was no check in the lean, and nothing piled. The Wind bore off as a road bears off round the shoulder of a hill, which has gone that way for as long as there has been a road. He leaned with it. He went by on the one hand, and did not look in; the lean eased, a long way beyond, and the hiss ran on toward his feet as though nothing had ever lain in front of it.
   184	
   185	He came back and took the millstone, and it went by on the other hand. He took the wick. He took the bellows, which lived closest of the nine, and bent least far and most sharply. He took every one that had a sound, in its turn, and none of them touched the place or slowed for it. Then he let the going-along go by itself, with nothing to ride on, the whole way round, as a man walks the bounds of a field, until it stood again where it had set out.
   186	
   187	It was the first time that he had been all the way round.
   188	
   189	He lay and counted what he had, and it was a good deal. He knew where the place was. He knew how far out the leaning began, for each of the nine. He knew that the way round was worn smooth, with never a ripple in it; and he knew now how far it was about. He went down that column with something like pride, as a small pale woman went down a row that agreed.
   190	
   191	Then he saw what kind of column it was.
   192	
   193	Every line of it was about a thing that moved. It told him where the Wind leaned, and how the millstone went by. There was not one line in it about the place. There never had been. *Doesn't flow. Doesn't change.* He had written those on a leaf, and had taken them for findings about the place; and they were not. They were only the instrument's way of saying where it left off.
   194	
   195	The going-along felt current, and it had never done anything else. It had been a healer's means of telling how the rivers ran in a body, quick or slow or choked, and it knew a river only by going along with it. Set it where nothing ran, and it did not come back and say *empty*. It did not come back and say *full*, either. It had nothing to go along with, and so it said nothing at all; and for the better part of a year he had been writing that nothing down in pencil, with the day at its head, as though it had been an answer.
   196	
   197	He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*. A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go. Laid on a boulder in a field it has nowhere to run. A patient man may walk it round the outside, and knot it, and learn how far the thing is about; but of what lies in the stone the cord gives no figure whatever, and it was that blank which he had been entering, with the day at its head, as though the stone had told him so.
   198	
   199	He took his fingers from his wrist, and put his right hand down to push himself up.
   200	
   201	It came down on the foot of the gatepost. The going-along had not been called home yet. It went down his arm to the palm, as it had gone to a healer's wrist when he had her leave, and there it stood. The stone gave it nothing to travel on. He had always known that it would not; the tenth did nothing whatever on a dead thing, and he had never troubled to think about that.
   202	
   203	What held him there, half risen, with his weight on that hand, was that he knew the feel of it.
   204	
   205	It was the same nothing. To the tenth of his fragments, a gatepost and the middle of himself came back alike.
   206	
   207	He did not care for that at all. He made himself hold it at arm's length until he could see what it was worth, and it was not worth much. It did not say that the place in him was stone, or dead, or of any sort. It said that this instrument could not tell the two apart. Karis kept a rule for exactly this, and had said it twice over a cord in a frozen yard, and he had not understood until now what it cost her to keep. He had learned something about the tool. He had learned nothing about what the tool had been laid on.
   208	
   209	He called the going-along home, and it came, and lay down. He was cold through, as he always was after it, and as empty as if he had hauled on a rope all morning.
   210	
   211	He needed an instrument for what did not move.
   212	
   213	He knew of two. One stood on the crown of the town ahead of him. It had been built to read whatever was brought near it, and he had written in pencil that he would not go within a square's width of the thing. The other sat underneath the same town, with a short strange name, and read how things were built. Whether a man who could tell how a wall stood could tell how a boy did, Cael had no idea.
   214	
   215	He lay a moment longer, looking up through the hedge at a sky with nothing in it, and found that he feared the second a good deal more than the first. He had only to keep away from the first. He would have to walk up to the second and ask.
   216	
   217	---
   218	
   219	Lira was on her feet when he came through the gate, with a stem of grass between her teeth.
   220	
   221	"You were as long as Brom's boot," she said. "Did you go in?"
   222	
