   236	
   237	The town came up with every furlong. He kept his eyes on its foot, and he learned the foot of it very well. The lowest wall ran clear round the bluff, as far as he could follow it, with square towers at even distances, and he counted eleven of those before the river hid the rest. There was a gate in it with a tower on either hand. A bridge went up to the gate, long and low, and there were things crawling on the bridge that he took for wagons.
   238	
   239	He did not raise his eyes above the second wall. He knew where the third was, and what stood within it, as a person knows where a dog is lying in a room.
