   292	
   293	"There's a thing at first light," she said. "I'd take it kindly if you were on that bench for it. Not to do anything. Only to be there."
   294	
   295	"What thing?"
   296	
   297	"My daughter's girl is in her year. Fourteen since the hay. We ring them on the threshing floor, at the first light after the moon's full, and it was full last night." She had her arms folded in her apron. "It's never gone amiss here. But her mother's mother went down like a felled calf, at hers, and lay till noon. It's in the family to take it hard."
   298	
   299	"I'll not lay a hand on her unless she's hurt, and unless she says. She'll have enough that's new without a stranger's fingers."
   300	
   301	"I never asked for your hand. I asked for the bench."
   302	
   303	"Then you've the bench," said Oryn.
   304	
   305	She ate what was brought out, and lay down beside the mule.
   306	
   307	She had seen it done forty times on that road, at one gate or another, and had never got used to how little there was to see. Inside the Line it was a matter of offices. She had stood in one herself: a room with a desk, and a ring chalked on the boards, and a clerk who looked at a plate of brass and not at her. The district had named her day a year beforehand. Out here there was no desk, and no plate, and nobody to write. An old woman scratched a ring on a threshing floor with a hazel stick, on a morning that she chose by the moon; and the child stood in it; and it came.
   308	
   309	Whether it needed the ring, Oryn had never learned. Nobody at any of those gates had been willing to try it without.
   310	
   311	---
   312	
   313	The floor was of beaten clay, in the open, with a low kerb of stones about it.
   314	
   315	They were all there by the first grey: the whole hold, thirty or more, standing off beyond the kerb with their breath smoking, and not one of them speaking above a murmur. Oryn sat on the bench by the gate. It was forty yards. She had her hands in her sleeves.
   316	
   317	The old woman drew the ring.
   318	
   319	She walked it backward, bent, with the stick's point in the clay; and when the two ends met she stepped over the line, and held her hand out, and a girl came across the floor to her.
   320	
   321	She was thin, and tall for her age, with her hair in one plait and a sheepskin on her shoulders that was too big. She was frightened, and she was trying to walk as though she were not. Oryn had a square face that showed little, and she kept it so; but she had looked for that walk, and it was there, as it always was. Every one of them came across the clay like a person carrying a full pail.
   322	
   323	The girl stepped into the ring, and the old woman stepped out of it.
   324	
   325	Nothing happened that a watcher could have named.
   326	
   327	There was no light, and no sound, unless it was a ewe somewhere behind the wall. A thin child stood in a scratched circle with her hands shut at her sides, and a hold stood round her and held its breath.
   328	
   329	Then her head turned.
   330	
   331	It was a small movement, a little to the left, as a person turns toward a voice. Her eyes went with it. They went to a place in the air that was just beyond the corner of her sight, and when they came there the place had moved, and they followed it. Oryn watched a girl of fourteen look at something a hand's breadth from her own cheek that not one of thirty people could see.
   332	
   333	She had seen that forty times as well. It was always the eyes. A child found, on such a morning, that there was a thing in the world which belonged to nobody else, and which nobody else would ever be shown; and the first that she did was to try to look straight at it, and fail, and try again.
