editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:109:"*In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong*" — her breath had given out upon a comma, and she took another without lifting her finger — "*there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:119:"*— set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it. And this is the reason of the apparatus, and this is all of its reason, and let none who come after forget it, though the un-bound be forgotten: that they should not come again.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:147:"That word stands here. It is spelled as he has spelled it, letter for letter, in the old signs. *The Architect.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:155:"It gives its own year, at the head," she said. "It is in the count that the old town's books keep, and I have had twelve of those open for a week. By that count this was written about four hundred years ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:159:"The rest is what you heard. It says what the apparatus is for, and it says that there is no other reason. It was set over the land so that a kind of people should not come again." Her voice had dropped. "It calls them the un-bound. I said that word at this table on the first day, as a limit; I had only ever met it as the name of a layer in the ground. I did not know that it had been a people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:259:*and the* — there the box stood, with nothing in it — *set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:149:The road brought him round at last, by the back of the smith's, into the open place where the trough was. He had gone the whole circuit of the bluff, and he had not once lifted his eyes above a line of pale stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:65:*—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:287:"It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:303:He had spent the whole year learning that the world was built to prevent him — four hundred years of tiers and registries and a machine that lit for everyone but him, all of it laid down so that what he was would never come again. And at the center of the first place they ever made, the oldest silence on the continent, the one built before there was a word for it, he had felt no exclusion and no prevention and no wall. He had felt at home. He did not have a word for that yet. But he knew, now, walking back out across a floor older than the system that had expelled him, that it was not an accident — that the place built to end him was the one place he fit — and that the difference between *the system was built to exclude me* and *the system was built to prevent me* had a third sentence past it, waiting, that he was not ready to write. Someone had made the pattern. He was, he was beginning to understand, the pattern. That was as far as he could say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:185:"Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," he said. "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:67:"I am told that it is the one thing which I may be relied upon to do," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:131:There was not room for another length. Lira let the eye fall; she took the slip from Karis, and went on along the lip with it, laying it end over end, and saying the figures herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:199:"You've been on your knees," said Oryn. "And you've been at the thing that bills your eyes. I can see both from here." She came the last three paces. "I told you last night to ask me again when you'd stood where you were going. You've stood. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:317:They had a thing among them which he was not told, and he had known that since a spring. *It's six people's*, the boy had said, on a road, and Vastin had found it the most courteous refusal that he had ever been given. He did not guess at it. He had spent a working life guessing at what people kept, with a seal to make them show it, and he had come out here in part to leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:223:*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:173:"He told you at a spring. You look at what's there. You leave your hands where they are." Cael felt for the next tread with his heel. "That's all I've ever had of it. It's nine steps to the next landing. I'm below you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:239:"I'm going up for an hour. Set it on the table's end. Touch nothing." He went by them toward the stair with his eyes already half shut. "There's a jar of water on the shelf. You may have what's in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:165:She looked down at her right hand, and shut three fingers of it into the palm, one at a time, as a big man had counted findings at a spring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:219:It came down upon the rock, once, six strides away. The old man had sat through a reading and an account with his hands shut upon it, and his face turned to a bay of bundles; and whatever he had been holding off had got past him. He spoke to the shelves, low, and with care, as he had once begun to draft an order at a spring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:227:"There is what that leaf says. It says it in its own words, of its own time, and I shall copy it on one page, and nothing else shall go upon that page." She took a clean leaf from her notebook, and laid it on the desk, bare. "And there is this. What you were about to say is not on the skin. It may be so. I do not know, and nor do you. If it is ever written, it will be written here, when somebody can show it; and until then this page has nothing on it, and nobody is to rule a line from the one to the other because a word stands in both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:237:She had said it at a spring, over six years of an office's sheets, to stop a sentence of her own. She said it now with her bound hand on a bare leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:239:"I was drafting," said Vastin. His voice had very little breath behind it. "I was told at that same spring, by another person, what it is when I do so. I was drawing a conclusion, in a room where I have been at some pains for nine days to draw nothing." He looked down at the ash. "I leave it where it stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:"I'll say when it's my turn to be told." She did not go nearer. "Two days ago, at a board, I opened my mouth about that leg, and you answered a thing that I'd not yet put. You said that you offered it to nobody. So I said that I still hadn't asked, and I let it lie, since that's my rule and I keep it." She pushed her left sleeve a little higher. "This is the asking. There are teeth in that, and under the teeth a joint that's been walked three hundred miles and then stood on by eight of those. I want my hands on it. And I want two fingers on your wrist while I'm there, to learn what's left in you. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:35:"Then stand," said Oryn, "and you'll stand still, and you'll put your weight on the big one's shoulder and not on that stick. I'm not kneeling on a frozen road to have you fall on me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:71:"It was. Off. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:255:There was an outcrop of the dark plated stone a furlong to the south, as big as a barn, and under its eastern face the ground was green, where a spring came out. It rose in a basin that somebody had built round with stones, a long while ago, and ran over the basin's lip and away down the moor in a thread of rushes. Beside it a single slab of the rock had fallen flat from the face. It lay level, waist-high on its own rubble, twelve feet long and half as broad, with the outcrop standing between it and the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:19:It was not a fraud. A fraud wants something. It leans the scale so that a house may keep its place, or a seat its revenue, and when the leaning is found there is at least a person behind it who has got richer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:39:It was a draft. A small grey man had told him at a spring what he was when he drafted, and a small pale woman had laid a bare leaf on a desk and asked him to put nothing on it. He did not finish it. He did not let himself hear how it would have ended, even in the place behind his eyes where a man hears his own clauses before he speaks them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:163:"I thought so, and I read it again to be certain. I have read forty years of orders that said *must not*, and every one of them says who may grant leave. This one reserves no leave to anybody. It is not locked against the office. A lock is a thing that somebody has the fellow to." His hand closed on the board, slowly. "They sit upon it, and they have built the whole of their record on the top of it; and they could no more go into it than I could go into that hearthstone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:145:"May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:147:"You may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:247:*The gap was kept. Nine, with three bows, on a shelf; two heaps of stone rigged to fall. Twelve of us went in. Eleven came out. Teague's boy went a stride past the edge of the wall, for the bow, and was killed there. I heard his name once. I am not writing it; it was not said to me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:41:"That is the first," said Vastin. "It is yours now. It is a great deal of paper to say that an office has been walking round your four places for six years, counting, and has told nobody. I should like the second laid apart from it. At the bare end, if I may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:187:It was not a fall. Brom was off the door before it could be one, and had him by the upper arm, left-handed; Oryn's two hands had come up, and stopped in the air, since nobody had asked her for them. Vastin hung in a large man's grip, open-eyed, and as pale in the face as the paper on the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:43:They went into it in the order that the stair had taught them. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, with the stick going up a tread ahead of him and the bound leg swung after; and Cael came two steps below, where a man would fall. Lira followed, with her arms through her beckets. Karis was behind her, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; and Brom came last, and his box all but brushed both walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:109:A wall on a road may be gone round. In that place there is no round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:129:He was off it, and had it before it could fall back. He could not raise his arms. He could bend, and he bent, and got both hands under a slab of the dark stone as long as his own forearm and a hand thick; and he took it up as far as his thighs, on his legs and his back, as he had taken a dray.
