editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:249:"May I know whom I am thanking?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:97:"The hide says where four places are, and not what any one of them is looking at. *The next* is yours, and I shall not enter it." She blew the lead off the leaf. "Going east, the next is the older, every time. You had that from an office's corners, at a table. A thing that leans at what was there before it may as well be looking back as going on. I have that they lie one way. I have not what for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:107:"I do not want you on the ground. I want the oldest row read through, sheet by sheet, by somebody who knows what a form leaves out. I have had it in my hands four times, and each time I was looking for something else." She lifted the sixth tape from the flat herself, and held it up to him. "You said at a spring that you had not been through it. Lira will hand them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:155:"There is one thing more," she said. "It is mine, and it is not on this blanket yet. I began to say it at a spring, twenty-five days ago, and I stopped myself with my own rule, since I had not enough."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:171:"They fail the way a hundred lamps fail when one wire's cut. Not the way a hundred souls fall silent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:207:He had read that day at a spring, a figure at a time, and laid it against another that he had carried since he was fourteen. He had put the sheet back. He had not written it, and he had told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:243:He stood with a slip in either hand, on one sound leg and a length of ash, and Cael watched him read the two of them again, with his eyes only. He had drafted three orders at a spring, in as many breaths, and been stopped at each. He had tried to open a door under a town with a rank. Cael waited for the mouth to open, and for a schedule to come out of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:269:"I am not. I told you once that I had papers which I would not show, and I asked that it be said one time, so that nobody need ask twice." He had taken up the stick again. "I ask the same for these. I can name the two. If I did, there are people on this shale who would know where to carry that row of sheets; and I should have done, in a sentence, the thing that I was told at a spring not to do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:97:"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:237:"I don't know," he said. "There's a thing that stands before the bearing, and I've not ruled on it, and I'll not rule till that boat's in. It may be days. It may be a good many."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:283:"She said not to fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:97:Wind had him half way to her before the fall was finished, and he told it no, and walked the four strides. He touched the toe of her boot with his stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:99:"Two all," said Lira, from the ground. "I didn't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:133:"She saw you not fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:85:Brom had turned on his stone, below. He had put out one flat hand, without knowing that he did it, as a man steadies the foot of a ladder that somebody else is on; and when the ash came down he let the hand fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"He may be either. A hold's man does not ride a mile behind a dray for nine miles when he could be past it in a quarter of an hour. A lad sent for a boat does not stop when a file stops." Seln looked down the fold. "I do not say that it is the same man. I say that I told you a road would be watched, and that somebody is on it who behaves as a watcher would. I can put it no higher, and I take no comfort from how low I must put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:99:"Is the pass that goes with the work. A person without the tier may not live above a wall. But the wall cannot do without him by day. So it lets him in by the piece. He has a slip, with a coloured edge, that says which door he is going to and how many he is; he is counted in at the gate above, with a pen, and he is counted out again before dusk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:She looked at him as a person looks at a horse that may be going to kick, and then she shrugged with one shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:233:"I don't doubt that she did. Wrist. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:229:There were two at the timbers, with the long stocks laid upon the upper baulk. One of those rubbed his right hand against his thigh, over and over, as a man does whose fingers have gone dead on a string. Three sat with their backs to the hut and staves across their knees. He judged by the set of the heads that two of the three were asleep sitting, and that the third had been told to see that they did not fall over. One walked. He came down the shelf to the gap, and through it, and along the path eastward for two hundred paces, and back. He did it once in the time that a careful man takes to say a floor schedule through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:"You will rope three boxes for a stair, as though you knew that there was one, and Brom will keep off that foot. At noon there will be two of you at the cook-house, and no more." He turned his eyes on Karis, and then on Cael. "One at the counter, who has something to show; and one in the wood-yard, with a bowl in his hand, who is nobody. The yard is where porters are fed, through a hatch, and its door stands open to the room for the heat, so that a person may hear from there and need not be seen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:31:"You may see his back," she said. "I should be obliged if he did not see your face. He is said not to care for being spoken to, and I do not know what he would make of being looked at."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:So this was Cleon. The name had been said at a spring, by a small pale woman with one glove off, and asked after once; nobody had said it since. It sat on a dusty coat and a pair of shut eyes no better and no worse than a name ever sits on a stranger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:97:"On every sheet, so that every shadow in all of them should fall the same way, and a person who came after me might subtract it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:51:He looked at that squarely, standing over a spring on a moor with a sheet in his sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:He crossed the turf, and made no more noise than he ever did. He set the sheet down between those two hands, with its head away from the boy, as every paper had lain before him in his life. He squared it with one finger. Then he went back to the water, and stood with his face to the mule. He counted the sound of the spring, since a man who has placed a thing does not watch it found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:13:They did not all sit. Vastin stood in the gap of the fold, where the hurdle went, with his back against the stone and the bound leg out before him. He had come six miles from the spring on that stick, at the tail of the column, and had been offered a mule twice; and he had the grey look of a man who is counting what is left in him and finding it sufficient by a very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:"Arm," she said to Lira. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:129:"Fifteen days. It held a hound yesterday, by what I'm told, and it's none the worse. It's slow, and it'll be slow for as long again. They've stone floors where you're going, and stairs. Don't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:159:"Knee. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:165:"This is the first day. There are twenty-nine after it. It's stood a spring and six miles of road, and it's no worse than I left it, which is more than you deserved." She sat back. "Stick; binding; no kneeling. No walls. You'll want to carry something tomorrow, when those boxes come off my mule. You're not to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:193:Cael had a thing in him, since noon, that had walked out of a row of paper and that he had not written down. He heard those three words fall on it; and he said nothing, and was aware of a small grey man at the hearth who said nothing either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:205:"You were told not to fall on one," said Oryn. She was drawing the mule's girth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:207:"Then I'll fall on the box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:He had kept his hand a hand's width from that page for the whole of a lamp, so that he might still say no to it. He had ruled on a slip instead, at a plank, in daylight. He had walked three days up a road behind a job. Somewhere on that road the no had gone, without his having marked the stride; it had gone at a spring, perhaps, between two hands laid flat on a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:137:"May I read that?" he said. "The ankle. It's the one thing I've got that does nothing unless there's a hand on somebody. I can't shut it for you. I could say how it lies."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:281:*Edge?* It had asked that one word of every corner and rim since a friend's sword put it in his arm, and tonight he told it yes. The lantern ran down the steel like water down a hair. He held the knife out, flat, and let a straw fall across it, and the straw lay on the floor in two pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:"You are proposing to stand where I should fall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:A careful man writes a sum twice, once in figures and once in words, so that the one may be checked against the other, and a clerk at any counter did it still. If this cutter had done it, then those little groups were the names of his numbers; and if they were, each sign in them was not a thing that bore or hung. It was a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:213:"I do not say that they are yours. I say that a man may be followed without being told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:301:"She told you not to fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:303:"She told me not to fall *on* it. I've not. I've done that eleven times since the first day, down the dark end, when nobody had the light." She was breathing a little quickly, and her chin was up. "I can't use it to catch me. So I'm learning to come down without it. If I'm ever to go off something in a hurry, I'd sooner not find out then where it goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:"Then I shall add one thing to it, which you may enter or not. Those people have reached for us three times. They came up a stair under a town, and they stood to a pair of timbers at a fence, and yesterday they let a hill go." His voice was as mild as ever. "Each time they have gone away again, and each time they knew a little more than they had known. I have never once seen what any of them looks like. I do not think that the third was the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:197:"Knee," she said. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:45:"I'm a healer," said Oryn. "My name's Oryn; I walk this road. Your child's lying crosswise in you, with its back to the door, and I can turn it. I do it from outside, with my hands on your belly, slowly, in between; it'll hurt you some, and a good deal less than what you're doing now." She waited until the eyes had hold of hers. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:101:"A hand is a thing that a clerk looks at for the space of a count. I was in a registry's service for fifteen years, and for the most of them I was looked through; but it was clerks who looked through me, and a clerk remembers furniture. There may be one man in that yard who has seen me walk a file from one desk to another, in a house above Ostrand or in some other. If he finds such a face among the porters, he will not ask me who I am. He will ask himself what the rest of you are, to be worth it." He drew a breath. "I should cost you the count. And there is this besides: when a door has shut on five people, somebody ought to be standing where he can see who comes to look at the door. That person cannot be on a card."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:121:"He will be told nothing by me. I have been practising; you may have observed it, and I am told that I do it very ill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:253:"Ribs. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:"No." It came through shut teeth, very distinct. "It does not go anywhere. I told that girl so. It stands where I stand, and I may not lift a foot; if I shift so much as a heel, the whole of it is gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:211:He did not fall. Cael had thought that he would, and was half way down the bank to him. But the man had both hands on the stick. He bent over it until his forehead was almost on his knuckles, and stayed so, with his feet where they had been. They had not moved since the stick came down. He lifted the left boot now, an inch, and set it down, as if to learn whether it was still his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:171:Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:241:He had not looked at Cael, and he did not look now. He had come down through two gates and asked a keeper his road, and sat an hour on a bucket with the fire hurting his eyes; and he had kept one thing the whole of that hour, which was where he did not put them. Cael had seen people look away from him all his life; they had done it at counters, and in the corridors of a house on a bluff, as a person turns from something that may be catching. He had not known that it could be done as a courtesy, and held to, like a term.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:23:"Arm," said Oryn. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:They did not fall alone. The bank took fire from them, as dry grass takes it. From the top to the bottom the loose shale let go, all at once, like a blanket that somebody has dragged off a bed; and the noise of it was not a noise, but a thing that stood in his chest and shook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:137:"May I?" said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:227:He did what anybody would have done beside a thing that was going. He let the maul fall, and jumped back from it, flat against the inner wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:251:The frame went, with everything behind it. It did not fall outward, as it had been built to fall. It was put. The uprights, and the prop, and the cartload of loaves went together along the shelf, a body's length, as a drawer goes home into a chest; and they stopped dead in the mouth of the hollow, and came apart there against the back wall, as a load comes apart when the cart stops short. The fire went out under them. A handful of grit ran off the lip, and fell on the bed where two men had passed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:267:To the west there were three hoods and a small grey man. One of the three had his arms bound to his sides by a thin cord with a pear of lead on the end of it. One had let his bow fall, and was looking at it. The third had backed against the rock with his hands open. Nobody had a string bent. Seln stood among them with a short knife held low, at his own pace, and he had not touched any of them with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:295:Seln had let the cord fall from a pair of arms himself, and stood back; and that one went last, rubbing his wrists. At the cleft he turned his hood toward the shelf for the space of a breath. There was nothing in it. There never had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:331:"Shoulder," Cael heard her say. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:7:"We may talk," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:17:"We are not. The river is the Line here, and the sill lies under the gate. This is ground that the town uses and does not own." He put his hands into his sleeves. "I had better say the rest now, since it will be wanted before night. The hold was one rule in two halves, and only one of the halves is lifted. You may speak. Nobody declares."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:75:"At the spring you said what he was. The man under it." He shifted the box. "You said a historical-Path practitioner, and I let it go by, since I'd a mule to fetch. What does one do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*. A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go. Laid on a boulder in a field it has nowhere to run. A patient man may walk it round the outside, and knot it, and learn how far the thing is about; but of what lies in the stone the cord gives no figure whatever, and it was that blank which he had been entering, with the day at its head, as though the stone had told him so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:257:"He was gone for some time. He came back the colour of that road, and a young woman who had been set to keep the hedge asked him three questions and was content with half an answer." The stick came down. "I say so in order that you may know it was seen. I have found that people on this side of the Line prefer to be told. I do not ask what it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:275:"At my pace. You may say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:229:"May I ask a thing that I ought perhaps to have asked this morning?" Vastin had his bread in his hand, and he did not lower himself onto a stone until he had the question out. "You have told me what I am to do if one of them comes at me. You have not told me what I am to do if it comes at one of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:He gave the rest in few words: the pan lay under the western end, and the herd's weight stood east of it; a person could come at it from the west, along the foot of the band, and never be on the fall of the hill beneath a beast. They would go two at a time, and no more, and nobody was to go above the band at all. They were to walk as the herd walked, three steps and a stand, and whoever was at the pan was to come out the way that he went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:143:"It's an arm. Use it. It'll be slow for a month and it'll ache in the wet for a year and then it won't. Don't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:217:"May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:103:The barricade went. It did not fall, or break; it was put. Both baulks went together, and the two trestles with them, and every lashing, all of a piece, as though the whole had been one thing and somebody had taken it up. It went the length of a man's body down the shelf, and stopped there dead, as a cart had stopped in a meadow, and went not a hand farther.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:295:On the shelf nobody had moved. One hood was staring at an empty bow in his own two hands, as a man looks at a jug that he has just let fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:323:He went over the iron as if it had not been there. Whatever came back to him at that stride, he did not so much as break step for it; he was along the shelf, and past the timbers, and Cael lost him at the western end, where a fall of rock went down into the ravine like a broken stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:343:He had got down that fall of rock in the time that it takes to say it, and he was on his knees with both hands under the grey hair, to keep it off the stones. He was not saying anything. Cael could see his shoulders going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:351:"That's a shin, and it's through in one place. I can knit it, when it's been laid straight; I can't lay it straight and knit it with the one pair of hands." She did not touch him. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:23:He did not write it. He had kept one other thing out of those pages on purpose, at a spring, eleven days ago; he marked that this made two, and that he could not write that either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:281:"They know that he may be out. It is a different thing, and it is the thing that matters." Seln's voice was as mild as ever. "You and I have gone round this town tonight like mice in a wall. He has gone round it like a man taking the air."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:313:Seln looked at him, and did not say that it was foolish. He said, "A person may walk up a street. Do not speak first, and do not stand as though you were waiting to be let in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:357:"So that we should know how much time they have. That is the whole of it. They need not take you in a stair. They may stand under a lamp for a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:377:*He may go through the blue gate at night. I may not go through it at all, except as a figure. It is a hand wide.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:He lay on his face on two hands of blue ice and breathed. His left leg was wet to the knee, where a plate had let him through as he went down, and the cold of it had begun to bite; and his head felt like a room from which the lamp has been carried out. He heard the cart mules blowing. He heard a ferryman's rope go slack and fall across eighty yards of snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:81:"I don't know. I've never gone so far into anybody who wasn't dying." She looked down at him. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:205:It was not the first thing that he had kept back. He had kept a date from everybody, at a spring, and a warning. But he had never before kept a thing from the person whose hands had drawn it out, with those hands hardly cold upon him; and he found that it had a weight which the others had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:37:"I'd as soon not sit on them a second time," said Brom. "There's a handcart in that stable, with shafts for one. I've asked. She says we may have the use of it while the loft's ours, if it comes back swept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:305:The cut was harder going up. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, and Cael went two steps below him, where a man would fall. On the bottom flight a tread rocked under Brom's weight, and he stopped, and trod on it again to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:243:They came over the north bank at the mark, as Lira had said that they would, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were five, grey and long, pouring over the worn places with their heads level. And behind those, out from under the strip that nobody had been able to see, there came three more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:"We know what we may not do," she said. "No sigil, anywhere on that hill, for any reason. I should like to know what we may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:127:The boy had not stirred. He had gone down as a felled thing goes down, an hour since, in the corner by the door, sitting, with his head against the turf and his mouth open. He had run the length of a shelf and down a fall of rock. He had held a man's head off the stones for a quarter of an hour without being told to. He had carried water down, and the board's webbing, and a healer's pannier, and had come up under one corner of a blanket. Then he had sat down to wait until he was wanted, and it had taken him like a blow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:161:He had had the spear lift a corner of it, in the forenoon, and had looked, and had let it fall. He lifted it himself now, left-handed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:175:He let the hide fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:189:"I said that I'd be here for this, on a morning when nobody was shooting," she said. "It's noon, and I'll take it. Wrist. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:261:"Or a place that's been used for one. I can't tell at that distance, and I'd be guessing." She let her hand fall. "The brass goes through it. I'd lay my knife."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:89:"You drafted an order at a spring," she had said. "Each row to be taken up singly; a schedule to be made of every sheet as it is turned; and the schedule to be signed by two hands. I told you that it was quite a good order, and that I had nobody to sign the second half." She had held out the leaf. "I have somebody now. I should like them counted into their boxes by a person who has done it before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:"Eleven," said Vastin, and made his stroke. "I have eleven. The box may be shut." He turned his head a little, toward the big man with the lid. "I should be obliged if it were said back to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:Cael heard the pencil go down again, and he did not turn round. He had seen that man stopped at a spring in the middle of an order, by nothing more than a small grey person's eyes. It seemed to him a large thing, and one that nobody in the gallery was going to remark upon, that the same order had now been asked for; and it was being carried out to the letter, with a stub of pencil on a kit's case, by the man who had not been let to give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:45:"I want my hands in that once before you sleep on it. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:241:She had not meant to, and Cael saw her check it. There had been a slab at that spring, twelve feet long and waist-high, lying level on its own rubble in the lee of the rock. She had called it a table. She had laid six years of an office's paper out upon it under river pebbles, and nothing had stirred but a corner now and then by the width of a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:271:Cael knelt at the basin's lip, upwind, and dipped the pot. The water was as cold as a knife. Neither of them said one word. He did not know what that man had made of a date, and he had never asked. It came to him, on his knees in the bents, that he was not the only person at this spring who kept a thing out of his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:"I said by a lamp that I should give you a number when I had checked it in daylight," she said. "I have checked it, and I am not going to give one, and I owe you the reason. What I counted that night were groups of signs. I let you think that I was counting places, and I thought so myself." She laid her pencil down. "I do not know that. Two clerks may sign one place in two ways. One place may be entered under one group for its floor and under another for its fence. Until somebody can read a sign, a group is a group, and I shall not put my name to how many holes in the ground it comes to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:171:"The class of persons to whom it may not be shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:223:He counted them. It was not hard. He lifted each sheet by its lower corner with one finger, and let it fall upon the last, and said the number in his head; the stock was soft, and it clung a little, and twice he went back three sheets to be sure that he had not lifted two together. He came to the row's foot, and to a figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:79:*There?* said Anchor, of the arch. It was a tall one, with a groove cut down each jamb where something heavy could be let fall. He told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:211:A small grey man had told him at a spring what that hand was. He had not much cared to be told, and it had been true. He laid the hand on his knee again, palm down, and he looked at the room and did nothing to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:253:"The second is the leave. I have seen that form, in another house: it is the oldest provision in the registry's book, and the shortest. A practitioner who is registered under historical studies has standing entry to a lower archive, by the week, renewed at the yard door, and he may have matter brought down to him for study. For the practitioner, that is the whole of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:273:"The fourth is what the form asks, when matter is brought." He drew his hands into his sleeves. "It was drawn a long while ago, when a scholar's bundles came with whoever happened to be carrying them, and nobody thought such people worth a line apiece. So it has a line for the matter, which is described. It has a line for the matter's bearer, who is one. And it has a line for hands, to carry, which takes a figure and no more; the figure may not be above four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:39:A sheet had been laid before him once, at a spring, with its head turned away; and the person who laid it had gone to a basin and stood with his back turned, so that it need never be asked what was made of it. Cael had understood only afterward what that had cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:He had stood over a spring six days since, with a stolen sheet in his sleeve, and had gone through the three things that his training let him do with a fact. He had not been able to look at the case that morning. He had said to himself that a man cannot hold two closed things up to the light at once; and it had been true, and it had also been convenient.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:93:He had passed it three days ago, under a keg. He had not needed to turn his head. He knew how it would be: a slot of light, and a night clerk behind it, half asleep, with a book. The courier lays his packet on the ledge. The clerk enters it by its seal and its weight, and tears off a slip. Neither is permitted to ask the other a question. It is the oldest rule in the service, and the only one that he had ever wholly respected. A registry enters what is laid on its ledge. It may bury the thing afterward, or burn the copy, or hold it for six years in a drawer where nobody will receive it; but for the length of one line, in one book, on one night, the thing has been set down as having come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:105:There was a fourth thing which he had not thought of at the spring, since it had not then existed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:273:"You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." His chin went toward the three tables, and his eyes did not leave her. "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." The two fingers found the brown tape again, behind him, without his looking for it. "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." He let the hand fall from the shelf. "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:49:He had been stood on a plate once; and something had been written about him at the head of a sheet, in a place that a printer had ruled for it, and he had carried it ever since. He had known at a spring that a hole in the ground could be filed in the same fashion. He had not known until this morning that the two filings were one word, in one line, on the back of a skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:59:"There is a stroke with a bar. That is on the stake, and in the book, and on every sheet of the four places that I matched to the hide." She drew it in the middle box, small. "I told you at the spring that there were other sheets, under other signs, and that I would not call them places. I turned their corners down, and I did not tell you everything that I had seen on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:77:"It is three marks. Call them what you like." Karis took the pencil up. "But a grade grades something. The one that is given at a kindling grades a person, and there is a man in this room who signed for a good many. This one is not for size, as I showed you at the spring; a place of twice the measure has the same bar. It is not for which is fenced. So I have gone through everything else in which one of these sheets differs from another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:81:There was a number there, small, after a clerk's flourish; Cael had seen it at the spring, on every sheet, and had taken no more notice of it than of the printer's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:127:"I was coming to that," said Karis. She did not seem put out. "The sheets with two bars have one line that the others lack, above their eight stations. It gives the four signs of the place behind the iron, and then a point of the compass, and then a measure in lengths. I saw that at the spring as well, and took it for a fence's survey tied to its own floor." Her pencil tapped the line. "It is a run, from the fourth place to this one. An office has written down how far it is from the one to the other, and which way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:245:Three of the stakes were gone from the weir, and Cael saw a captain see the gap. The tall grey building stood beyond, with its wheel going round in a white fall. There was the fence of split posts, and the dozen roofs, and the ford below with a flat stone on either bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:317:"Shoulder," said Oryn. "The left. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:345:"Arm," she said next. "The left. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:363:"Hand. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:389:"Sit on the plank. Leg out. I want my hands on that knee, and on your wrist. May I?"
