editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:7:The first twenty strides were over the rows. He did not look down at them. He felt them through the soles of his boots, course after course, each cut as deep as a finger, as a person feels the boards of a bridge; and he kept his eyes upon a star that stood low in the east, a little to the left of where the far rim must be. He had chosen it before he lifted his foot, since there was nothing else out there to steer by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:The star was gone. There was a smear where it had been, with a halo to it, among a great many other smears; he chose the lowest, and hoped. He had four hundred and six strides still to go, and he would have to walk them as a person crosses a room in the dark, with nothing to trust but the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:123:He was a boy with a book who had run out of things to count, and he could not have said what he had meant to do upon arriving. He had wanted this since a fire in a stable yard; he had said so on his feet, to six people, in the only speech that he had ever made. He had not thought past the standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:131:It did not come like a blow. It came as a person notices that a noise has stopped which he had long since left off hearing: a mill-wheel, or rain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:137:He looked for it. He did not turn anything upon it; he had no eyes left for that, and no wish. He only felt for it, as a man feels in the morning for the edge of the bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:He did not understand at first what she had done. She had slid her right hand down to the stave's heavy middle, and tucked the upper half of it along her forearm, as a person carries a pole through a doorway. She had a short stout stick now, in one hand. She came in with it very fast, and low. The thing darted at his thigh and was gone before his own wood got there, and came again at his wrist; and when he covered the wrist she went past him on the mended foot, turning, and rapped him smartly across the back of the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:119:It had never been meant to arrive. She had sent the slow hand out to be seen, as a person holds up a lantern, and while he was walking down the outside of it the right-hand end came round from behind her hip with all her quickness in it and stopped an inch from his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:The first was a woman on a mule, at a ford where two rivers ran together. There was a cairn on his bank and another on the far one, and they did not stand opposite. He was looking at that when she rode into the water from the other side and came over on the slant, cairn to cairn, with the stream never above the animal's knee. She was brown and spare, and her hat had the brim cut away on the right. A hatchet hung at her saddle with its edge turned outward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:237:The third was a boy with a tally stick, on the western landing, on the evening of the twelfth day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:243:There was a man on that landing who was hiring. He was brisk and well-kept, with ink on his cuffs and a clerk at his elbow, and he went along the waiting carters and the men without carts and looked at each as he would have looked at a bale, for the mark. He came to Vastin last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:23:He had the sheet itself this time, and daylight, and nobody at his shoulder. He read the technician's date a figure at a time, with his thumb moving under it, as he had not read anything since he was a junior: the year first, and then the day. He turned the sheet over, and looked at the brush's work on its back, and turned it again, and read the date a second time from its last figure to its first. Then he shut his eyes. He set the other date beside it, the one that he had copied twice, and went along the two of them together, as a clerk goes down a copy against its original.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:45:He could take the boy aside. That was better. But it would still be Seln's mouth, and Seln's choice of words, and Seln's face to be read while he chose them. Afterward the boy would have to decide what to tell the others with somebody standing by who already knew. He had been handled so since he was a child. People had found things out about him and then come to tell him what they had found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:61:A thing given so cannot be shared. He would never know for certain that it had been read. He could not stand beside the boy in it, or be asked what he thought, or say that he was sorry. He could not be thanked, which he did not mind; he could not be blamed either, and he found, with some surprise, that he minded that. He would be handing over the heaviest paper that he had ever carried, and he would have to do it as a man slides a bowl along a board, and look away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:73:He had not thought of it. He had taken one sheet from the foot of one row, to be sure of a thing before he did anything with it. He had forgotten that she never trusted a count until a second person had made it. She had put that row into the one pair of hands in which a cord had read short. He saw those hands now. They lay flat on the rock, on either side of the heap, and they had gone the colour of the paper. The boy was looking at them as a man looks at a tool that has failed him twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:95:He had heard it read, by a lamp, in Lira's voice, from the far end of a table. There was the printed head. There were four short lines in a quick hand that leaned to the right, as a man writes on his knee. He knew what the first of them said, and the third, and where the fourth broke off. They had gone round in him for three days, with the stripe that lay across the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:99:He read it as he would have read any other, and went on to the line beneath. Then his eyes came back. He had read a great many such dates, upside down, on a great many desks, and they were all of them much alike; but this one he seemed to have met, as a person seems to have met a face in a crowd.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:217:He had said it once before, at a board, as a man reads a place off a waybill. He did not say it so now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:There was a hut, low, of turf and stone. There was a fire. There was a long shape beside the hut that was a hide thrown over something and weighted with stones. And across the strip, at the end nearer to the brow, where it was narrowest, two timbers had been laid one above the other on legs, as high as a man's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:165:He gave it as a term, and he put his own word on the end of it, as a person sets a thumb to a slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:229:There were two at the timbers, with the long stocks laid upon the upper baulk. One of those rubbed his right hand against his thigh, over and over, as a man does whose fingers have gone dead on a string. Three sat with their backs to the hut and staves across their knees. He judged by the set of the heads that two of the three were asleep sitting, and that the third had been told to see that they did not fall over. One walked. He came down the shelf to the gap, and through it, and along the path eastward for two hundred paces, and back. He did it once in the time that a careful man takes to say a floor schedule through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:245:One of the three by the hut coughed. It was a wet cough, from low down, such as a person has who has slept a cold term through on stone. The one who was awake leaned over, without a word, and drew a blanket higher on the other's shoulder. The walker, coming in through the gap, stopped by the fire. Seln could not hear what he said. He heard that it was put as a question, and that the seventh answered it with a figure, and that the figure did not please anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:261:*The thing in the middle is a bridge with no river. Teague has a carters' name for it: the Sunken Span.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:19:She stood by the second chair, with her notebook against her chest. She had slept; he could see that she had, and that it had been done on purpose, as a person eats before a long road; and it had not taken the white out of her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:107:She set one gloved finger in the air above the first line, an inch off the skin, as a man had taught her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:The keepers in blue had seen it now. One of them said *lower archive* to the other, as a man says the name of a bird that he saw once and has now seen twice; then the feather of his pen went along five caps without stopping at any. The old keeper at the round arch read the seal from its stamp inward, exactly as before, and said *one, and four*, and told them again to mind their lame one on the steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The door had opened. The clerk stood on its sill, with the book shut under his arm, and he looked at Vastin once, as a man looks at an inkwell that has gone over and has by some mercy missed the page. Then he stood aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:125:"The name, the rank, and the office, in the order of the roll. I did not decide to do it. I heard it as a man hears a clock strike in his own house; and by the time that I knew what the sound was, it had struck three." The stick's foot grated on the stone. "He had his pen up. He would have written it. It would have stood in that book tonight, under a card with one name on it, and gone up to a counter by the fourth bell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:169:Far below them the lamp had come out into the passage, and somebody had turned to look back up the stair; Cael could see a small bright point at the bottom of the three flights, and a shape beside it that was a girl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:He had heard a leaf of skin read out that morning, once, by a woman who would not say it twice. He did not say it over. He had been asked not to, and he was a man who kept to what he was asked; he had learned that lately, and at some cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:149:He stood on the top step of twenty-seven and looked at that. He had read every sheet that had ever been written about him from the far side of somebody's desk. There had been a file in a district office, and a thicker one on a bluff; there had been a finding under two hands, and a paper with an hour in it. Each had lain with its head toward the person who owned it, and he had learned to take them so, as a matter of course, as a person learns to read a shop's sign from behind the glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:177:Cael had been asked not to say it over, and he did not. He gave it as a carter gives a load to the man who is to take the other shaft: who had put it up, and whom it was meant for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:203:It was not the paper. The paper weighed nothing. It was what he had not let himself pick up in a gallery, at a desk, with a woman's back turned to him and a man's eyes shut. He had counted a room there. He had held a lamp afterward, for five hours, a hand to the left, and counted pebbles. He had the thing now as a person has a bowl that is full to the brim: level, in both hands, with all his care gone into not spilling it before there was a place to set it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:The broker turned back a corner of the sheet himself, and laid his hand on the nearest coil as a man lays his hand on a horse that he means to sell. Brom did not look at the tag; he took the rope's end between his finger and thumb, and bent it back on itself, and opened the lay with his nail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:189:He showed them as they walked. A flat hand, with the palm to the ground, was *stand*: wherever a person was, and as he was. A shut fist was *seen*, and one looked where the fist went and did not ask. Two fingers lifted were *come on*; that was Seln's, mostly. And a hand turned over empty, as a girl had once turned hers over to him on a hillside, was *nothing*: the thing had gone, or it had never been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:9:Brom looked at the row when it was done. It went the whole way round the fire, twelve bowls of two different turnings, and there was a person coming for each.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:51:She did not undo her own work. She had bound that shin to its board with four turns of webbing, and she put her thin fingers into the second turn and the third, and drew them apart, a hand's breadth, as a person holds open the mouth of a sack. It left a strip of bare skin between. She held it so, and looked at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:It was harder than it had looked. A man had done it in three breaths with staves coming over a baulk at his head, and Cael, with nobody near him and all the dusk to do it in, found that he did not know where his own hip was. He brought the knee in over the heel. He let the shoulder down, and turned it. He was a long way off the line, and he could feel that he was, as a person feels a picture hanging crooked; and the thing that lay along him felt it too, and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:173:It came to her as she blotted the line, and she set the pencil down. Being believed had been the thing that she wanted, for all of those years, as a person wants a meal; and a man under a town had told her what it was like when it arrived. He had said it with his eyes shut, in the hearing of five people. He had said that it was the end of being able to be wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:211:A measurement was a fact about a ruler. She had two rulers on that lid. The one had been laid upon every crossing that she had seen, by seven people, and it told her how a thing went out and came back. The other was a man's own account of himself, a long while dead, with his name scraped off it by a hand that she could not identify. Neither had been laid on the other. There was nothing on that lid, or in three boxes, or in a courier's flat, that would serve to lay them so.

