editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:217:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:153:"A hundred and eighteen," he said. "I'd have it in my hand when that sheet comes off his cordage. I'd like to see the man's face who tells me a thing is four-strand when I've the price of it showing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:221:*Three hundred and seventh day. The fifth place, at the end of the line. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight; he was the mark that the round closed on. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:*Two hundred and eighty-third day. Treswick, the district under the first wall. A hundred and thirty-six. Saw the man, from a wood-yard, at five strides. He looked at one sheet for as long as it takes to swallow, and he did not read it as anything of mine has ever been read: he asked it nothing, and he started at the bottom. He wouldn't hear what she thinks they are, and he wouldn't have a name, and he never looked at the yard. I'd wanted that, and I find I'd wanted the other as well. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:287:*Two hundred and eighty-fifth day. Treswick; the loft. A hundred and thirty-six. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Lamp, from about the third hour. He says left and I go left.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:259:*Three hundred and third day. A fold behind the brow, above the fourth place. No fire. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-four; he has said that he will not stand it, and it is entered. Oryn is three days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:225:"A hundred and sixteen. Five on. Three off," said Brom. "A hundred and eighteen in hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:287:He had begun a cold term in that doorway, with a mule and a frozen trough. The mule was there still, a hundred and thirty-nine days on, with her fee paid. The trough was open water. He went out to the end of the planks before he sat down, and gave the read the ground, as he had on the first morning; and he could feel the shelf going. It was grey, and soft, and full of water, and it took his weight on one side and let it drain away on the other. Out in the channel the river ran black from bank to bank between two rims of rotten ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time, as he would have gone over a count. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on four sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:"I've no doubt of it. He asked for four." Brom let the end go. "A four's laid round a heart, and it keeps its round in a block, and a ferry's rope lives in blocks. You could give me this, and I'd still be owing him sixty yards of four." He put the purse inside his shirt, where the dead slips lay. "A man lent us a rope and told us what he wanted back. I'll not hand him something else and call it paid, however good it is. When you've four, there's a hundred and eighteen marks will come down this road to look at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:17:The prices were what he knew best, since he had carried all ten down the roads of this country for a hundred and thirty days and used them as seldom as he could. He could have said to a grain what each fragment charged him for being kept shut. He knew a good deal less about what each would do when it was let out, and where it would fail him, and what it cost afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:Cael had Hesk's book inside his coat, and he did not need to take it out, since every figure in it had stopped at this river. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:153:The horse-walk was a ring of beaten earth round the drum, eight strides across. He chose the post that faced him on the far side. Wind had been in his feet since a painted floor, a long while ago; it was the first of the ten, and hers, and for a hundred and thirty days it had leaned him toward the first stride of everything while he told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:243:"Seventy-six in hand," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:281:*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:341:*Three hundred and fourth day. The watch's post, on the shelf above the fourth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-five; he stood inside the line this morning, and it took what he carries, and gave it back. Oryn is four days past her cairn. She knit a shin in the ravine's bed and was grey for a quarter of an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Thank you. That is exactly as much as I wanted." Her finger had gone to the hide, and it touched each ring in turn, going east. "The Fallow's newest sheet stands in its sixty-first year, and the Stair's in its ninety-fourth; the hall's is a hundred and thirty-eight. And the place behind the iron—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:He took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from between its leaves into his palm, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for six days, under a ruling of his own that was a hundred and thirty days old: he did not put his hand to a thing that measured, since a cord had run short in it and two edges had disagreed. An old man in Denvash had sent them to step off a line with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:Cael looked along the skin, from the left hand to the right. There were four rings, and a point that was not yet drawn, and a number against each: sixty-one, and ninety-four, and a hundred and thirty-eight, and two hundred and six. They rose as they went. Beyond the last of them, in a strip of shadow, stood something that the office had stopped counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:223:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:299:Nobody argued it. Brom had gone into the mill by then, and he came out with the miller's wife behind him and a sack on the bound shoulder; it was a mark's worth of meal and a flitch, for five days of country with no gate in it. He said the figure as he came: a hundred and twenty-seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:105:"You have not touched it in a hundred and thirty-eight days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:245:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock below the eastern mouth of the gap. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-seven; he held a roof today, sitting down, by Oryn's leave, and the knee never took a pound of it. Oryn is six days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:99:*Three hundred and first day. A dead hold on the east road, in the lee of its gable. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-two. Oryn is a day past her cairn. A cup of water a head, and the kettle.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:321:*Three hundred and second day. The ridge's back, in a hollow. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-three. Oryn is two days past her cairn; she went by the chock-stones and did not look up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:277:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:*Two hundred and ninety-fourth day. Treswick; the loft, and one in the stall; the last night of both. A hundred and thirty. The back of the leaf: a list, nine lines, a thing and its number to each. Two copies, hers and the reader's, and his goes with her. The fourth line is the mark's; of its last six signs, three are sure. Fifty-two sheets counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote* withdrawn*. Vastin: fifteen of thirty, and the stair alone. Karis wrote with the sewn hand. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:23:"A hundred and twenty-eight in hand," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:He went, and sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book. The brass legs slid from it into his lap, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for three days and had not once opened them upon a distance. A ruling of his own stood in the way, and it was a hundred and twenty-seven days old. Whatever measured had read one way in four hands and another in his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"A hundred and thirty-six," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day, night. Millrace; the cart-shed. A hundred and twenty-seven. Told Oryn, before she read me: the two readings at the brook and the post, and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. Then she read me, asked first, for forty minutes, as deep as she goes. Her finding is hers. I have it word for word, and I am not copying it here, since she can say it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:283:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:261:*Three hundred and fifth day. A shelf of shale under a step, four miles short of a gap in the hills. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-six. Oryn is five days past her cairn; Brom's week was out today, and she had her hands in a shin tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:"A hundred and thirty-eight in hand," said Brom. He put the slip inside his shirt with the purse. "That's his, to draw the tack on. I'll have it after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:61:It was tarred hemp, no thicker than a pencil, with a brass eye whipped into one end and a knot at every fifth stride. A dead man had carried it down forty steps under that floor and run it half way to a wall. Karis had walked behind it for a whole cold term, in an inn yard, and Cael had not laid a finger on it for a hundred and thirty-one days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:187:*Two hundred and ninety-ninth day. A beck under the ridge's foot, a mile short of the ring. A hundred and twenty-seven. Vastin: twenty of thirty, and walking. The Stair's rim at noon. Karis laid the kit's cord on the dead man's figure, from the old iron to the floor: nine lengths and a piece. It is what it was. I did not touch it. A hundred and thirty-one days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:227:"Four," said Brom. "Meal, and a cheese, and corn for her, and the two skins." He set the sack down. "A hundred and twenty-three. And I never named a thing. He'd the skins filled and standing in the gate, and a price on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:299:*Three hundredth day. The track's end, outside the stone, at the hall's marsh. A hundred and twenty-three: four at a gate, for two skins, meal, a cheese, and corn. Vastin: twenty-one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:263:"A hundred and thirty-four," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:271:"A hundred and sixteen in hand," said Brom, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:9:*Three hundred and seventh day, night. The rim of the fifth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us tonight. At first light four go west, and one north.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:129:Lira saw it. She did not turn her head toward that end of the bench; but she stood a little straighter in her one great boot. Then her eyes went past Cael, sixty yards down the meadow, to the long eave. A square woman was standing there in an open doorway with a child's wrist between her fingers. She had plainly been standing there for some time. She looked at a girl with grass down one side and an arm hanging whole, and she said nothing whatever, and went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:145:He put no place on it. He folded it, and laid a copper on top, and left the two together inside Hesk's book for the boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:151:Seln had forty-six notches. Brom had asked Karis for the purse, and had it in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:163:Brom looked at the purse in his hand, and across a hundred yards of brown water at a stack under tarred sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:235:A boy came along the carts a little afterward, with a flat cake in his hand, and laid it on the stump at Vastin's elbow, and went away again without a word. It was warm. Vastin had been waited upon at tables for the whole of his service, by people whose names he had taken care to learn, and he had thanked every one of them. He looked along the carts for somebody to thank, and nobody was looking back. So he ate the cake, and put a copper on the stump where it had lain. The copper was there in the morning, with the frost on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:255:"No," he said. "You'd be off the rail at the first cake of ice. I can't use you. I'd not give a copper for you tonight, and I mean nothing by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:223:It was as the boy had drawn it. The bars stopped on the near lip and began again on the far one, twelve yards off, with the dark between. He went up the lip on his stomach, away from the iron, until he found the head of the cut and could cross on rock. Then he came down the far side as slowly as he had ever done anything, and lay in the grass above the path. The gap was sixty yards below him, and the wind was in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:255:He had no frame. He had given it back to the man that he hired it of, and paid the copper; and he looked exactly as he had looked on every other evening, which Cael thought must have taken some doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:169:He stood beyond the painted pole, on the kerb side of the carts' street, with a carrying-frame at his feet that he had hired of somebody for a copper; and when the handcart came through he took the frame up, and fell in at its tail, like a porter who has waited all afternoon for a load and has decided to walk home behind another man's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:97:"Copper," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:99:"A Copper would have done that with the grey on him, and I'd have thought it no more than his work. You did it in your shirt." The chin lifted an inch. "I watched your neck. You never lit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:157:"Now I've sixty yards of rope to buy going out, and a bowl to carry to a mill." He went down three steps. "He counted my neck. I've been counted by better; Pike never asked me what I was. Mind that landing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:33:"No goods," said Brom. He had the purse inside his shirt, and he put his hand flat on the place. "He said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:65:Pressure had begun to gather under his breastbone at the first sight of the raft. It was the narrow hard push that he had from the Bronze from Ardenmere, and at sixty yards it would have gone through a foot of ice as an awl goes through leather. He had learned in a shed what it did afterward. It went on, into whatever stood behind; and behind that ice were a deck, and a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:109:She lay almost end-on to him now, sixty yards out, with her bow toward the bank and her stern trailing down the stream. The ice had slid off her and gone. On her deck the ferryman was at the sweep, with his feet against the rail, heaving her head round. He could do that or he could do anything else. He could not leave it. The torn strap hung from its block over the place where her stern had been, with its tail whipping in the wind a yard out of anybody's reach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:161:"I know what's on my bank." But he said nothing about a figure, and he went back down the planks to the last of his passengers, who was getting over the rail with a stick in his hand. "A copper a head. You stood in her, same as the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:241:"A copper and a half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:243:"It's a copper," said Brom, under his breath. "It's been a copper since we came."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:247:Vastin had a small purse on a cord. He counted out one coin, and then a second, which had been cut across with a chisel; he looked at the cut edge for a breath, as though it were a kind of seal that he had not met, and laid the half beside the whole on her palm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:139:"There'll be none that'll touch me in there. I'm Copper in a book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:15:Brom left the file there, at two fingers from the front. He stood under the gate-walk with the mule's empty bucket, and said something up at it; and after a while a rope came down over the posts with a full one on its end, and went up again with a copper. Nobody opened the gate. Nobody put a face over the wall. He brought the bucket back along the road at arm's length, so that it should not slop, and held it under a brown nose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:187:Cael had fought that man through a whole cold term, and had never seen him do a thing so slowly. He went three paces, and stood; a grey head came up, sixty yards off, and looked at nothing, and went down, and he went three more. It took him as long as a pot takes to boil to cross that slope. Then he was a long pale shape kneeling at the black rock, with a skin's neck held under the place where it wept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:301:So four more had been wanted, and they had been fetched before the light went. The tall man took the first pair, and the boy took the second. Nobody had told him that he might; a captain had only looked at two skins lying on the grass, and then at him. He went down that slope at three steps and a stand, with nineteen grey backs above him and the whole herd gathered to the wet. Cael, who could see a furlong well enough and nothing nearer, watched a small dark shape take as long over sixty yards as it had ever taken over anything in its life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:131:The open place was empty. There were forty feet of trough, with the pipe running into its upper end, and not a beast at it, and the smith's two fires were banked. The man in the hood went to the pipe, and bent, and drank from his hand. He drank for a long time, and stood afterward with the water dripping from his fingers and looked at nothing, in the middle of a paved space with no cover on it for sixty yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:149:"In an empty street nobody hides," said Seln, into his collar. "He goes home. You have the right-hand houses; I have the left. Sixty yards. Do not match his step."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:153:*What does it weigh?* asked the read, of a tread sixty yards in front of him that he could have had through the soles of his feet, heel and toe, for as long as he liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:The purse had moved by two at the landing. The ferryman had been on his rope-house roof when they walked down the bank, on his knees, with his palm laid on Brom's shingles. It had kept a thaw out, and he said so, and five marks came up through Pike's hand before the supper was on, with a line said twice to go with them. Then he had looked at the brown mule, who was standing at his shed's door as though she had never left it; and he had asked at last. It was three, for the cold term, straw and standing, and he had named it without once lifting his eyes from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:Cael had Hesk's book inside his coat, and he did not need to take it out, since every figure in it had stopped at this river. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:61:In the west each of them had been a line in a registry: an Ember of the third rank in Iron, and a Wind of the first; an Iron Skin who had stopped at Copper and said so without shame; and a Shadow, Bronze, whom fifteen years of service had taught to be overlooked. Nobody out here had asked any of them for a tier since the ninth day. The board at the foot of the meadow priced the job and never the person. By its reckoning the five of them stood eleventh among the crews on a sheet of sailcloth, which was all the rank that this country kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:Karis took out the purse. She had paid six to the keeper without looking, four days before, with her eyes on a door. Today she opened her notebook first, and did the sum, and did it again beneath; then she counted twenty marks onto the tarred oak in four heaps of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:281:*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:303:"Every figure that I keep has stopped," he said. "The purse won't move till the fence. The cloth can't move at all. I've nine lines and no post, and a number that I've promised her not to change."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:97:"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:Cael read the slips from the left-hand post. The thaw had opened the holds, and the holds wanted hands. There was a mill-race to be cleared of ice, and a byre roof, and on the south road a bridge of two logs that the flood had carried a furlong. *Carts over, the first boat; hands on the landing; a copper the cart.* Under that one there were strokes already, in three different thumbs. Not one slip of the eight asked what a person was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:169:The hide-woman went away muttering, and Cael understood, watching her go, how that healer got through a bench of nine. She had a purse in her that she called her reserve, and a mending drew on it at about the rate of the hurt, a toe for a toe; and so she gave it only where nothing cheaper would serve, and gave words and mutton fat for the rest. Between the child and the carter she sat down on her keg with her head lowered, and her lips moved to fifty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:211:Brom was looking at the wet letters with his feet flat on the ground; he had been Copper in a registry's book for years, and had never once cared to see his name in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:217:One of the ten had sent its bill to somebody else. On the hundred and eighty-first day he took Lira's long knife up to the smith, with a copper of his own, and brought it back with an edge on it, and laid it beside her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:253:"Sixty yards," he said, when Brom went by. "Four-strand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:91:Nobody asked him how long a while was; he had given a great many words, for that purse. Cael came round the basket for the paper; there was a bucket beside the girl's that nobody had used, and he sat down on it at last, since his legs had been asking for an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:19:"Because of the blue coats," said Brom. "I'm Copper. That's this street and the one behind it. I read the boards."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:107:There was a colour for Copper, even. It was the street below, and the one behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:131:"Copper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:It was the large young man who had set it there, and he had six others on a board. Vastin felt for a copper. He was told, as he had been told on four evenings running, to put it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:241:"I did not go above the trough," said Seln. He sat down on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung, which was as near as he came to resting. "I hired a frame for a copper, and I sat for three hours on the porters' bench at the top of the street, and was twice offered work. The man beside me has carried to the registry's yard for eleven years, and he likes to be listened to. I have four things from him, and one difficulty. I shall give them in his order."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:87:Brom set his thumb to the carrier's way-slip, in the grease of the cart's own hub, beside the line that named the parcel; it was a mark that a one-armed man had seen on a good many slips, and would know. Cael found a copper of his own for the carrying. No name was asked for, and none was put down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:It was Brom's ruling, and he made it at the half-door with the purse in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:13:Brom built the fire in the ring, from thorn that he bought of the carters for a copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:"I should not care to have it thought that I walked here to lay a knee before a healer. It was sound when I set out, and it has carried me, and I have no claim on anybody's purse for it. I offer it to nobody. I had rather that were understood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:81:Cael had told a board that he would rule when the first boat was in, since a desk might have changed the price. The boat was in. He had the price now from the office that used to certify such things, and it was last year's price, to the copper. Nothing about it had altered but this: a second person had come to the same town's name, by another road, and had not known that it was already on their table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:133:Cael took Ephram's letter down himself. His eyes had come home by then, as far as print; he could read the single line under the fold, and the *C.* at its foot, and the date that he had put on nothing. He gave it to the ferryman's boy with the copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:15:"I'd not doubt it. He paid my woman a copper to be told where the straw was." The crook went out toward the moor. "Those are mine. I've not had a ewe on this top since the first frost, and I'd three hundred lambed in the yard for want of it. I've had thirty-one carts up that cut in a fortnight, where I told you thirty. The salting-shed's full to the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:"I didn't come up onto this top to make you glad. I've a thing to put to you, and I'll put it once, since I was told beforehand what I'd hear." The pale eyes had come to Brom, who had the purse. "That seep'll have its glass back at the first frost. Another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. I said so on that bank with your wet one standing by. I'd as soon not sit in my yard and wait to learn which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:95:He had given that word to a captain three times, at a hearth and on a landing. He had never before given it to this man. It was a clerk's word, and he had kept it from him as a person keeps his purse from somebody who once had the counting of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:"I did. I have sat on a board in front of six people for two days, going backward, at a copper a day. I find that the ground is no farther off, and that it is free."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:163:No copper was laid upon a stone that night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:223:Vastin got down off the tail-board for the last time, and paid his third copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:233:"Round a heart," said Brom. "Sixty yards. I paced it off against her wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:243:"It is." He stooped, and took the coil by its eye in one hand, and dragged it up the planks toward his rope-house without help. He said the rest over his shoulder. "She's a copper a head. Not today. Today she's paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:49:There was a keg by the fire, three parts full. Brom had drawn a skin from it before anybody could tell him not to, and then he had stood over it, frowning, and laid three coppers of his own on its lid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:155:Cael did not know what had been looked at. It might have been a case. It might have been a door, or a light on a crown, or the whole of fifteen years. He found that he could not ask. He had been given three words by somebody who weighed them like coin, and it was a great many for that purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:23:They roped the boxes. Brom took his on the right shoulder, as he had for four days, and Lira got her arms through her beckets, the right and then the left. Cael felt the third box settle between his shoulder-blades, with a purse in it under five thumbs. The old man hooked the tarred case with the head of his stick, and brought it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:179:He could do nothing else, and so he did that. He lowered himself onto the lid, with his two arms hanging and his heels dug in against the tread. The man below pulled at a rope with a great deal of a Copper from Velmere seated on the other end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:49:The yard lay at the room's lower end, and it was only a space between the cook-house and the next building, roofed over with boards and stacked to the height of a man with split logs. There was a hatch in the wall, and a boy at the hatch, to whom Cael gave a copper, and from whom he had a bowl and half the copper back. A dozen porters stood about among the logs, eating on their feet; and he found a place by the doorpost, with a stack at his shoulder, where he could see the whole length of the counter end on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:263:"A hundred and thirty-four," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:25:Cael turned the four sentences over while the stick came across the floor, since he had never heard that man waste one. A person who arrived in this country with a seal came over the board's head: he sent a clerk to the plank, or a paper, and had himself posted. This one had been told three times to go to twelve feet of oak, and had gone, and had read it from the left-hand post as any carter's boy would have; he had been asked what he had done, and had answered for himself, and had been taken at his word. He had paid a copper and a half for a loaf that cost the meadow a copper. The board had done to him what it did to everybody. Seln had wanted to know, before he stood away from a door, whether the man had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:41:He sat. It took him some time, with the leg, and he did it without putting a hand on anybody's shoulder. The keeper had come to his cask-room door to look at a stranger at the standing crew's board; Vastin lifted a finger to him, and asked what a bowl was, and put a copper down on the wood before Brom had lifted the ladle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:Brom filled the bowl, and set it before him, and looked at the copper. It was a thing that he could weigh, and he seemed to find the weight correct.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:163:"I thought so, and I read it again to be certain. I have read forty years of orders that said *must not*, and every one of them says who may grant leave. This one reserves no leave to anybody. It is not locked against the office. A lock is a thing that somebody has the fellow to." His hand closed on the board, slowly. "They sit upon it, and they have built the whole of their record on the top of it; and they could no more go into it than I could go into that hearthstone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:267:"There's the loft," said the keeper. He had not gone away; no keeper would have, with such a stranger at his board. "Up my stair, over the tap. Straw, and a blanket if you've not your own. A copper the night, and I don't carry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:271:The small purse came out on its cord, and a fourth coin went down on the wood. Vastin looked at it for a moment after his fingers had left it, as he had looked at the cut half of the other. It came to Cael that the man had very likely never paid for a bed in his life. An office had sent a clerk before him to every inn on every road that he had travelled, and the reckoning had gone home in a bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:"I felt it, at three of those lines. So did four of you. There was no dimming. It went out at one stride, for a third rank and a first and a Copper alike, and came back at the same stride, whole. It was not as a person tires. It was as a row of lamps—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:189:The roofs were slate, every one. The street had a gutter down each side of it, with water running, and no dung. The windows had glass in them. At a corner there was a cistern with a brass spout, and a girl was filling a jug at it; and she did not put a copper in any dish, since there was no dish. A row of children's voices came out of an upper window, chanting something all together, and he caught the shape of it before the words: it was a list, and it went up by steps, and at the top of each step there was a colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:9:He was grey, and his mouth was shut like a purse. A shin that had lain two ways the morning before took his weight, a little at a time, with a board bound along it. When he had gone the length of the shelf and back he shook the arm off, and stood by himself, and looked at the east as if it owed him money.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:11:They left a fire burning low, and a keg with three coppers on its lid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:159:"I said one word of it. I have kept a table since." She turned the notebook so that it lay toward them. "Every time that one of us has gone over such a line, I have put down who it was, and what that person carries, and of what rank, and the stride. I began it on the Fallow, for another purpose. There are three ranks of Iron in it, and a Copper, and a Bronze. Since yesterday morning there is a boy in it whom I had never seen cross anything, and a man who signed for other people's for forty years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:205:"Skins," he said. "There's a gate down there, and there's none for three days after. Mine have their own purse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:125:A woman with tarred hands had come up beside him. He asked her the price of sixty yards. She named it, and it was a fair one, since he nodded as he nodded at a wall that stood plumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:127:"I'll take it going out," he said. "I'll not carry sixty yards of rope up a hill to carry it down again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"A hundred and thirty-six," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:205:The old man had opened his mouth. Then he had looked at the nine who would have had to wait for him, and had shut it, and gone to the tail-board. He had offered his copper, and the carter would not have it; he said that it was the captain's fare, and paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:269:"She said both." He got the third up, and it was the reserve's box, which was the lightest; and they did hurt, in two straight lines, and they carried. "I'm entering that you were told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:3:The copper was on its stone in the morning, with the frost on it, and Vastin stood over it for a moment before he took it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:11:"A board's got a keeper. That's his pot, and he'll have his copper for it from anybody who isn't on a sheet." Brom drew the strap home. "A road hasn't one. Out here it's whoever carried the barley, and I carried it. Put that away."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:185:He slid the beckets off. Brom shifted along, and laid one forearm across the lid of the third box, with a purse in it under five thumbs, as a man lays his arm along a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:19:"You hung my fathom back as you found it. I've sold rope in this shed to men who'd not do as much for their mothers." She had the coil down already, across her two arms. "Sixty yards. It's the price that I named."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:21:He counted it onto the head of a tar barrel: two marks, in silver, out of the purse that lay inside his shirt beside a thumbed slip. He watched the coil while he did it, and Cael saw the frown go out of his face a little at a time, like a knot being worked loose with a spike. That debt had ridden in him since before the snow. It had gone over a river and come back, and up a moor, and into a town, and through eleven days under a registry. Here it lay at last, sixty yards of the right kind, across a woman's forearms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:33:"That's for the western landing," said the woman. "Three days down the moor road. There's a broker there who's sold three-strand for as long as I've laid it, and a fortnight since he sent up for four. Forty coils. He wrote that a big man from over the water had stood in front of his stack with a full purse, and called his rope the wrong kind, and gone away with the purse; and that he'd not have it said to him twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:55:"I have a copper," said Vastin. "I have had it ready since last night, in the expectation of being asked what I should cost to carry. I should prefer to pay it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:"There's a tail-board. It's a copper a day to sit on, and you'll get down for every hill; I'll not have my cobs pull what can walk. And you'll hold your tongue on the moor. I'll not need to tell you why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:61:He counted the copper into that brown palm, and it was kept. Cael saw the old shoulders ease by the width of a finger. It appeared that this road, at least, was going to take his money.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:131:He had not thought of that when the copper was paid. Vastin sat on the board's edge with the bound leg laid along a coil and the tarred case across his thighs, and both raw hands upon the stick's head; and since he faced the way that they had come, he faced Cael. There was two yards of road between them. It did not grow or shrink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:"A hundred and thirty-eight in hand," said Brom. He put the slip inside his shirt with the purse. "That's his, to draw the tack on. I'll have it after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:175:He said it without any sourness, as a clerk enters a balance that he does not yet know how to settle. Then he asked the hold's woman, who was passing with a pitcher, where a guest might sleep who was on nobody's sheet, and paid her a copper for the answer, and went out by himself into the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:205:The man had come out of the long house with his copper spent. He had stood under the lantern at the byre's door, looking down at the linen on his knee, and his right hand had gone to the breast of the brown coat, where a pen would have been. Cael knew the gesture by now. An officer hurt on duty wrote it up within the day, no doubt, under two hands, with the circumstances; there would be a sheet for it, and a tray. The hand had found a receipt in a small woman's writing, and nothing else. It had stayed there a moment. Then it had come away empty, and Vastin had looked up at the black shoulder of the hill, and for the space of three breaths his face had had nothing in it that Cael had ever seen there: no room that he was being fair to, and no file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:111:Brom looked at Cael, and Cael lifted one shoulder, under the strap. They got the loads off. Brom let his own box down to the floor by inches, with his knees, so that it should make no noise in that place; and the third came off Cael's back, with a third part of fifty-two sheets in it and a purse sealed under five thumbs, for the first time since a frost in an inn yard. Karis did not put anything down. She stood with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, at the very rim of that good light, and her face had gone as still as water in a butt. She had waited the whole of her life for this room. It appeared that she could wait while a stranger came to the end of his paragraph.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:289:"That's a copper to the smith each time," said Brom. "I'll have Karis enter it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:109:He did not come to the plank, and he asked nobody anything. Cael saw him go in to the keeper, and come out again putting up his small purse; he had paid for the second night in the loft, and it appeared that he did not mean to have a third. He took up his pack by its one sound strap. Then he stood by the gate with the stick grounded until the mule had gone through it, and Oryn after the mule, with her strip of canvas rolled under her arm. When the last of them was a dozen strides down the bank he came after, at his own pace, and kept that distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:137:"A copper a head, and she's a head." He looked at the boxes. "They're not. They're on her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:139:Brom counted eight coppers out of the small money into that long sour palm. Then he looked at what he had done, and took one back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:143:Vastin had his copper ready. He gave it with the short stiff bow, and stepped over the rail bad leg first, and went aft without being told, to the post where a new strap of stern line had been rove. He stood there through the whole of the crossing with his hands on his stick, a foot from the thing that he had held two days before with his back, and did not touch it. The ferryman, at the sweep, looked once at him and once at the strap. Cael thought that it was the nearest that man had ever come to thanking anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:149:Brom had the purse in his fist before the mule was off the scow. He had carried that debt since before the snow: sixty yards of four-strand rope, to a ferryman who would take no silver for it. A brisk man with ink on his cuffs came along the stack to meet him, with a clerk at his elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:151:"Cordage," said Brom. "Sixty yards. Four-strand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:"I've no doubt of it. He asked for four." Brom let the end go. "A four's laid round a heart, and it keeps its round in a block, and a ferry's rope lives in blocks. You could give me this, and I'd still be owing him sixty yards of four." He put the purse inside his shirt, where the dead slips lay. "A man lent us a rope and told us what he wanted back. I'll not hand him something else and call it paid, however good it is. When you've four, there's a hundred and eighteen marks will come down this road to look at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:257:Brom had brought seven bowls out of the pannier, and nobody had seen him count. He filled the seventh with what there was, which was cold barley and a heel of cheese, and set it on a stone; and Vastin thanked him by name. A little afterward a copper lay on that stone beside the empty bowl. Brom looked at it for a while, and left it where it was.

