editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:317:They had a thing among them which he was not told, and he had known that since a spring. *It's six people's*, the boy had said, on a road, and Vastin had found it the most courteous refusal that he had ever been given. He did not guess at it. He had spent a working life guessing at what people kept, with a seal to make them show it, and he had come out here in part to leave off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:15:Cael had his own stave, which was no longer than his forearm and hand, with a rag bound round the grip. He stood four strides off. Wind was in his feet, as it was at the start of everything; it leaned him away to the right, with a burst ready behind the lean, and he told it no and felt his own two legs take the weight instead. The read asked next, civilly, for the ground under that stave. He shut it. No Paths meant the ten, whatever he called them, and he had written so. They were not much. He had bought them on this grass, a touch at a time, from a seven-foot pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:He did not understand at first what she had done. She had slid her right hand down to the stave's heavy middle, and tucked the upper half of it along her forearm, as a person carries a pole through a doorway. She had a short stout stick now, in one hand. She came in with it very fast, and low. The thing darted at his thigh and was gone before his own wood got there, and came again at his wrist; and when he covered the wrist she went past him on the mended foot, turning, and rapped him smartly across the back of the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:45:She looked at him with her chin up. She had told him on this bench, in the snow, that he went back along a pole because he had never learned to do anything else. He had not liked hearing it either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:51:"On my arm's lateness. Not at my arm. There's a difference, and you know what it is, because somebody did it to you with a pole all the cold term and you wrote it down." She put her left hand back on the stave, a yard below the right, and shut the fingers one at a time. "I'll keep it on. You stop minding it. That's a bargain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:87:He knew what she was asking, and it was harder than anything that pole had ever asked of him. He had spent a cold term watching that arm held against her breast in a healer's knot. Every habit that he had was a habit of going round it. The tall man had fought him through a cold term and had never once struck at the taped side; but the tall man had a rule for it, and she had taken the rule away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:113:She did not pick the stave up by its middle this time. She took it as he had seen another person take a pole on that same ground, with her two hands a yard apart about its centre, so that she had as much wood before the leading hand as behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:143:*Ephram. You lose because you run it alone. I lost to a seven-foot pole all the cold term, three to none and then three to one, and each time somebody on a bench told me what I'd done before I had wiped the snow off. Find a bench. Tell Rooke the yard out here is quieter too. He'd not say it was worse. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:167:"Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:169:"Noted," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:277:"That's the same three that I counted you for a pole. Out, home, and don't look at the end of it. Look at his hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:279:"I lost to that pole the whole of a cold term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:169:He stood beyond the painted pole, on the kerb side of the carts' street, with a carrying-frame at his feet that he had hired of somebody for a copper; and when the handcart came through he took the frame up, and fell in at its tail, like a porter who has waited all afternoon for a load and has decided to walk home behind another man's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:21:Two yard-men were running already, with a pole. The man in the cloak was shouting for a lever, and pointing. They went where he pointed, to the high side, where the other wheel stood a foot clear of the ground, and got the pole's end under the axle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:27:"You'll roll it. It's sitting on that corner like a box on one becket. Lift the high side and the whole of it goes over onto him." He had come to the low corner. He crouched, and looked along the under side of the bed, and at the stub of the axle, and at a boot. "Leave that pole. Put it down. All that's holding it off his leg is the slate that slid, and the slate's not done sliding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:71:So he sat on the middle box, at the first stump, a pace outside the cut, with the ash held upright before him in both hands like a surveyor's pole. He looked along the line to the north, and then to the south, as he might have looked over a form before he signed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:157:"It's six people's," said Cael. "That's why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:159:The left-hand forge was the kind that Cael knew. It had a hood, and a bed of charcoal, and a great leather bellows with a lad swinging on the pole. A smith in a scorched apron was fitting a shoe to a carter's cob, in a stink of burned hoof, and three more carters waited behind with their beasts on short ropes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:93:"Noted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:257:He had watched it come back along a captain's arm, twice. It came up his own like a pole that has been struck on its end, from the palm to the elbow and through the shoulder; and it did not go down to any heel. It had never been going to reach so far. It stopped low on the left, in three old places at once, like a foot going through a cracked board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:He had reversed his spear. Cael saw that before he saw the long quiet face. The leaf of steel was behind him, pointing at the stones of the stair, and it was the butt of the ash that he carried foremost: seven feet of pole, blunt end first, as he had carried it in a square of pegs under a tree, where a touch was only a touch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:287:They went. The cleft was at the shelf's eastern end, a few strides from the stair's head, and to reach it they had to pass the two men who stood there. Nobody barred them. The first hood came on with a stave half raised, and saw which end of an ash pole was toward him, and went by it at the length of an arm and up into the rock. The others followed, one behind another, quickly, and not one turned to look. The three from the west had to pass a boy who knelt on their shelf with his arms round his own ribs. They went by him at the length of a stave, and nobody lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:291:When the last of them was by, he lifted the pole's butt a hand from the rock, and grounded it, once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:137:A light came round the corner by the chandler's, low, jogging, with a rumble of small wheels. It was the watch: two men in leather caps, and a handcart between them, and a lantern slung from a pole at its front.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:93:"Noted," Cael said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:They cleared it from the top side, as Lira had said, working east from a place twenty yards beyond the old cow, who lay last in the row. Seln and Cael had the keeper's shovels. Brom had lashed a barn board across the end of a pole, and he drove it before him left-handed, with the pole's butt in the pit of his good arm, so that it took off a yard of snow at a push. Karis came behind with a besom and swept what the shovels left. She had Hesk's clock on a stone, and a peg in the snow at every tenth yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:13:"He'd have named something. I'm not him." Lira looked him over from the boots upward, as she would have looked over a name on a card. "I don't want anything for it. I've an arm tied and a morning to get through. Stand there. No, a stride back. Now come at me, slowly, as if I'd a pole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:21:"Then you didn't know where the pole was, did you? Go back. Look first. Then go." She began to slap her knee. "Out. Home. Out. Home."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:31:The tall one grounded the pole, and lifted it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:The second time he did not wait to be sure. The rag came straight for his breastbone, and he let it have the place where he had been, by a hand's breadth, so that seven feet of ash went by his left shoulder close enough to stir his hair. *Out.* He felt it stop at the end of two long arms. *Home.* It began to gather; and he went in down the outside of the wood, with his sleeve rubbing along it, one stride and a second, until the leading hand was behind his elbow and the whole pole was a thing that lay beside him and pointed at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:41:The pale eyes came down to the place, and nothing happened in that long quiet face at all. Then the tall one lifted the pole's butt a hand from the snow and brought it down, once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:49:The tall one's hands had moved. They had been at the pole's butt and its first third; now they lay a yard apart about its middle, so that there were three and a half feet of wood before the leading hand and three and a half behind. It came out, and it was not long enough to reach him. He saw that. He went in on *home*, as he had been taught, and *home* was over before he had taken his first stride, since there was only half as much to gather. The other end of the pole came round from behind its owner's hip and met him under the short ribs on the right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:53:He had no answer ready for a pole with two ends. He stood off, and the tall one stood, and neither of them moved for some time. Then the rag twitched toward him, a foot, no more. His legs had been waiting ten days for that beat, and they went. There had been no *out*. He walked straight onto the point, which had never left home, and it stopped him by the breastbone as a hand stops a child at a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:65:"The hands moved, the very next breath. You showed that one a trick, and it was learned before you'd got back to your mark." She rubbed her nose with her knuckle. "I've a count for a long pole. I haven't one for a short pole with two ends. I'll have to sit here and think."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:81:He had the whole river from that roof. A cart had left the green. It was a heavy thing on four wheels, behind two mules, and its driver had cut the bend to save himself the furlong. It stood eighty yards out, a little upstream of the landing, with its tail gone down through the ice to the bed's edge and its pole pointing at the sky. The two mules were on their knees in the traces. Something dark was moving in the broken water behind the tailboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:157:Seln had gone to their heads by a way of his own, well out to the left, and Cael had not once had to tell him where to tread. He had his coat off and laid across the near mule's eyes. He had the end of the ferryman's line through the ring of the cart's pole, with a knot in it that would never be picked, and he looked back along eighty yards of rope to the round shed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:171:Cael did not see the drum turn. He saw the rope come up out of the snow. It rose along its whole length, dripping, and drew as straight as a ruled line from the shed to the cart's pole, and creaked. The two cart mules felt the collar ease. They got their feet under them, with Seln's voice going on between their ears as evenly as a clock, and the tilted bed began to rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:69:Cael heard it done from the lean-to: a rattle of chain, and a gate going down, and then the great wheel slowing through three turns, each longer than the last, until it stood. The knocking that had gone on under every word since they came into that valley left off. There was only the race, running by to waste, and the small hiss of the lantern on its pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:269:"If there's road," said Lira, and he heard her lie down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:111:He saw it arrive, a stride from his face. The straight arm jarred from the palm to the shoulder, like a pole that has been struck upon its end. There was the small dry click at the wrist, which he had heard once across twenty paces of grass. And there was a second sound after it, which he had not heard in any meadow. It was duller, and it was higher up the arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"Noted," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:135:It was a pole, painted in bands, let down across the way between two posts; and beyond it the road dropped into the long sloping yard that he had crossed fourteen times from the other end. He could see the dock, and the sheds, and the back of five storeys. Beside the bar stood the beam on its post, with a pan as broad as a cartwheel hung from one arm by three chains. A man in a leather cap sat under it on a stool. He had a slate on his knee, as big as a tray, ruled in columns.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:199:"Behind it. Noted?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:201:"Noted," said Teague's boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:209:It was a deep voice, and unhurried, and Cael had never heard it. He had lost to that man's pole for the whole of a cold term. He had kept the other side of a captain from him at two timbers, and had watched his hand lie upon a boy's breastbone like a gatepost; and in all of that, across a cold term and a road, he had not had one word from him. Lira's chin had come up. Brom had stopped with a strap half buckled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:273:"You're quick. I told you so in a yard. Quick's a fine thing when there's a pole coming. Under one of his, the whole of the trick is that you go nowhere, whatever you see. I stood under it in a stair with a knife in my hand, and I wanted to go six times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:97:*Write it down and stop touching it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*. A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go. Laid on a boulder in a field it has nowhere to run. A patient man may walk it round the outside, and knot it, and learn how far the thing is about; but of what lies in the stone the cord gives no figure whatever, and it was that blank which he had been entering, with the day at its head, as though the stone had told him so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:151:"Noted," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:But the tall man had been looking at him. He stood behind his captain's right shoulder, with seven feet of ash in his fist; and when Cael's eyes came to him, he lifted the pole's butt a hand from the rock, and grounded it, once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:219:"Noted," Cael said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:63:They stood with their backs to it. They wore long coats of no colour, and hoods, and each had a cloth tied across the lower half of his face, so that there was nothing under the hood at all. Each had a stave. They were not porters' poles. They were as long as a man and as thick as a wrist, and shod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:107:*Write it down and stop touching it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:165:"You've one up there that you couldn't tell," said Lira. She did not say it unkindly; she had read two lines in a book, the night before, and had read them twice. "You wrote that you can't tell the taking without the rest, and the rest isn't yours. It's six people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:"Noted," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:143:There was a cart there, on its side, with one wheel in the air. Beyond it lay the bed of a second, without wheels, and a scatter of grey staves that had been barrels. A third had gone clean off the road at the bottom and lay in the heather with its pole snapped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:205:"Mind the stair. It leans."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:229:Seln was at the painted pole, with a hired frame at his feet. He fell in at the cart's tail, like a porter who has been paid to, and said what he had to say to the setts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:11:"I said that my crew comes, and it comes. But I'll not walk it up to one of those places as it is. In there a Path's a thing that you had yesterday." He looked at the fire while he told it off, as he might have told off a load. "My spear's a tall body with a pole. My bow's a man with a bad hip. The boy's a boy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:49:There was a bundle of sticks in the snow by the bench, cut to every length, and Cael chose a stave of ash as long as his arm, since the only steel that he owned was a knife. The tall one took a pole. It was seven feet of the same wood, as thick as a wrist, with a wad of rag bound over one end; and its owner carried it out to the middle of the square as that spear had been carried on every road that Cael had seen it on, upright, and grounded at each stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:55:The pole's end was against his thigh before he had finished the thought.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:63:The read asked next, civilly, as it always did. A pace and a half would have had that pole from the moment that its butt left the snow: its weight, and which way the weight was going, a full beat before his eyes could tell him. It did not feel like doing anything. Not a person in that meadow would have known. Teague had said *not a sigil*, and Cael had no sigil, and never had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:He tried the outside. He went round to the tall one's left at a fighter's shuffle, to make the pole come across its owner's body; and the tall one turned on the back heel, a hand's breadth, with no more effort than a door takes, so that the rag was pointing at his breastbone again. He ducked in under it. It drew back through the leading hand like a bolt through a staple, a yard, and came out, and struck him on the point of the right shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:71:His shoulder sang with it. He set his teeth and did what a boy does who has been hit twice from a distance, which was to go at the thing that was hitting him. He beat the pole aside with his stave, with all his arm, and stepped in behind the blow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:73:The pole went with the blow. It dipped under his stave, and came round in a circle no bigger than a plate, and laid itself along his right ribs as lightly as a hand is laid on a shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:77:He stood in the trodden snow with his breath going up. He had been touched on the thigh, and the shoulder, and the right side; and in each of those exchanges his left had stood open, since he had been guarding it for seventy days by habit, and three times that pole's end had gone past the opening without so much as looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:79:The tall one grounded the pole, and walked back to the eastern pegs. No word had been spoken on that side of the square from the first step to the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:83:He had not been watching the pole. Cael understood, late, that the man had kept his eyes on a pair of boots for the whole of it, and had learned more from them in three touches than a boy with a book had learned from a face in a dozen readings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:113:"It comes out and it goes home. Did you see? Out's quick. You'll not beat *out*; nobody will, off that one. But it has to be gathered again before it can come a second time, and that's slower, because there's seven feet of it to gather." She slapped her knee twice, a quick one and a slow one. "Out. Home. You go on *home*. Not back, and not round. In. Down the outside of the wood, close enough to rub your sleeve on it, till you're past the front hand. Inside the hands a pole's a broom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:231:There was the sound of a pole being laid down across a floor, and the steps stopped. When they began again, they were slower.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:173:"Noted," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:319:She had lit a lantern in the lean-to, and hung it from the turf roof's pole. The wheel was still going on the other side of the stone, and Brom sat on the plank across two kegs, with his shirt off, like a boulder that somebody had brought indoors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:401:"Noted," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:209:They drilled him for the last of the light, and it was Lira who did most of it. She had taught a boy from another crew to wait for a pole, and she went at an Archmarshal in the same fashion and with no more ceremony, walking backward before him down forty yards of rutted road with her right hand going. He stood for the flat hand and came on for the two fingers; and when the fist was thrown out to the left he looked left, and found a thorn-bush, and regarded it with great attention.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:He had made the factor say it twice. The old man with the stick had stepped out, and struck the ice once, and there had been a thing across the cut from one bank to the other, as high as the scree. Eight hounds broke on it. It stood for exactly as long as the man never lifted a foot; and when it went, he went down with it, like a tent with the pole drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:83:Teague looked at him, and he was all wrists still. He had grown the width of two fingers since the snow, and had learned from a girl on a bench to wait for a pole; but he went at each thing a half-beat before he had seen the whole of it, and nobody had yet got that out of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:249:Something came back. Cael saw it arrive: the straight arm jarred, from the palm to the shoulder, like a pole that has been struck on its end. And he heard the wrist, at twenty strides, across a meadow: one small dry click, such as a latch makes.

