    11	He catalogued the fire because it was going to be the place, and a place should be had before the thing that happens in it.
    12	
    13	The inn's yard, walled on three sides, a shed on the fourth, empty. A brazier on legs, iron, with the inn's kindling in it and a broker's broken crate on top of that, burning low. Six of them round it, and the seventh not there, on a north road, with a cup that Lira had brought out from the long table and set on the wall at the far end where a healer sat when she sat. Karis on an upturned bucket with the notebook shut in her lap and her bound hand on it and her face the colour of the ninth day of a week that had been asked for as seven, sitting the way a woman sits who has stood up from a table for the first time in ten hours and has found that her legs have opinions. Brom on the shed's step with the foot out. Seln at the yard gate, facing the ring road, because the ring road was where a thing would come from. Vastin on the wall, on the knee, with the stick across his knees. And Cleon.
    14	
    15	Cleon had come up.
    16	
    17	That was the thing Cael catalogued twice, because it was the thing he had not expected. The man had never once, in nine days, left his table before the bell, and had never once come up the stair with the party, and had never once been seen above ground by any of them except on a stair at noon going to a table on the third tier to eat alone; and he had come up with them tonight, at the bell, with the leaf left on his shelf under the registry and nothing in his hands, and had walked down the carts' street behind the handcart like a man who did not know how to walk behind a handcart, and had come into the yard, and had stood at the brazier's edge in a coat that had been good once, and had said nothing, and nobody had asked him why, because the why was on a leaf.
    18	
    19	Cael took the notebook from Karis. She gave it to him without opening her eyes. He opened it to the page — the passage in the small square hand; the patch drawn as a patch, with its one upright stroke; the three words under it — and crossed the yard to Lira, at the brazier's far side, and put it in her hands, open, and did not say anything, and stepped back.
    20	
    21	---
    22	
    23	She read it.
    24	
    25	He watched her read it because it was the one thing in the yard he was going to let himself watch, and because he had watched her read a great many things in four years — a posting on a board, a page in a hearing, a line on a wall sheet — and had never once watched her read a thing that was about him and had been written before either of them was born.
    26	
    27	She read it the way she did everything, which was fast, and then she read it again, which she did not do with anything, and her face did not change, because Lira's face did not change when a thing mattered; it changed when a thing did not. She read the three words at the bottom. She looked at the patch drawn as a patch. She closed the notebook, and held it a moment, and handed it back across the brazier, and he took it, and she looked at him — not the count; not a finding; the look she had given him in a hall at sixteen when a marshal had gone inside to read a page — and said the one true thing, which was not comfort, because Lira had never once in her life mistaken comfort for the truth.
    28	
    29	"So the whole time they weren't afraid you'd break something. They were afraid you'd *exist*."
    30	
    31	The fire took the next few seconds, and nobody gave them to anything else.
    32	
    33	He had known, coming up the stair, that she would say the true thing, and had not known what the true thing was, and now he had it, in nine words, from the person who had always been first, and it was worse than the leaf and it was better, because the leaf was a record and this was Lira, and a record sits and Lira did not.
    34	
    35	"Yes," he said.
    36	
    37	"Right." She put her two hands at her sides. "Then that's what it is."
    38	
    39	That was the whole of Lira's part, and it was, from Lira, the whole of the thing.
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	The circle, then, because that was the order: Lira first, and then the circle, and the circle took a thing in the order it took things, and had done since a hall.
    44	
    45	Brom priced it. He did it from the shed's step with the foot out, looking at the fire and not at anyone, at the length of a ledger line, which was how Brom said a thing that had cost him money and would cost him more. "So it's not that they didn't have a column for him." He turned it over once. "It's that the whole sheet was drawn so there couldn't be one." He was quiet. "That's a bad sheet. I've turned down a real job on the strength of it being a bad sheet, and it's worse than I priced it." He did not say anything else, and did not need to, because Brom had said the thing in the ledger's terms and the ledger's terms were the ones the crew kept.
    46	
    47	Seln said one thing. He said it from the yard gate, with his back to the fire and his eyes on the ring road, in the voice for an inventory, and it was about the cache, and it was the nearest thing to a decision that Cael had ever heard him make aloud. "There's a thing on my back that a registry would pay for," he said, "and the registry's built on that leaf. I'd wondered, for four years, what I was keeping it from." He did not turn round. "Now I know what the office is *for*. That's worth knowing. I'll keep it a while longer." That was all. He had looked at a door at night and had decided nothing, and had heard a leaf read, and had decided nothing again, and had said so, which was a thing Seln did not do, and Cael entered it where he entered the things that were true about Seln, which was a short page and had just got longer.
    48	
    49	Karis had two pages, and said so, and said nothing else, and the saying was the last thing she did that day before she was done, because a woman who has read a leaf three times in a hole and copied it once by a lamp another person held has spent the year and the week she had, and the crew knew it, and let her spend it, and did not once say her name to wake her, because a seat kept is a seat kept whether the person in it is asleep or on a north road.
    50	
    51	She said it with her eyes shut, from the bucket, with her hand on the notebook that Cael had given back to her: "Two pages. The leaf says a man made the apparatus and why. A dead surveyor says a hundred lamps go out on one wire. I'm not drawing a line. I've said it in a hole and I'll say it at a fire. I've a year and a week of not-guessing behind me and I'm not going to spend it tonight because I'm tired." She opened her eyes and looked at Cael, once, across the brazier. "And I'm not going to say the third word again. I said it once, to the leaf. It's yours now. Do what you want with it." And she shut her eyes, and was, he thought, asleep before the sentence had finished being in the air, sitting up, on a bucket, with her hand on a book, which was a thing he had seen her do at a marsh and had not seen since.
    52	
    53	Cleon said nothing.
    54	
    55	He stood at the brazier's edge with his hands at his sides and looked at the fire the way he looked at a leaf — not at it; at the place between it and himself — and Cael, who had watched him for nine days, guessed that the man was not thinking about the leaf; he had said at a table that he had done a decade's thinking on it, and a decade was all the thinking a leaf allowed. He was thinking, Cael guessed, about the yard: six people and an empty shed and a cup on a wall for somebody who was not there, and a boy with twelve pieces of a craft he had been told about in a hole, and the thing that the man had been right about for thirty years, sitting on a bucket asleep and standing at a gate and sitting on a step with a foot out, being what the leaf said, and none of them looking at him for an answer, because a leaf does not need a man to answer for it. He had said, at a table, that he had not slept well for ten years, and Cael did not think tonight was going to be the exception. He had come up a stair he did not come up, to stand at a fire he had not been asked to, because a thing he had carried alone had just been carried into a yard by six people, and a man who has carried a thing alone for a decade wants, once, to see it carried, and then he can go back down.
    56	
    57	He stood a while longer. Brom, who did not talk to strangers, said to him, from the step, "You came up," and Cleon said, "I came up," and Brom said, "Good," and that was the exchange, all of it, and it was, Cael thought, the first time in thirty years anybody had told the man that a thing he had done was good, and the man did not know what to do with it, and stood with it, and kept it.
    58	
