    60	
    61	---
    62	
    63	Vastin spoke when the fire was at its working red.
    64	
    65	He had not moved. He sat on the wall with the stick across his knees and the stillness on him that Cael had seen in a hole that morning, and had not shifted it, and had said nothing through Lira and Brom and Seln and Karis and Cleon, because none of it had been his, and he was a man who had learned, this week, at some cost, not to say a thing that was not his. And then it was his, and he said it, to the fire, in the ledger voice, with nothing in the ledger but the sum.
    66	
    67	"I believed it was imperfect." He said it as a man reads a line from a file he wrote himself. "That's what I told a boy across a table in a borrowed room in Ostrand, in the autumn, and it was the truest thing I had, and it had taken me forty years to arrive at. Imperfect. A machine with a fault in it, that had filed a boy wrong." He was quiet. "In Ostrand I learned it was corrupt. Not a fault. A hand on the scale, at the top, deliberately. I resigned over that, or walked, and I have spent a winter thinking that was the bottom of it, because a man who finds out the thing he served was corrupt thinks he has found the bottom." He looked at the fire. "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*."
    68	
    69	The man was not done, and the fire knew it, and nobody spoke into the gap.
    70	
    71	He said the word carefully, as a man says a word from a form. "That is what the leaf says. I heard it read. The un-bound. A kind. The whole of the apparatus — the measure, the seat, the tier, the mark — set over all the land so that a kind should not come again. I have administered that apparatus for forty years. I have signed the mark at more kindlings than I could count if I had a winter to count them. I believed, every time, that I was measuring what was there." He stopped. "It was never for measuring. It was for *preventing*." He looked, then, for the first time since the fire was lit, at Cael. "At a category. Which turned out to be a person. Who's sitting across the fire."
    72	
    73	Lira, across the fire, said, to Vastin, the only thing anyone said to him that night, and it was not kind and it was not unkind; it was Lira: "You signed the mark at kindlings."
    74	
    75	"I did."
    76	
    77	"His?"
    78	
    79	"No. I read it, after. I did not sign it." A pause. "I would have."
    80	
    81	"Yes," Lira said. "You would have." And she let him have that, because it was true and he had said it himself, and a man who says the worst thing about himself before he is asked has paid for it, in the crew's ledger, and does not have to pay twice.
    82	
    83	He did not say anything else. He did not draft anything. He sat on a wall with the stick across his knees and the Compact's own held line looked across a brazier at the thing the line had been drawn to prevent, and did not look away, and did not ask for anything, and Cael looked back, and did not give him anything, because there was nothing to give and the man had not asked, and the two of them sat with it, and the fire burned down.
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	He had it. He had had it since the leaf, and had held it at a lamp for a day and up a stair and across a yard and through five people, and he had not let himself have it until they were done, because a thing you take should be taken after, and not in the middle of somebody else's taking.
    88	
    89	He took it now. He did not catalogue it.
    90	
    91	He stood up first. He did not know why, and did not ask himself; he stood up from the wall he had been sitting on and stood at the brazier's edge with the fire at his shins, because a thing you are going to take you take on your feet, and the five of them let him stand, and did not look at him, because the family's register for a thing being taken was to leave the taker his face. That was the discipline, and it ran the other way tonight: he had spent a day not letting himself count a thing, and now he let himself have the thing and did not count it, because a man who counts a thing like this has found a way not to have it, and he had promised a healer he would find out what the log did to him, and here was the one thing he was not going to put in the log first.
    92	
    93	He had come inside believing the system had no place for him.
    94	
    95	That was true. He had believed it since a room at fourteen with a door that locked from the outside, and he had written it in a plain hand at the end of every year since — *no tier, no rank, no file I can see* — and it had been, at the end of the last year, a relief, on a landing in the snow, and it had gone soft with use over a winter, and he had walked into a city with it in his coat like a thing he had stopped noticing the weight of. *No place for me.* An omission. A machine that had been built for other people and had not, when it came to him, had a column, and had filed him under the nearest thing, and had shut a door on him that it would have shut on anyone the machine could not read. He had been on the wrong side of the door by accident of what he was. That was the sentence. He had lived in it for five years and it had a shape he knew to the hair.
    96	
    97	The leaf did not say that.
    98	
    99	The leaf said the door was him.
   100	
   101	Not shut on him. *Built* — all of it, the measure and the seat and the tier and the mark at the kindling, every gate on every tier of every city, every hall he had stood in at fifteen and seventeen, every page a marshal had read and declined to read, a whole continent's ordering, older than any wall on it — built, the leaf said, and set over all the land, for one reason, and the reason was written once and the writer had cared about nothing else being remembered: that a kind should not come again. Not *excluded*. A thing that is excluded is a thing the makers forgot to make room for. A thing that is *prevented* is a thing the makers built the whole house to keep from being born in it.
   102	
   103	He was not the case the machine could not parse.
   104	
   105	He was the reason the machine had been built to parse.
   106	
   107	He sat with that. He did not turn it into a count. He did not put it beside the still place, or the cord, or the light on a hill, or the row that came out short, because those were entries and this was not an entry; this was the page the entries were written on. Five years. He had thought, for five years, that the system had forgotten him. It had not forgotten him. It had been *waiting* for him, since before the walls, with every door shut, and he had walked up to the first one at fourteen and it had done exactly what it was built to do, and had called that a fault, and had filed him, and he had carried the filing across a continent thinking it was an accident.
   108	
   109	It was not an accident. That was all of it. It was the most deliberate thing anyone had ever done.
   110	
   111	He looked at the fire. He thought of a landing in the snow with a ferryman's ropes on it, and a man who had stopped numbering him because the number had got too small, and a woman on a north road who had said she did not know what he was, and a healer's two fingers on a wrist, and a clerk's pen going across four faces at the speed of four boxes, and he found that every one of them was still true, and that none of them was any smaller for the leaf, and that the leaf had made them, if anything, a thing worth having in a way they had not been before, because a man who is prevented and happens anyway has a different ledger from a man who is merely forgotten, and the entries on it are the same and the sum is not.
   112	
   113	Lira was across it, with her two hands at her sides, looking at him, and she had said the thing, and he had it, and the having was not a change; he did not permit himself a change declared at a fire, only a difference filed, and he filed it, once, aloud, in the only sentence he was going to allow himself, and said it to the fire and not to anyone.
   114	
   115	"For five years I thought the system forgot to make room for me," he said. "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen." He turned it over once. "I happened anyway."
   116	
   117	Lira, across the fire: "Yeah." A beat. "Now what."
   118	
   119	He took the straightedge out of his coat, and the dividers came with it, and he put the dividers back and kept the edge. "Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine." He looked at the straightedge in his hands. "I want to stand in the middle of it."

