/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n -F -e 'In the years of the unmaking' -e 'Since my Kindling I thought' -e 'So the whole time they weren' -e 'Four hundred years of a whole world' -e 'West,\" said Brom' -e 'He had spent the whole year learning' -e 'That is as far as the instruments go' books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-{13,14,15,16,21,22,23,24}.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:27:"*In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong* — that's the record's phrase; I've read it four times and it says what it says — *there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more.*"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:99:*Three hundred and seventh day, night. The court's rim. A hundred and twenty-three in hand; nothing owed; nothing earned. Nineteen. Thirteen: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. One anomaly, the still place, read by two instruments — the tenth, which says nothing, and the twelfth, which says a shape — and understood by neither. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. The same kind of made. That is as far as the instruments go and I am not going past them.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:137:"West," said Brom, and picked up the box.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:139:He had spent the whole year learning that the world was built to prevent him — four hundred years of tiers and registries and a machine that lit for everyone but him, all of it laid down so that what he was would never come again. And at the center of the first place they ever made, the oldest silence on the continent, the one built before there was a word for it, he had felt no exclusion and no prevention and no wall. He had felt at home. He did not have a word for that yet. But he knew, now, walking back out across a floor older than the system that had expelled him, that it was not an accident — that the place built to end him was the one place he fit — and that the difference between *the system was built to exclude me* and *the system was built to prevent me* had a third sentence past it, waiting, that he was not ready to write. Someone had made the pattern. He was, he was beginning to understand, the pattern. That was as far as he could say.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:29:"So the whole time they weren't afraid you'd break something. They were afraid you'd *exist*."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:115:"For five years I thought the system forgot to make room for me," he said. "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen." He turned it over once. "I happened anyway."

