editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:165:Nobody hurried. The boxes stood ready by the cross-way, three of them, roped, with a tally lying on the lid of the third under a stub of pencil. It had two hands' marks at its foot, a K. and a square upright V. Cael had seen the old man look at his own letter after he had made it, as at a thing that he had not expected to be asked for again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:89:It was his own count, and it was made with his own eyes, which could not do two things at once. The lean came up short against it, as a dog comes up against a door that has been shut without unkindness. At the third pier the grit had not come. At the fifth he knew that he had found a thing which he could use. He had spent his life counting what was in front of him so that he need not feel it, and for once the habit was worth something to somebody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:147:That was the weight of the morning, and it was all in those two yards. He had entered a wall once, at this man's asking, and had entered more than was asked for. He could not pay it. A thing of that kind could not be told by itself; whoever heard how the eleventh had come would have heard how all of them came, and that was not Cael's to hand over. It belonged to four others who walked in front of the dray, and to a healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:219:It was a country for people who had made their peace with one colour. The swells went away eastward, brown beyond brown, with the ruts drawn over them like a pair of pencil lines; there was no tree, and no wall, and the only thing that stood higher than a sheep was the dray. The wind came out of the east steadily, with nothing in it but cold. It found the gap between a collar and a neck. It leaned on a box as a hand leans, and by the second mile Cael had the ache of it across both shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:267:He had written none of it. He had said none of it, to a healer or a girl or a man with a lamp. In the fifteen days since, a leaf had been read aloud in a gallery which made a good many things look different, and he had not once let himself carry that sheet across to stand beside it. That was for the clean page, if it was for any; and a woman had laid that page down bare, with a bound hand, and had said that nobody was to rule a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:115:"I saw what you carried, and I saw what you took in the arm setting an old man down." The grey eyes went over him once. "A count can't do that part. I knew it when I made the count. I'd not have you think that I hold you short."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:289:Cael looked at them. There was a quiet man, and a girl with a box on her back, and a big one with his arm in linen. There was a small pale woman who had been right, and an old man on a stick, with a surveyor's case. He made the sixth himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:303:He had spent the whole year learning that the world was built to prevent him — four hundred years of tiers and registries and a machine that lit for everyone but him, all of it laid down so that what he was would never come again. And at the center of the first place they ever made, the oldest silence on the continent, the one built before there was a word for it, he had felt no exclusion and no prevention and no wall. He had felt at home. He did not have a word for that yet. But he knew, now, walking back out across a floor older than the system that had expelled him, that it was not an accident — that the place built to end him was the one place he fit — and that the difference between *the system was built to exclude me* and *the system was built to prevent me* had a third sentence past it, waiting, that he was not ready to write. Someone had made the pattern. He was, he was beginning to understand, the pattern. That was as far as he could say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:17:"This is his drawing of the fourth line. He drew what was there, and where he could tell a stroke of the writer's from a scratch of the knife's, he has ticked it. This is mine, made the same afternoon with a sewn hand." The pencil crossed to the leaf. "I was sure of three signs in the last six, and I told him so. In that gallery, by one lamp, with the skin before me, it was the truth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:175:It was one word. He had not begged anybody's pardon, or said that he was drafting. He had made a stroke at the foot of the thing, as a person does who has heard a term and means to keep it; and then he looked up the table at the boy whose word it was, with something in the grey face that was very nearly surprise at himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:339:The old woman said nothing. She took the webbing up again, and laid it shorter this time, in two yards and not four, with the turn behind the arm, and drew it down with a jerk that made the big man grunt. Then she looked at the healer, and Oryn looked at the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:1:# Chapter 39 — Aimed
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:27:*Aimed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:191:"On skin. She's made it fair. It's in my coat, and it's to be read; she's asked that nobody say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:173:Cael made an entry then, in his head, since there was still no hand for a book. He made it before a boot went over, and in the tense that it would have to be true in afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:285:Beside Karis, Teague's boy had made a noise in his throat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:5:It was full dark by then. The lamp over the painted door made a round of yellow on the far side of the arch, and the basket made another in the yard, redder, with the pump and the mounting block standing at its edge. A horse shifted in the stable, and somewhere up the hill a bell was rung for the shutting of a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:125:"I held that for the whole of my service, and was easy in it. Above Ostrand I was shown that the fault was meant. I called it corrupt; I put down a seal upon that word, and walked three hundred miles on it, and thought that I had come to the floor." The stick's foot turned upon the cobbles. "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:185:"Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," he said. "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:The star was gone. There was a smear where it had been, with a halo to it, among a great many other smears; he chose the lowest, and hoped. He had four hundred and six strides still to go, and he would have to walk them as a person crosses a room in the dark, with nothing to trust but the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:105:*Four hundred. Four hundred and one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:235:"As I made it," said Karis, very low. She had her notebook open already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:325:The old sentence began itself in him then, as it did whenever he was tired: the one about a man, and an apparatus. He had been stopped in it twice by a bound hand, and he found that he no longer needed the hand. He set it down unfinished, among the nine orders that he had not drafted on a shelf of shale, and it lay there quietly enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:65:Cael counted heads, since a hill had come down on them and he knew of nothing else to do. He made it ten under the roof, and an old man at its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:125:"A boy was killed by the sixth flight. I went over the lip as the seventh was loosed. There is one flight between the two, and I have asked myself where I lost it." He kept his eyes on the path. "Before the fourth there was no slab. There was a bank that would not have borne a cat. From the foot of the bare rock to the lip took me three flights, at the pace that a man goes who must not be seen. I have made it that each time. It does not come out as two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:287:"It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:143:"The ravine bends there. It turns in toward the middle, and cuts the fence; you can see the bars stop on its near lip and begin again on the far one. At the bend its edge comes to within a stride and a half of the iron." He made the two lines nearly touch. "That's their door. Past it there's the path that Seln saw, a pace wide, going on round the fence to the east. It's the only way off that shelf that isn't through their own timbers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:151:"It is the fourth ring. It has the stroke and the bar, as the three behind us have, and the office has held a sheet upon it for two hundred and six years. It is twice their measure: four hundred and forty yards from that iron to the middle, and two strides." She spoke as she would have read out a label. "And I have not one sheet from inside it. Nobody among us has put a foot over that line. I can tell you what three such places have done to five people, three times. They took every sigil at a stride, and gave it back at a stride, whole. I cannot tell you that a line of twice the round does the same."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:195:He had not been able to say so by one lamp, a hundred and eighty days ago. He could say it now with his eyes shut. The most of the bars were scaled as the old stake at the Stair was scaled, in leaves, like bark. He would have put five cold terms upon those, and perhaps ten. Here and there among them stood a bar with only a skin of rust on it, set since. Where one had rotted at the turf, somebody had drawn it, and driven a new one into the same hole. And every thirty or forty paces he came to one of another kind. It was thicker than its neighbours by a finger, and its head had been beaten by a heavier mallet. The scale on it had scaled again, and come away, and scaled, until the thing was as rough as a root.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:203:It was out of him before any of the others had drawn breath. He nodded as he said it, with the whole of his head, as a horse nods; and the broad man beside him made a sound in his throat that might have been a laugh on another morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:331:He had not been grey, and he could not have been. A man cannot go to stone and set another down gently in the same breath; and Brom had been offered that choice in a stream-bed, with three bows bent on him, and had made it without so much as lifting his head to see what it would cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:5:Brom had one corner, and the tall man another. Teague's boy had the third, and would not give it up; and Cael, who had only the one arm that morning, was told by Lira to stand aside and took the fourth all the same until she put him off it with her shoulder. They laid the man in the hut that seven strangers had slept in, on a heap of somebody else's bracken. He had not made a sound that could be heard at five yards, from the bed of the ravine to the blanket in the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:111:"I'd made a rule about ledges," he said. "In an inn, before we walked. I'd told myself that a man who turns back on one for somebody who isn't his gets his own killed. I thought that I'd have to keep it against one of theirs." He looked at the linen. "I never priced keeping it against you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:171:He knew the mark. He had seen it in pencil on a sheet of paper, in a back room, with a small pale woman's finger beside it; he had gathered that it was a grade of some kind, and had to do with how old a thing was. He had not come for marks. He had said so, at her door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:203:It was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides. He had seen the figure written by lamplight, on a night when nobody would cross. It was a long way to walk with nothing on either hand, and the grey thing grew as they came, and he found that he could not make it stop growing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:311:"It was fifty-two," he said. "I'll enter that I made it less, twice. I'll not enter why, since I don't know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:357:*A course on the west pier: fifty-two signs. I made it fifty-one, twice. Ruling: hold.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:15:She made a mark in her notebook, and the file went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:181:"If a man designed the apparatus, then the sigils —"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:133:He did the whole of it three times, and each time the slip went down once, and there was no room upon that rock to lay it a second time.

