editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:"We know what we may not do," she said. "No sigil, anywhere on that hill, for any reason. I should like to know what we may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:97:"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
