editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:7:So Cael took her down to the river later that same morning, the hundred and thirty-first past the Line, through a hand's depth of new snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:17:The prices were what he knew best, since he had carried all ten down the roads of this country for a hundred and thirty days and used them as seldom as he could. He could have said to a grain what each fragment charged him for being kept shut. He knew a good deal less about what each would do when it was let out, and where it would fail him, and what it cost afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:153:The horse-walk was a ring of beaten earth round the drum, eight strides across. He chose the post that faced him on the far side. Wind had been in his feet since a painted floor, a long while ago; it was the first of the ten, and hers, and for a hundred and thirty days it had leaned him toward the first stride of everything while he told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:199:Pike brought his book out to the plank at noon on the hundred and thirty-third day, for the first time since the ferry shut, and there were three slips on twelve feet of oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:281:*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:3:Lira took the longest stave in the bundle, on the two hundred and seventy-third morning, and nobody who knew her could have mistaken why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:23:It was the left. Everything that came from her right side came whole, with her shoulders and her hips behind it, and arrived when her body said that it would. Everything from the left came a little after. He could see the order given: her shoulder turned, and her weight went over. Then there was a wait, no longer than a blink, while a thin white arm that had hung in grey wool for a hundred and seventy days gathered itself to push the lower end of that stave round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:43:"You won it with one arm." He had not meant to say it so plainly, and having begun he could not stop half way. "You've fought with one for a hundred and seventy days, and you're better at it than I am with two. I can't touch you like that. But it isn't what you came out here for. You said so, on that floor, with a reed in your hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:139:The last of the shelf went on the two hundred and seventy-fifth day, in one piece as long as the landing, and after it the river began to rise in earnest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:149:By the two hundred and seventy-sixth the water was over the landing's lowest plank. It came down the colour of a ploughed field, with a noise that could be heard in the inn's kitchen, and it brought whole trees with it, and once a drowned sheep, and once the better part of somebody's footbridge. The cable hung over it in a long belly. The ferryman sat on his keg from first light with his arms folded, and for an hour he did nothing but look. Half the meadow stood on the bank and looked at him, for there were nineteen fires along that grass now, and thirty carts with their shafts down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:171:Twelve days before that boat, when the count at Lowmarch stood at two hundred and sixty-five, Vastin had come down to the last ford on the east road with a stick in his hand. He had never heard of the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:77:On the hundred and sixty-first day Cael was on the rope-house roof with a mouth full of nails. It was the job that Brom had put his hand up for, at five, and they had been at it on and off since the fence, whenever the frost let a shingle be split. Seln knelt at the ridge with a hammer. Brom stood on the ladder's fourth rung and passed shingles up left-handed, a dozen at a time, in a rope sling. Below them, at the shed's door, Lira had the brown mule by the cheek-strap and was telling her what she thought of a beast that ate a ferryman's straw and did nothing for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:239:*Hundred and sixty-first day. A cart through the river at the bend, eighty yards out. The carter, his two mules and the cart brought off on the ferryman's line, wound in on his drum by Oryn's mule, with Lira leading her. Brom fetched the man out lying down, left-handed. Seln had the beasts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:281:"Then it will wait for your eyes." She shut the book, and ran the cord through her fingers to the first knot. "On the hundred and sixty-eighth I shall want all five of you in the inn yard at noon, with dry hands. I have had this cord for forty-six days and I have not yet seen anybody measure with it. It is time that we learned how he did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:3:Karis drove her hazel peg into the inn yard at noon on the hundred and sixty-eighth day, with a dead man's mallet, and it took her three blows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:131:The two edges were sound as well. She had laid each to its own pricks six times since the hundred and sixty-eighth, at different hours, with her hands warm and with them cold, and they had not once shown her a hair. They agreed with one another, and with the table. There was nothing whatever the matter with any of the three things, and it was their soundness that she could not put down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:*Hundred and sixty-eighth: one cord, two edges. Four hands agree. One does not, by a piece and by a hair. Cord re-measured on the hundred and seventieth: sound. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:199:He laid the lane on the hundred and seventy-sixth night, in the meadow below the ash, when the three fires were covered. Storm was the seventh of the ten, a Gold's worth of weather that had come to him out of a bout with a Gold, and it was the heaviest thing that he carried. It wanted the air. To give it the air he had to take the ground from the read, since at that weight he could have one or the other. It was like shutting an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:217:One of the ten had sent its bill to somebody else. On the hundred and eighty-first day he took Lira's long knife up to the smith, with a copper of his own, and brought it back with an edge on it, and laid it beside her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:267:Karis said the other thing on the two hundred and sixty-third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:65:"It's why I asked you. A hundred and sixty-three days ago six people stood on a rim, over a place called the Stair, with crossbows. They'd cloths across their faces. No seals, no word, nothing you could have written down. One of them put a bolt in him. They knew which hole we were in before we'd climbed out of it." She leaned across the board. "You say nothing of yours crosses the Line. Something did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:3:They came down to Thornwater's ford in the forenoon of the two hundred and sixty-fifth day, and Cael did not at first know the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:81:She had Lira on the bench on the two hundred and sixty-sixth morning, as soon as there was light to see by, with the frost still white on the flags.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:287:He had begun a cold term in that doorway, with a mule and a frozen trough. The mule was there still, a hundred and thirty-nine days on, with her fee paid. The trough was open water. He went out to the end of the planks before he sat down, and gave the read the ground, as he had on the first morning; and he could feel the shelf going. It was grey, and soft, and full of water, and it took his weight on one side and let it drain away on the other. Out in the channel the river ran black from bank to bank between two rims of rotten ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:3:The scow went into the river stern first, on the two hundred and seventy-first morning, down a slip of greased planks, with the ferryman at one corner of her and Brom at the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:21:"You said a week on the two hundred and sixty-eighth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:"Out of yours. I've my own." He pointed with his chin at a cake of ice the size of a barn door, which was coming down the middle of the channel with a whole young alder frozen into it by the roots. "She hangs on that cable by two blocks. One of those takes a block off me, and I've a boat going down to the mill sideways with whoever's in her. I'll not hang her till the river's done sending them. That's four days of ice yet off the top holds, and after the ice there's the water that was under it." He counted on his thumb. "The two hundred and seventy-seventh, at first light, if she drops in the night. Not before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:137:Pike had eight slips on his oak by the two hundred and seventy-second morning, and a healer at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:277:"I got it standing on a plate with half a hall looking. It was the best morning that I'd had. And he says that a civil man at a gate keeps it and gives me a slip." She beat a course down harder than it needed. "I'm not asking what you'll rule. I'm saying that I sat there and tried to mind, and I couldn't tell whether I minded. I've not stood on a floor for two hundred and seventy days. I don't know any longer what I'd be holding it for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:3:Seln cut the forty-seventh notch on the landing as the light came. It was day two hundred and seventy-seven, and half the meadow stood behind him to see a boat go out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:265:*Two hundred and seventy-seventh day. The read: the ground, at the landing, from the post's foot to the old sill. In earnest. Deployment since the Line: thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:The carters were gone before the two hundred and eighty-second day had any light in it, and they had left their fire raked out and a stack of thorn beside the ring for whoever came next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:53:It lay across the roadway under the arch, a single long stone, worn hollow in the middle, with a socket cut in each end where a bar had once dropped. He knew what it was before Seln's hand moved. He had stepped over its fellow going the other way, on another road, two hundred and eighty-two days ago, with four others; and he had begun a count that morning which he had kept ever since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:87:It ran straight from the gate toward the bluff's foot, between tall houses of the pale stone, and it was full from wall to wall of things being moved. There were carts, and handcarts, and people bent under frames, and barrels rolled along by boys. There was a great noise of wheels on cobbles. After two hundred and eighty days of roads on which nobody spoke, it came against Cael's ears like a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"A hundred and thirty-six," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:39:Nobody said anything to that. A board at a ferry had been named on a paper in the Compact's house for a hundred and seventy-five days, and Cael had known it for nearly as long; he had not until tonight seen the paper lying under a lamp, with a pale woman's glove beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:173:*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, one row looked through and no sheet counted; one broker's report; one page, copied by him from a register; one technician's sheet, copied by me this night and laid back in the second row. Read in part. Understood in no part. — K.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:265:It was the third time. She shut it, as she had shut it at a board on the two hundred and sixty-eighth, and at the same board three days after; and the sentence, whatever it was, went back down with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:299:*Two hundred and seventy-seventh day, night. The first boat brought no paper for us. It brought one man, who was the Archmarshal and is not. He held the ferry on her cable with his back. He has handed over three things: six years of an office's surveys, which it told nobody; one line, copied from a register, that sends its reader to a town; and a sheet that somebody stopped with a brush. Karis has all three. She has looked through one row and copied one sheet, and she says that she understands none of it yet. The town is the one that she named. The price is what it was. I said that I would rule when the boat was in. Tomorrow, at the plank, with hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:3:They went out to the fence at first light on the hundred and thirty-eighth day, by the cold road, with the mule carrying meal and two of the keeper's shovels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:21:It was the two hundred and seventy-eighth morning past the Line, and cold, with a white mist on the river that hid the cable's middle. Under the long eave, on twelve feet of tarred oak, Pike had his book open and his stone on the leaf. He was driving a tack with the butt of his penknife into the last clear space at the plank's right hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:"I found that on two of our places, in the field, and not on the others," said Karis. "I have turned it over for a hundred and sixty-five days. I could make nothing of two with it and two without. And here it is on all four." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The Fallow has it. The hall has it. A man with a punch did not trouble to strike it on their iron, I suppose, or struck it on a bar that we never dug for. The office has never once left it out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:"A hundred and thirty-eight in hand," said Brom. He put the slip inside his shirt with the purse. "That's his, to draw the tack on. I'll have it after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:207:She sat with the flat across her knees and her two gloved hands laid on it. Cael had seen her so on the hundred and thirty-third night, with a strip of brass and a calfskin, going past the edge of what she had. She did not look tired. She looked like a person who has added a long column and is afraid to read the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:"There's a thing that none of you has asked me in two hundred and eighty days, and I've been glad of it. I'll say it once, since it bears on what I want of him." She did not look at Cael. "I'd my rank off a board at the foot of a bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:197:The boxes came off the mule on the two hundred and eighty-first day, as soon as there was light to rope by, and the frost was white on the fork-stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time, as he would have gone over a count. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on four sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:283:The ruts went over a lip of bare rock and began to go down; and the whole country opened in front of him, as it had opened once from a shelf, on the second day past the Line, when it was the east that he was looking at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:285:This was the west. The moor fell away in long brown folds to a plain, and the plain was green. There were fields on it, with hedges, and he had not seen a hedge for two hundred and eighty days. A river came down out of the north and went across that green in loops, shining, and in the nearest of the loops the land stood up in a bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:9:Nobody did. They had come two hundred and eighty-one days through a country where a road was a place for hands and not for mouths, and the habit had gone deeper into them than any of the six had known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:99:He had done both. It was a hundred and sixty-one days since he had laid two fingers on his own wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:15:It was the two hundred and seventy-ninth day. They were on the road in the grey before the sun, which is dusk turned round, with a ground mist lying in the hollows of the moor as high as a man's knee. The country had risen all the day before. It was heather now, and bent, and outcrops of a dark stone that broke into plates, and the ruts climbed through it toward a long shoulder of hill that stood against the west like the back of a beast lying down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:3:The dray came into the yard behind them, on the two hundred and eighty-sixth day, by the carts' gate at the bottom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:The day, when it came, was the two hundred and eighty-third. She had the second box unroped before there was any sun, and its lid off, and she knelt on the boards of the loft with fifty-two sheets in three heaps before her: eleven, and twenty-nine, and twelve. They were her own first copies, the ones that she had drawn on her knees under a ring of stones, and on a stair, and in a drowned hall, with a lantern held low. Cael had carried them a long way. He had not seen them out of their wrapper since a cold term.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:*Two hundred and eighty-third day. Treswick, the district under the first wall. A hundred and thirty-six. Saw the man, from a wood-yard, at five strides. He looked at one sheet for as long as it takes to swallow, and he did not read it as anything of mine has ever been read: he asked it nothing, and he started at the bottom. He wouldn't hear what she thinks they are, and he wouldn't have a name, and he never looked at the yard. I'd wanted that, and I find I'd wanted the other as well. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:He had wanted that answer for four days, since a board at Lowmarch, where he had kept the question behind his teeth and let a small pale woman ask hers. He saw now that he had wanted it for a great deal longer. He had turned it over at an academy, and on a bluff, and across two hundred and eighty days of country; and he had tried mercy in the place where the reason should go, and then policy, and then some long game of the seat's, and every one of them had been a thing done to him or for him. He had been given the true one in a borrowed room, and had never quite believed that it was all. It had not once occurred to him that what lay folded inside it might be a man's relief on his own account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:259:"A week from today is the two hundred and ninety-third," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-27.md:255:"The loft's paid to the two hundred and eighty-eighth. A week from today wants two marks more, and I've got them. It's seven days of the hill, both ways." He looked at a bound leg. "And of that stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:87:There was a white frost in the inn yard at dawn on the two hundred and eighty-fourth day, and the three boxes stood in it on their ends.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:3:The second card took them through two gates, on the two hundred and eighty-fifth day, a good deal more easily than the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:"Three boxes, corded. One surveyor's case, tarred. One courier's flat. One hide, rolled. Brought down for study on the two hundred and eighty-fourth." He read it from his own line, with his finger under it. Then he looked up, at the small pale woman on the other side of his ledge; and it was the first thing that he had ever asked any of them. "The same?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:They had come two hundred and eighty-five days to that hatch, and got through it once on a figure. It was going to be shut on them by an honest man, who would not write six for three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:287:*Two hundred and eighty-fifth day. Treswick; the loft. A hundred and thirty-six. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Lamp, from about the third hour. He says left and I go left.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-fourth day, and the yard was grey, with the ashes of a fire in an iron basket and eight bowls stacked on a board beside the pump. He had got himself out of the stall on the stick and one shoulder. The tarred case lay across his thighs already. It had lain in the straw at his elbow on every night since he came down a ladder on a rope; nobody had given it into his keeping, and nobody had asked for it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:*Two hundred and ninety-fourth day. Treswick; the loft, and one in the stall; the last night of both. A hundred and thirty. The back of the leaf: a list, nine lines, a thing and its number to each. Two copies, hers and the reader's, and his goes with her. The fourth line is the mark's; of its last six signs, three are sure. Fifty-two sheets counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote* withdrawn*. Vastin: fifteen of thirty, and the stair alone. Karis wrote with the sewn hand. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:15:"You told me to write it down and stop touching it. On the two hundred and eighty-first day I read myself twice, with yours: once in a field by a brook, and once at a milestone, in the dusk." He kept it level. "I went round the place, and not into it, and I told myself that round wasn't touching. I knew what you'd meant when you said it. I read your words for the leave that I wanted. I'd do it again, and I'm not asking you to say that it was right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day, night. Millrace; the cart-shed. A hundred and twenty-seven. Told Oryn, before she read me: the two readings at the brook and the post, and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. Then she read me, asked first, for forty minutes, as deep as she goes. Her finding is hers. I have it word for word, and I am not copying it here, since she can say it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:301:They forded with the first of the light, on the two hundred and ninety-ninth day, and the woman on the flat stone counted them across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:51:The book came down from the eleventh shelf of the eastern gallery, on the two hundred and eighty-eighth day, in a girl's right hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:Brom paid at the half-door in the morning, on the two hundred and eighty-ninth day, and the woman swept two marks off her ledge with the side of her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:263:"A hundred and thirty-four," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:283:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:375:*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:9:It was the two hundred and ninety-fifth morning past the Line. They had lain thirteen nights in that loft, and Cael had not once gone in at the painted door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-second morning past the Line, and Cael saw it from the kerb of the carts' street, where the hill dropped away and all the roofs of the district lay below. It stood out on the brown water as it had stood the day before, small and dark, on five piers. He had looked at it then as a person looks at a road that he came in by. Now it was the oldest thing that he could see from that hill which anybody had built. He had that on the word of a small pale woman, who had laid her hand where the masons' pale stone was cut to fit it. And the new thing behind his eyes came round to it, across half a mile of air, like a head lifted from a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:217:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:227:*Held, with no ruling: a cord, two edges, a hair. A row that counted a sheet short. I have not laid a hand to an instrument since the hundred and sixty-eighth day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:263:He did not look again at the line, either; he turned upon the boards, so that the third box was between his shoulder and it, and drew the lantern nearer. He wrote the fourth entry on that page in the form that he had used once before, on the hundred and sixty-eighth day, for a thing that he could neither prove nor put down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:287:On the evening of that same day, the two hundred and ninety-second, and four gates north of a fork-stone, Oryn was asked to stand by for a child in her fourteenth year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:5:He did it on a box, by a bay of bundles, in the afternoon of the two hundred and ninety-third day. Nobody wanted him. The lamp had come back to the long table, and the boy knelt beside it, a hand to the left, as he had knelt on every other afternoon. The small pale woman was making a second copy. The man in the dusty coat sat in his chair with his eyes shut. It was a room with one office in it, and Vastin had not once that day felt his boot begin to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:277:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:"I said that I'd leave that until I'd a column for it," said Pike. "I've not found the column. I've had a hundred and sixty-eight days to look." He dipped. "A blank's a kind of entry, so long as a man knows why it's there. But a blank with nothing against it can't be told from one that was forgotten. So I'll put a day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:229:Cael saw it from the bench under the great ash as soon as there was light to see by, on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, when the meadow was grey and forty fires were being blown up out of their ashes. It was a salt cart, with a pair of mules, and it had been drawn onto the grass at dusk by a carter who did not know how soft the thaw had left that corner. It stood now with its near wheels sunk to the axle. Half its sacks lay on the turf beside it. The carter had the mules out and was standing with his cap in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:He had lain a day under that square with five people and a bolt. He had come up out of it at dusk without a sound, and walked two hundred yards of ditch with his hands dying on him, and had stood up beside a bar of iron where six hoods could see him. A hundred and eighty-five days had gone over the place since, and a cold term's snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:61:It was tarred hemp, no thicker than a pencil, with a brass eye whipped into one end and a knot at every fifth stride. A dead man had carried it down forty steps under that floor and run it half way to a wall. Karis had walked behind it for a whole cold term, in an inn yard, and Cael had not laid a finger on it for a hundred and thirty-one days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:187:*Two hundred and ninety-ninth day. A beck under the ridge's foot, a mile short of the ring. A hundred and twenty-seven. Vastin: twenty of thirty, and walking. The Stair's rim at noon. Karis laid the kit's cord on the dead man's figure, from the old iron to the floor: nine lengths and a piece. It is what it was. I did not touch it. A hundred and thirty-one days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:239:"I shut that shoulder on the two hundred and ninety-eighth. A mend that deep wants a week of somebody's eye, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends herself on a thing and walks off while it's deciding. That's the three hundred and fifth." Her hand had come up, and a thumb went down. "There's a knee behind it that's on my linen and twenty-one days old. And there's an edge at the end of that brass that's twice any I've stood at, with ten of you going at it. I said in a loft where somebody ought to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:He saw them at the third mile on the two hundred and ninety-sixth morning: a scatter of grey backs on the swell ahead, with their heads down in the bents and the east wind laying their wool over. There had been none on that ground sixteen days ago. A flock is not turned out where eight things lie up round a hole in the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:105:It was a stride farther down, level with the middle of the road, where the water ran now. Brom had gone by his own flat stone without a look, since he was not a person who visited places. But Cael had stood in that road on the two hundred and seventy-ninth day, behind the middle of an invisible thing, with his face two feet from it. He could not have lifted a hand there, while eight broke on what was in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:He did it without a word, on the stone that he had stood beside on the two hundred and seventy-eighth night with his bread in his hand. He let himself down by the stick and one palm, with the bound leg out before him across the ring; and when he was down he looked round the six faces, in the order that he kept, as though he expected the thing to be remarked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:203:It was noon of the two hundred and ninety-seventh day when the road came down through the alders, and the river lay beyond them, wide and brown, with the cable swung across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:The man in the dusty coat had ruled it from his chair, on the two hundred and ninety-third morning, before any of them had a load off. It was the seventh day of his week; there were twelve of the old town's tally-books lying open along a table's edge, and he would not have a tape touched while one of them was out of its bay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:61:"I've been wanting to do that for a hundred and eighty days," he said, to the two halves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:61:The ridge broke off there, as it had on the hundred and twenty-third evening, and under it the valley began. Cael had carried that place in his head for a hundred and eighty days. He found that he had it right, and that it was larger. It was a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills as a bowl is scooped, and open toward the south; and the light went level across it and laid the shadow of every tussock a yard long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:195:He had not been able to say so by one lamp, a hundred and eighty days ago. He could say it now with his eyes shut. The most of the bars were scaled as the old stake at the Stair was scaled, in leaves, like bark. He would have put five cold terms upon those, and perhaps ten. Here and there among them stood a bar with only a skin of rust on it, set since. Where one had rotted at the turf, somebody had drawn it, and driven a new one into the same hole. And every thirty or forty paces he came to one of another kind. It was thicker than its neighbours by a finger, and its head had been beaten by a heavier mallet. The scale on it had scaled again, and come away, and scaled, until the thing was as rough as a root.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:141:She did the sum standing, and he watched the pencil. A length was twenty strides, and she set down three thousand, one hundred and seventy-nine. Then she divided, as a joiner divides who wants the width of a wheel from its rim: by three, and a seventh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Cael stopped in the door. It was a little past sunrise on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, and the one small window laid a square of thin light across the whole of it. The courier's flat stood open at the table's head, with its six rows out and a pebble on each. The calfskin lay unrolled beneath them, under the Ternhall brass, and a dead man's book was open at its first page. Three of the Stair's sheets had been taken from their box and set in a row; and in the middle of everything, side by side, lay a leaf of her notebook and a square of stiff card with a turn of brown tape beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:41:"That is a thing which was made to be set in the ground," said Karis. "It has a mark on it, which I turned over for a hundred and eighty-three days, and called two copies of one ignorance. I had the word for it all the time. I had read it aloud, once, in another line, and it had not occurred to me that a hole in a moor and a child of fourteen could be given a thing out of the same drawer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Thank you. That is exactly as much as I wanted." Her finger had gone to the hide, and it touched each ring in turn, going east. "The Fallow's newest sheet stands in its sixty-first year, and the Stair's in its ninety-fourth; the hall's is a hundred and thirty-eight. And the place behind the iron—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:He took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from between its leaves into his palm, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for six days, under a ruling of his own that was a hundred and thirty days old: he did not put his hand to a thing that measured, since a cord had run short in it and two edges had disagreed. An old man in Denvash had sent them to step off a line with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:Cael looked along the skin, from the left hand to the right. There were four rings, and a point that was not yet drawn, and a number against each: sixty-one, and ninety-four, and a hundred and thirty-eight, and two hundred and six. They rose as they went. Beyond the last of them, in a strip of shadow, stood something that the office had stopped counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:223:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:105:"You have not touched it in a hundred and thirty-eight days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:143:She had a hair in the back of her notebook, which she had kept there since the hundred and sixty-eighth day, and it had been the whole of the difference then. She laid it in the first gap, and looked; she pulled a second out of her own head, and laid that beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:151:"It is more than it was. I shall write that it is more." Her pencil was moving. "I shall not write that it grows. I have the hundred and sixty-eighth day and I have this one. Two readings do not make a line, and I told a room so once."

