editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:85:"I shall say the short thing first, since everything after it leans upon it. I hold no office." He did not raise his voice, and it had begun to lose its hoarseness. "I left the seal of mine in a tray, some months ago, and came down a stair. I gave no notice of it, and I was given no leave; whether that is a resignation or a man walking out of his place, the office will have settled by now, and I did not stay to be told which. I have no rank that will open a door for you, and no seal that will shut one. Nobody sent me, and nobody has sanctioned a word of what I am going to say; you had better know that before you believe any of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:115:"You can forge a paper. I've seen it done; you showed us forty. You can stage a quarrel with a house, and limp for a week. I never heard of anybody who could forge a walk." He sat back. "That's a month on that leg, at what it'll do in a day, with nobody watching the most of it. A desk doesn't pay a man enough to do that. He'd have had to want to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:5:The river had dropped a hand in the night, and it was still no river that Cael had ever seen at that place. It ran from his right hand to his left, out of the north, brown and loud, a good deal wider than the hundred yards that it kept in the dry months. The cable went over it from a post above the landing to its fellow on the western bank; and the weight of the flood had dragged the cable's middle so far down the stream that it hung in a long bow, with its belly well to the south of both posts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:183:"Not that. Not for some months. Vastin will do; it did for my father."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:191:He was not an inspecting officer. He had left the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it. His clerk had come for that tray at the usual hour. Whether what he had done was a resignation, or only a man going down a stair, was a question for an office to settle, and he had not waited to hear it settled.
