editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:253:"No. I know that." She shut the book over her thumb. "I never asked for it. I want that in there as well. I could have, and I'd have got it, and you'd have hated me for a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:257:"A month. I know you." She gave the book back. "Is he going to get it? This man in a hole?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:157:It was the smallest calf that undid it. Its plates were paler than the rest, and it had kept to the inside of the line all day; but there was a trough on the fence line below, a hollowed log where the factor salted his cattle in the dry months, and a calf is a calf. It left the line. It came down the slope toward the salt at a wandering trot, with its beak out, and it set a forefoot on the thirty-eighth stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:1:# Chapter 16 — A Month
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:49:When she had done, she was grey about the mouth. She had spent on four small holes what a child at some northern gate might want of her within the week, and she had weighed that before ever she knelt, as she weighed every spending; it was why the joint was to have linen and a month, and nothing more. She took linen from the mule's pannier without getting up, and two laths, and bound the knee from above the joint to below it, turn upon turn, until it would bend no more than a post. Then she reached up, and he gave her his wrist, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:55:"A month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:67:"I didn't ask you to undertake it. I told you what it costs. Stand a wall on that leg inside the month, and you'll keep the stick for the rest of your days." She had turned to Brom already. "Boot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:"He took every word of that," she said. "About the month. He heard it, and he told me that he couldn't undertake it. And he's not knelt since, or gone faster than a walk, or leaned on anything but that stick." Her spoon went round the bowl. "I've had patients who said *yes* to everything and did none of it. I never had one before who said *I cannot undertake*, and then did it to the letter, as if it were a thing that he'd thought of himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:245:"I said a month on a stick. I never said that the stick had to go uphill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:103:"So it is. My master keeps eleven on his doors, and there's not one of them will shift a keg until he's lit up to do it, in front of whoever's passing; and then it's a line in somebody's book, every time." The clean hands turned the tally over. "The house is the third on the brokers' street, with the slate above its door. There is a guard's post at the store we keep inside the second wall. It's a standing pass, sealed, by the month. It's a bed in the house, above the wall, and the house's coat. Four marks, and found."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:It was a real offer, and Cael knew what it weighed. There was a house in Velmere that had wanted exactly this for its son, for as long as it had had him: a coat with somebody's colour on it, and a place inside a wall, and a figure at the month's end that could be written in a letter. A panel had sat once to tell that house what he was worth. Here a stranger had told him in a yard, after watching his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:245:"Thirty's the month," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:9:It was Seln's, far ahead, and Teague had been looking at the same thing for some time. There was the fence of split posts, and the long house, and smoke. There was the gate. It was the middle of a fine forenoon, a month and more after the thaw, when a hold has its beasts out and its people in the fields; and that gate was shut, with a bar across it, and not a head showed on the walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:169:"I said after the thaw. It's a month gone. I said whatever's on that bearing, and I've just heard him write the word." His eyes went along them, and they did not stop at five; they went on to the door-post, where a stick was grounded. "I'll say it here, where it's heard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:205:"There's a month on it," said Cael. "It's a healer's, and it's got twelve days to run. He stood one in the middle of it, against her word, for us, and it very nearly cost him the leg. Nobody stands it up before the month's out. Not for your field, and not for mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:207:"I'd not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count." Teague looked once more toward the door-post, and it was the look of a man reading a load that he does not mean to lift yet. "I asked what I needed, of a factor. I know where it stands, and that it stays. That'll do me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:67:He looked at that for some time. It was small, at such a distance, and pale, and it did not flicker. Every station that he had ever served under had burned so; a man who works in a registry leaves off seeing it within a month, as he leaves off hearing the clock. He had not been inside a wall with one for longer than he cared to reckon. He found that he saw it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:73:It weighed something under three pounds. He had known that weight for twelve years, to the ounce, as a man knows the weight of his own arm; which is to say that he had not felt it since the first month. It had gone through three postings on him, and across a Line. It had stood for some months between a researcher's grey boxes, on a shelf, with its key in his waistcoat, and had gone back onto the strap on the night that five people left a hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:257:"I asked you for one, the first day, and I've spent the half of it on my own page; I've read that every morning before I'd so much as look at yours. That's put by. A week. Mine. From today, and nothing else on the desk. And at the end of it the whole lot goes up that stair, every sheet, and the fellow with the green book writes that it's gone." He rubbed his eyes. "He entered three boxes three days back, and he's written *the same* under them ever since. If they lie on my tables a month, he'll begin to wonder what *the same* is. I've been asked for things before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:75:"She'll take hers away, and it'll be right," he said, to the skin. "And in a month she'll want to know whether a stroke was the writer's or the knife's, and she'll be three hundred miles off. So there's to be one that's drawn and not read. That's mine. It goes with her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:251:"Twenty-nine," she said. "She told me a month of slow. I've counted every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:Nobody answered him. Cael had heard those words given, on a frozen road, by a square woman with her sleeves pushed up: that a man who stood a wall on that leg inside the month would keep the stick for the rest of his days. It was the eleventh day of thirty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:75:He would have to go over the eleven, one at a time, when he had a table and a month. It was the kind of work that he was good at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:399:She did not spend on it. She took the slat off, and bound the joint again in her own linen with two new laths; and what she said, when she had counted his wrist, was that the month stood as it had been counted, and that he was not to mistake that for a kindness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:99:He did it by turning onto his right side first, and then onto his knees, as he had seen an old man do it for a month; and by the time that he was on his feet, and round the rock again, the fire was a red eye. Beyond it there was a lamp burning on the lid of the middle box, with a small pale woman bent over it, and Brom sitting by with his bound arm across his lap, since he had not yet found the way to lie on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:77:The timber of the frames was grey right through the checks. He had put his thumbnail into one. It had stood in that weather for two cold terms, and he would have laid a month's pay upon three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:169:"It's the same face." She had slipped her left arm out of its becket while she spoke. The box hung from the right shoulder alone, and she let the left hand take the other becket low, at her hip, and carry there. "Thirty. That's her month. I've been slow for the whole of it, as I was told to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:107:Cael saw him not do it, since the grey eyes stayed upon a patch of charcoal with a great deal of care. That man had said at a bench, before fifty people, that he would not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count; and it seemed that a captain who will not ask must be careful also where he rests his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:125:"I believe that I am its next sentence, and that nobody on this rock is willing to say me. I am obliged to all of you for it." He drew a breath through his nose. "Three days ago, at noon, I had it entered that I would not stand a wall before my month was out. It was entered at my own asking, and it is in his book. I was told eleven days more, at a mill, by a person who was entitled to tell me. This morning I have twenty-seven of thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:175:"And hear the rest, since you asked me fairly," said Oryn. "It'll take everything in you that isn't the knee. I had my hands on you at a mill for the better part of an hour, and I know how much there is. You're not young, and you've walked the country on a stick. When it goes up, you'll drain like a keg with the bung out; and when it comes down, you'll not get up. I can't give that back. It's not a hole in you. It's a spending." She let go of the leg. "The month stands as I counted it. Twenty-seven."

