editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:131:Brom had brought the pot up, late, with seven on the board, and had set the seventh at the end by itself. The old man had been got up against the gable's foot, with a rolled coat behind him; and when he put out his right hand for the thing, the fingers would not shut upon its rim. The left, which had held a ferry's strap and a mule's head, lay open on the straw and did not stir.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:149:It had been waiting for him since the stair. He had felt it there through all that came after, through a lane and two gates and seven stitches, standing in the place behind his eyes that belonged to nothing else, as each of the ten had stood, as patient as a clerk. He had not looked. He had had an old man's arm across his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:275:Cael thought about it. He had six people in a loft, of whom one could not stand, and another had seven stitches in her hand; and there was not a second house under that wall which would take them without a name. There were three days of a week, and fifty-two sheets on three tables, and a small pale woman who would go down to them tomorrow if she had to be carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:283:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:61:Lira went down it with her face turned up to the scree. She had lain on those plates with a knife, on a day when the left arm could do no more than hold. He saw her mark the place, and shut that hand once at her side, and look away. Karis did not look at the banks at all. She was watching the water go under the dray's wheels, with her lips moving, like a person who has written six findings about a thing and suspects that there is a seventh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:89:The old man looked from the big man to the stones. He had said one word in that cut, and had been given one. Seventeen days ago, on the same ground, the loudest thing on the whole moor had been his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:105:It was a stride farther down, level with the middle of the road, where the water ran now. Brom had gone by his own flat stone without a look, since he was not a person who visited places. But Cael had stood in that road on the two hundred and seventy-ninth day, behind the middle of an invisible thing, with his face two feet from it. He could not have lifted a hand there, while eight broke on what was in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:He did it without a word, on the stone that he had stood beside on the two hundred and seventy-eighth night with his bread in his hand. He let himself down by the stick and one palm, with the bound leg out before him across the ring; and when he was down he looked round the six faces, in the order that he kept, as though he expected the thing to be remarked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:159:Brom had been ladling cold barley into the seventh bowl. He stopped with the ladle lifted, and a slow look went over his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:203:It was noon of the two hundred and ninety-seventh day when the road came down through the alders, and the river lay beyond them, wide and brown, with the cable swung across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:23:Then there was the iron, and the twenty-seven, with an old man going down them on his seat and a boot's heel in Cael's palm at every tread. At the stair's foot the lamp's ring was hung on his fingers, without a word, by somebody who did not look at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:183:"Twenty-seven," he said. "Unassisted. I mention it for the tally."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:209:They had come up the only stair in sight, all twenty-seven steps of it, and it lay behind them in the dark. Cael watched her take that in, and then watched her take in the rest. There had been a row of signs on a sheet, with each one hung from the one before; and a man had said that he did not read such a thing, but was walked down it. She had fifty-two sheets of that in three boxes, and she was about to walk them east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:219:Cael watched him find it. The pen had set down *the same* on that leaf day after day, and tonight the same was not so. The tables below were bare. Three boxes stood upon his paving which would not come back in the morning, and there was no card for the morning in anybody's hand. His eyes went along the stones at the height of a boot, and found seven that stood and one that hung. Then they went back to the form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:257:He had turned his cobs out on the green by the overflow. He took the seventh bowl from Brom as a thing that the road owed him, and he had it empty before the old man had begun; and then he lay down under his own tail-board with his cap over his eyes. Nobody lit anything. It was a furlong off the road, and a person might have spoken there. Nobody did that either, beyond what the bowls wanted. They had six miles of wind in their ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:He went, and sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book. The brass legs slid from it into his lap, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for three days and had not once opened them upon a distance. A ruling of his own stood in the way, and it was a hundred and twenty-seven days old. Whatever measured had read one way in four hands and another in his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:27:The clerk wrote *the same*, and *four*. The old man went down twenty-seven steps on his seat, with his heel in Cael's palm, and said at the foot that he believed he had improved upon his time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:35:He said it to the ring. His red eyes had come to the brass, and to the smear upon it, and they stayed there as though a line had been drawn at the edge of the metal; they did not go on by the width of a finger to the hand that held it. For seven days they had gone over that hand as over a hook. This was not the same. Cael watched a man take care, and he knew what the care was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:69:"Not tonight. I'll not have that tape cut by anybody who's listening for a bell." He took his fingers away. "In the morning. It's the seventh. Come down with your eyes rested, if you know how that's done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:139:"Four," said the woman, when he had told her what he wanted. She looked at him as she had looked on the first day, when he asked for a seventh bowl. "In front."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:145:They ate in the lower room, off the yard, since it was level with the cobbles and one of them could be brought there on a shoulder. It had a single long table, and a hatch, and a candle in a dish. What came through the hatch was a goose. There was a pie after it with a lid as broad as a shield, and a white loaf, and a dish of apples baked in something sweet; and there was a jug of cider, cold, from which Brom poured seven cups.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:149:"There's seven cups."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:283:"Twenty-seven days out of the wool," she said, of the arm that had done it. "It's good for something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:The man in the dusty coat had ruled it from his chair, on the two hundred and ninety-third morning, before any of them had a load off. It was the seventh day of his week; there were twelve of the old town's tally-books lying open along a table's edge, and he would not have a tape touched while one of them was out of its bay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:83:He set the two side by side, as he would have set two returns from one office. He did not go past what they said. At the fence they had been told to hold a place, and to see what came to it. Here somebody had been told to stop a crew. And between the one order and the other, seven people had walked east for a day with what they had seen across a row of bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:125:"A boy was killed by the sixth flight. I went over the lip as the seventh was loosed. There is one flight between the two, and I have asked myself where I lost it." He kept his eyes on the path. "Before the fourth there was no slab. There was a bank that would not have borne a cat. From the foot of the bare rock to the lip took me three flights, at the pace that a man goes who must not be seen. I have made it that each time. It does not come out as two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:185:Brom laid seven bowls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:187:He had laid seven the night before, under a step of shale, and a boy on the other side of the fire had laid five. Cael watched him set them down now, on bare rock, with one hand. The big fingers went along the row when it was done, counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:215:"Somebody was told so. I can put it no higher. I do not know who gave the word, or where; I know that it had changed between a fence and a gap, and that seven people walked from the one to the other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:121:"He did, and it was exactly right, and I did not see what he had told me." Her finger lay beside the two small marks. "I could not read these seven days ago, since I was looking at them as an office's signs. They are not an office's. Some clerk has copied them forward for a great many years, from sheet to sheet, as he would copy a blot, without the least notion of what they were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:149:He stood on the top step of twenty-seven and looked at that. He had read every sheet that had ever been written about him from the far side of somebody's desk. There had been a file in a district office, and a thicker one on a bluff; there had been a finding under two hands, and a paper with an hour in it. Each had lain with its head toward the person who owned it, and he had learned to take them so, as a matter of course, as a person learns to read a shop's sign from behind the glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:241:"She gave me a look," he said. "I told her that there's seven of us, with the one that's owed. She asked what the eighth was for." He set the board down on an upturned bucket. "I said that I'd laid one for a mule before now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:281:The man sat. He took the bowl in both hands, and held it without eating, with his eyes shut and the steam going up past his face. That made seven in a stable yard, and eight bowls, round an iron basket, with a pump behind them and a hill over their heads.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:29:"I set a rule on you for a healer's reason," she said. "A person who's found a sore place goes back to it every night with his fingers. You kept my words, and you went round what they were for, as a carter goes round a toll by way of the field; and that's worse than breaking it outright, since it's what clever people do." She chewed. "And then you carried it seventeen days, and came down a ridge to tell me so before I'd laid a hand on you. That's the other half of it, and I'll take the two together. It's entered. What's the second?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:117:It was the two hundred and sixth breath. He had been on that plank for seventeen minutes, and she had only just finished with what he carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:137:He had not bidden it, or so much as thought of it, since he had been counting, and the count was all of him that was awake. The twelfth had lain where she said, a little flat thing behind his eyes, folded; for seven days it had leaned at whatever a hand had made, at pegs and rivets and the piers of a bridge, and each time he had given his eyes some other work, and it had come up short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:255:"You'd not look at her lantern. You looked at the roof for the whole time that she was telling you, and you came across that yard like a man going by a forge. And I can hear the water going now." Her voice was quite low. "I had you by the sleeve across a vault, seven days ago, with your lids shut. I know what it is that bills them. It only charges when it's looked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day, night. Millrace; the cart-shed. A hundred and twenty-seven. Told Oryn, before she read me: the two readings at the brook and the post, and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. Then she read me, asked first, for forty minutes, as deep as she goes. Her finding is hers. I have it word for word, and I am not copying it here, since she can say it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:291:The fourth lay in him where it had lain for seven days. A man with red eyes had poured a cup of water, under a town, and had said what he was to do if the thing ever read something that ought not to be made, and read it all the same. He was not to tell that man, and he was not to tell anybody. He was only to remember that it had been said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:27:He lifted two fingers of his left hand, and turned them a little. The tall man went away southward along the live ruts without a word, with seven feet of ash on his shoulder. He walked a quarter of the round, and stopped, and grounded the butt in the turf; and Cael knew the spot. That man had stood on it from first light until the middle of a forenoon, with his face bare, while a girl with one arm found an edge sixty times with her boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:139:He set out seven bowls. He had laid six for a hundred nights, and taken one back; then seven, in a loft, with one that stood empty. Tonight there was a person for each, and he went along the row twice with his lips moving, like a man who distrusts a sum that has come out even.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:The healer ate out of the seventh, and she ate it sitting, on a stone, with the brown mule's rope under her boot. Cael did not look at that for long. She had her left hand spread on her knee, and between one spoonful and the next a thumb went down, and then a finger; her lips moved, and the hand opened again, and she began the count afresh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:187:*Two hundred and ninety-ninth day. A beck under the ridge's foot, a mile short of the ring. A hundred and twenty-seven. Vastin: twenty of thirty, and walking. The Stair's rim at noon. Karis laid the kit's cord on the dead man's figure, from the old iron to the floor: nine lengths and a piece. It is what it was. I did not touch it. A hundred and thirty-one days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:211:She stood at the cairn's foot with the rope over her shoulder, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, as she stood everywhere. Her face was toward the path. He had seen her look so at a notch with frost in it, on the hundred and twenty-second day, with a grey jenny's breath on his neck; and that time she had clicked her tongue, and gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:195:It held. Cael had not been sure that it would, outside a meadow. A quiet man went ahead, small against the pale grass, and after him seven feet of ash with a leaf of steel on it. Then came a pair of shoulders that did not look round; and Cael walked a stride behind the left of those, where he had been put. He had never in his life walked at anybody's shoulder to be asked what he saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:299:Nobody argued it. Brom had gone into the mill by then, and he came out with the miller's wife behind him and a sack on the bound shoulder; it was a mark's worth of meal and a flitch, for five days of country with no gate in it. He said the figure as he came: a hundred and twenty-seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:301:Then he set down what he had carried under his box's lashing for seventeen days, which was a wooden bowl, on the end of the bench; and he turned a keg up on its end before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:367:The strip of shirt came off, turn by turn. Under it the palm was laid open to the light: a long straight seam across the heel, drawn together with seven stitches of black waxed thread, each as thick as a bootlace's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:379:The stitches came out one at a time, and Karis counted them, under her breath, in a tongue that nobody else there had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:115:*Seven. Eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:193:The seventh flight was loosed at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:He had reversed his spear. Cael saw that before he saw the long quiet face. The leaf of steel was behind him, pointing at the stones of the stair, and it was the butt of the ash that he carried foremost: seven feet of pole, blunt end first, as he had carried it in a square of pegs under a tree, where a touch was only a touch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:99:*Three hundred and first day. A dead hold on the east road, in the lee of its gable. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-two. Oryn is a day past her cairn. A cup of water a head, and the kettle.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:167:They were not grazing; he could see that much. Seven or eight stood at the wet, with their beaks to the black rock, and the rest were strung away eastward along the foot of the band, a length and a half apart, with their heads down in the lichen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:273:"She's coming," said Lira, when the seventh was at the water. "Along the top of the band. Don't look up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:321:*Three hundred and second day. The ridge's back, in a hollow. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-three. Oryn is two days past her cairn; she went by the chock-stones and did not look up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:1:# Chapter 49 — Seven on a Posting
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:65:It ran away on either hand with the curve of the ground, a row of small dark uprights set a pace apart, like stitches along a hem. He let his eyes go along it, as he had once, and under his breastbone a thin held note began to ask at every one. He told it no. Something else in him had begun to ask as well, lower down, in the voice of a post that has been stood in its hole: *This far?* He told that no also, and looked at the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:93:Cael counted them. He had seen their like on a rim once, and in a stepped alley under a wall, and each time they had been six. There were seven. They stood in a row behind the upper baulk, and every one of them had a hood up and a cloth across the face, and every hood was turned toward the brow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:141:He had seen it last, lying on his face, and had not known what he was looking at until he asked himself what seven people on a posting would want; and then he had seen that a person who shuts himself onto a shelf wants a way off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:163:He had not planned it. He had been set at a shoulder to say what he saw, and the field was not his; he had said so in front of fifty people. But he had seen those hoods on a rim, with a light at the end of his own arm, and again in a cut, where he had only stood. He had seen what a captain's whole arm did to a ton of oak. And he had watched seven shapes stand at a baulk in the cold, with nowhere behind them but a gap a stride and a half wide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:167:"Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:175:Teague gave his orders after that, and they were few. They would go down at first light, when the sun would be behind them and in seven pairs of eyes. The quiet one was to go round the head of the dish that night, by the old ruts, and lie up beyond the far gap, where he could see what went out by it and what came in. The big one would walk in front, since a bolt had to be taken on something. The two healers and the beast were to stay at the ruts, a furlong up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:211:There was a sheet for this. That was the thought which stayed with him as he went, and he could not put it by. Somewhere west of the Line a clerk had a form with seven lines on it, and a column for what each of the seven was paid, and a space at the foot for whoever signed. There would be a second sheet for their meal, and a third for the carriage of water in kegs from a fold a day off. He had copied such sheets by the hundred. He had never once wondered what any of them was a posting *to*, since the form did not ask it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:213:The case lay against his breastbone under his coat, shut, where it had lain for a great many nights. He had looked at a certain house from a roof, seventeen days ago, with that weight on him, and had ruled nothing. He had said at a fire that he would keep it a while longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:227:They had not gone to their blankets. He had not supposed that they would; twelve people had stood on a brow at dusk and looked at them. All seven were on the shelf. He found them one at a time, as he would have gone down a column, by the places where the red was hidden and then shown.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:233:The seventh had no stave and no bow, and Seln watched him longer than the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:237:*He is the one with the Path,* Seln thought. *And he has been told what is on the other side of that iron. They all have. But he is the only one of the seven who has something that it would take.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:245:One of the three by the hut coughed. It was a wet cough, from low down, such as a person has who has slept a cold term through on stone. The one who was awake leaned over, without a word, and drew a blanket higher on the other's shoulder. The walker, coming in through the gap, stopped by the fire. Seln could not hear what he said. He heard that it was put as a question, and that the seventh answered it with a figure, and that the figure did not please anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:249:They were afraid. He had sat in a hole under a floor for a whole day while six of their kind waited on a rim for the cold to do its work. He had thought of them as a design. They were seven people who had been posted to the far end of the country to walk round a fence. Somebody had told them that if anyone came, it would be a crew that walked out of such places with lights in its hands. They had bent two bows and stood to their timbers. And there was not one among them, he would have laid a year's pay on it, who had ever been told what the fence was round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:259:*Three hundred and third day. A fold behind the brow, above the fourth place. No fire. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-four; he has said that he will not stand it, and it is entered. Oryn is three days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:263:*There is a post on a shelf between a ravine and the iron: a hut, a fire, kit under a hide, two timbers. Seven, hooded. Two bows, four staves, one with a Path who has shown a light. I drew it for him.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:299:They went over the brow in the grey, nine of them, in the order that a captain had given the night before, with a big man in front; and Cael, at a captain's left shoulder, a stride back, saw the whole dish open below him without a shadow in it. The fence was a row of stitches. In the middle a grey thing stood on two legs with the last of the night under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:301:On the shelf, a furlong down, the fire had been blown up, and seven shapes stood behind two timbers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:303:The sun came over the ridge at their backs as they reached the old ruts. He felt it on his neck. It went down the slope before them, and touched the iron, and lay full upon seven hoods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:7:Brom laid seven bowls. The boy, on his own crew's side, laid five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:187:On her left hand was the ruled leaf, which she had torn from her notebook in front of five people. It had her table on it: seven names down the side, and what each carried, and a stride. Beneath the table she had written two sentences about lamps, in ink, and the day, and her initial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:211:A measurement was a fact about a ruler. She had two rulers on that lid. The one had been laid upon every crossing that she had seen, by seven people, and it told her how a thing went out and came back. The other was a man's own account of himself, a long while dead, with his name scraped off it by a hand that she could not identify. Neither had been laid on the other. There was nothing on that lid, or in three boxes, or in a courier's flat, that would serve to lay them so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:247:"Seven people went east from a shelf yesterday, with a day's start of us, along a path that goes nowhere else. There is one door in those hills. I lay a mile from it until dark, and I could not find so much as a heel-mark in the bed." His hands were at his sides. "I should have liked to see something. A watch that I can count is a watch that I can plan for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:261:*Three hundred and fifth day. A shelf of shale under a step, four miles short of a gap in the hills. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-six. Oryn is five days past her cairn; Brom's week was out today, and she had her hands in a shin tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:29:"So the seven are in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:"The seven, and two more, or three. They had a day's start of us, and I think now that they did not walk it to an empty place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:75:"Back's seven days to a mill," said Brom, "and those people keep the door for good."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:125:"I believe that I am its next sentence, and that nobody on this rock is willing to say me. I am obliged to all of you for it." He drew a breath through his nose. "Three days ago, at noon, I had it entered that I would not stand a wall before my month was out. It was entered at my own asking, and it is in his book. I was told eleven days more, at a mill, by a person who was entitled to tell me. This morning I have twenty-seven of thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:175:"And hear the rest, since you asked me fairly," said Oryn. "It'll take everything in you that isn't the knee. I had my hands on you at a mill for the better part of an hour, and I know how much there is. You're not young, and you've walked the country on a stick. When it goes up, you'll drain like a keg with the bung out; and when it comes down, you'll not get up. I can't give that back. It's not a hole in you. It's a spending." She let go of the leg. "The month stands as I counted it. Twenty-seven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-54.md:303:The healer walked in the middle, with the mule's rope in her fist, as though she had never owned any other road. She was six days past the end of hers that morning. Cael had it in his book, counted from a cairn, and he did not suppose that she had left off counting it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:11:Each was as thick as a thigh. They were lashed one above the other to a pair of trestles, and the upper stood as high as a chest; and behind it, in a row, were seven hoods with the new sun full upon them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:15:Two had crossbows, with the stocks laid on the baulk and the noses down the shelf. Four stood between and beside those, each with a shod stave held upright, like men waiting at a gate. Every face had a cloth across it. They were squinting. He could see that much at forty yards, by the way the hoods were tipped: a captain had brought nine people down a hill with the morning behind them, and it was costing those seven something merely to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:17:The seventh stood a pace behind the rest, with nothing in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:65:The tall man was on the far side, a stride out, with seven feet of ash held across him in both hands and the steel leaf high. He had not looked at Cael once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:107:Five of the hoods were on their backs on the shale, with their staves under them and their heels in the air. One of the long stocks had been lying on the upper baulk, and the lower had come down across it; he heard the wood go, with a crack like a branch in frost. Nobody was beneath a timber. He looked for that before he looked for anything, and he counted seven shapes, and every one was moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:125:A man had asked the ground for a thing, a yard from where he stood, with the whole of himself, at seven people who meant him harm with the whole of themselves; and some part of that asking had come up out of the same stone through a second pair of boots. It went through him as a knock goes through a door. It came to his right hand, and found nothing there to be laid against, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:263:There was a thing set in him like a post, which he had had from an old man in a stair; and it had come up at that gap as a dog comes up at a gate. *This far?* It was a stride and a half from the iron to the drop. He had stood at the foot of such a thing once for eighty-one counts, with nothing to do but be the reason for it. He had it in him now. One word to it, and there would have been a plane across those few feet of shale from the ground to the sky, and seven people shut upon a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:293:*Six. Seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:301:He understood that only afterward. They had kept that fence, those seven, or others like them before them, with a path worn into the stone to show for it. They had been told one thing about what lay on the other side. And five people had stood in it now for the time that it takes to say a slip through twice, with the wind moving their coats; and one of them was a child, and one was an old man who leaned upon a stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:313:Seven went east along a path, and not one of them was followed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:5:Brom had one corner, and the tall man another. Teague's boy had the third, and would not give it up; and Cael, who had only the one arm that morning, was told by Lira to stand aside and took the fourth all the same until she put him off it with her shoulder. They laid the man in the hut that seven strangers had slept in, on a heap of somebody else's bracken. He had not made a sound that could be heard at five yards, from the bed of the ravine to the blanket in the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:11:"Seven. I counted them through the gap, and I counted them again over the second rise, a mile on. They did not stop to speak to one another. Nobody came up that path to meet them, and nobody has been upon it since the dew but those seven and myself." He glanced once at the timbers, where they lay askew. "They have left a bow, a stave and their fire. I should say that they do not mean to come back for any of it today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:107:He went over it once more, as he had gone over it a dozen times already. He had been ten strides from those timbers, with one bow in front of him and five people standing on the grass at the back of it. They had gone over that iron because he looked at it. One of them was his. If seven hoods had seen a captain turn his back, with his arm hanging, to run and look into a hole, they would have seen a crew with its head off; and they had four staves and a great deal less to lose than he had supposed. He had heard a man whom he had walked beside for a great many cold terms go off a ledge. He had put his foot down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:173:But he stood over a bundle of bright iron for some time, with a dead arm, on a shelf that seven people had been paid to keep; and he thought that whoever sent a dozen new bars to the far end of the country, with a mallet to drive them and seven people to do the driving, was not a person who had forgotten that the place was here.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:227:The middle of such a place was what he had wanted, ever since a small pale woman said three words on a road. He had said so on his feet, at a fire, to six people. This was the middle of the fourth: the centre of a ring half a mile across, which an office had held a sheet upon for two hundred and six years and had fenced with iron and left seven people to walk round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:341:*Three hundred and fourth day. The watch's post, on the shelf above the fourth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-five; he stood inside the line this morning, and it took what he carries, and gave it back. Oryn is four days past her cairn. She knit a shin in the ravine's bed and was grey for a quarter of an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:33:*Seventy-eight. Seventy-nine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:99:He thought of Lira, who had told a boy that a line was the last step of a stair in the dark; and then he thought of the boy, for whom he had a heap of stones, seven miles off, and no name that he would ever write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:He knew what the quiet was. He had watched it take five people, and seven, at a stride. It took what was lit. It had never had anything of his to take, and it had taken nothing now; every one of the thirteen was whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:103:At the seventy-ninth length they stood opposite the place where they had begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:141:She did the sum standing, and he watched the pencil. A length was twenty strides, and she set down three thousand, one hundred and seventy-nine. Then she divided, as a joiner divides who wants the width of a wheel from its rim: by three, and a seventh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:147:"About fifty. Something over eleven hundred yards; I make it eleven hundred and seven, as near as hemp will say." She looked at the slip of ash, with its eight nail-marks all in the one place. "And it is round. I have measured a millstone that was not so round. I do not know what was used to cut it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:221:*Three hundred and seventh day. The fifth place, at the end of the line. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight; he was the mark that the round closed on. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:241:Cael had known since the stream-bed that he would have to say it again. It had been easy behind a brow, when nobody had been hurt, and he had only been asking a man to spare seven strangers whom he had no cause to hate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:249:It was a long look, and there was a heap of stones in it, seven miles off, and a cloth, and a bowl with a chip in its rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:255:"It is the fifth place, and it has two bars to its stroke, and its first sheet is older than any that I have a figure for." Karis spoke as she read a label. "I can tell you what four such places have done to seven people. They took what each carried, at a stride, and gave it back at a stride, whole. I cannot tell you that this one does as the four did. I should want one person over, and back, before a second went."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:359:The tall man turned his spear about, so that its head lay toward the west; the broad man got his stick under him, and the old woman took up her board. They did not go anywhere; the light was nearly gone, and there were seven miles of rock between that place and a gap. But they had turned. Their packs went round to the western side of the fire, and they sat down with their backs to the floor, three people and a healer of no Path, who had come the length of a country on another crew's line and had left one of their own at a gap in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:It was six days since she had stood at a cairn, at noon, and looked up a mule-path that went north over a saddle; she had counted them. Three gates lay up that path. There was a woman at the middle one who would be near her time by now, and an old man at the last whose chest she listened to at every thaw. She had sent them word from a mill: a week, or more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:181:He had remembered. For seven days it had cost him nothing, and then it had turned over in him on a plank, beside a stopped wheel, and given him two words. He had not let it give him a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:245:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock below the eastern mouth of the gap. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-seven; he held a roof today, sitting down, by Oryn's leave, and the knee never took a pound of it. Oryn is six days past her cairn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:3:He wrote the ledger by the fire, on the night of the three hundred and seventh day, with his eyes still sore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:9:*Three hundred and seventh day, night. The rim of the fifth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-eight. Oryn is seven days past her cairn. Eleven of us tonight. At first light four go west, and one north.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:But the tall man had been looking at him. He stood behind his captain's right shoulder, with seven feet of ash in his fist; and when Cael's eyes came to him, he lifted the pole's butt a hand from the rock, and grounded it, once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:It was the eighth day since she had stood at a cairn. Cael did not say so, since she knew it better than his book did.

