editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:129:He walked into a shovel's haft for a quarter of an hour, in front of a bench and four pegs and whatever of the meadow had not gone back to its fires, and he was told *late* eleven times and *early* three before he had one that she did not name. She counted him as a man at a rail had once counted her, on a bluff, every morning for a month, and she was no kinder than that man had been; her right hand went on slapping the two beats long after his own legs had begun to shake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:159:The leather came off first, which had been on that wrist since before the boy could lift a pack. Under it the skin was as white as a root, and the joint was thicker than its fellow by the width of a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:195:Teague looked at him. He was all wrists, and he ate as a fire eats; he carried more than his share on every stage and had to be stopped. He went at each thing a half-beat before he had seen the whole of it, and that would have to be got out of him by somebody before it was got out of him by a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:215:It was the fourth night that he had seen it: two before he went up the cold road, and two since. He had not gone looking; the house on the rise had one window on that side, and a man who has kept a watch on roads for as long as he had does not sit with his back to a window. The light came on after dark and burned for an hour or two, and for the whole of that time a small still shape stood between two hurdles, facing outward. It was the quiet one. Teague had needed two looks to find him, once, in a room. He found him now in one, and took no credit for it, since the man was not trying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:219:He put that with the rest. There was a boy who had come out of a hole in the ground declaring more than one kind of thing. There were six pairs of frozen breeches on a rope by a marsh, and a strip of brass that ran off the edge of a calfskin. And this morning there had been a pair of boots in the snow that leaned before every step as if something had them by the laces, and were dragged back each time, and went the plain way and were hit for it. He could not say what the boy had been refusing. He knew a man refusing when he saw one, and he had seen it three times in as many touches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:243:He had not moved a hand's breadth, and he had not chosen to stand, either. The fragment charged nothing and offered nothing until something struck him; then it was simply there, before he could have asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:263:"You go round like a frog before you do it. I could have hit you twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:13:"I'd two, before the snow. A man sends the second across by a skiff if his first one strands. I've no second."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:57:The gelding was being walked backward round his own ring. Seln was at the drum before Cael had turned; there was an iron dog hinged to the shed's king-post, which dropped into the teeth on the drum's rim, and he kicked it down. The drum stopped. The gelding stood trembling in his collar, and the whole weight of a boat and nine people came onto one rope and one post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:63:Three of the ten spoke to him together, as they had on this river once before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:67:*There?* asked Anchor, of the post. It was a fair question. A point laid on that oak would have held it as a nail holds a board, for as long as he could hold the point. He had held one for sixty counts once, and a second time to thirty with his other hand shut on a drum to keep himself upright. While he held it he would have nothing else. And he would be kneeling at a post with his palm on it, working the registry's own craft, before forty people who meant to cross that river to the west as soon as it would carry them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:133:The boy had a line on her before she had finished shuddering, and the ferryman had another, and the drum's dog dropped for the last time. Seven people came over the rail onto the planks as if the planks had been bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:165:Cael knew that he had heard it before. He could not place it, for it was hoarse, and it had no room round it; every time that he had heard it there had been a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:171:Cael knew the stance before he knew the face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:173:He had seen a man stand so in a doorway in Ostrand, on a landing three flights up, before he would come into a room. He had seen it on a bluff, for three days, across four feet of table. It was how a person stood who had spent his whole life being sent into rooms to say what was in them, and who meant to be fair to the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:187:Lira was on his left. He did not need his eyes for what she was doing, since he had stood beside it on a ferry landing under a bluff, every time that they went down into Ostrand. There was a face that she kept for men in grey coats. She had made it long before he knew her, for assessors, and had worn it past two coats at the foot of a road more times than either of them could have numbered. It had the chin a little up, and the eyes a little to one side, and nothing in it that anybody could have written down. He felt her put it on at Seln's word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:189:And he felt it not fit. The man before her had a drover's coat on, with the river in it to the thighs, and it smelled of wet sheep. His hands were open at his sides, and by the colour of them they were bleeding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:193:"I had supposed," said Seln, as low as before, "that I was counting a desk. I do not yet know whether that is what the desk sent, or what left it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:215:He was reading the oak. Cael could tell that much by where the brown shape stood, and how long it stayed before it moved a pace to the right, and stood again. He was reading every slip, from the first tack to the last, as a man reads the notices in a district that he has been sent to inspect. Cael had done the same on the ninth day, and Teague did it at every homecoming, and nobody had told this man that it was what a person did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:221:"He came up before us," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:261:He did not ask to come sooner, or to sit with them until then. He made the short bow again, to nobody, and went across the yard to the stone trough by the gate, and lowered himself onto its rim with his bad leg out before him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:13:"If she goes down, somebody is to save that before he saves me," she said. "I am not making a joke."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:17:It could have found him that ledge. It would have come back dull and late through a yard of brown river, as it had through the water of a drowned hall, but it would have told him where the rock was and where it stopped; and he had it half open before he had weighed the thing. This was a road. He had a rule for roads, and he had broken it once on a hillside, after a trial, for a calf. Two hounds had gone down this same water a long while ago and had never been accounted for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:19:And there was a person standing on the shingle beside him, a Wind of the first rank in Iron, who had been finding floors with her feet since before he had anything in his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:29:"I'll find those with my boot. They're a hand deep. Carts have been cutting them since before anybody's grandmother." She sat down on a stone and pulled the laces of the great boot as tight as they would draw. "I go first. You put your feet where mine were. Nobody walks on the downstream side of the mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:47:They came up onto the far shingle streaming, a little before noon. She sat down where the stones were dry and reached for the ankle with both hands; she had only the one to hold it with, and he saw her remember that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:55:The bar of Thornwater's eastern gate was drawn for them before they had called, and on the platform beside it a lean man lifted two fingers from the stave of a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:59:The steading's woman had the bar in her two hands. She counted them in through her gate with her lips, as she had on the first night that they ever stood in it; and Cael saw her get to five, and go on looking down the road for a sixth, before she remembered who was inside her fence already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:79:"There's a loft, and there's a pot," said Oryn. "I eat now, and I'm on my back two hours, and I'll look at nobody tonight who isn't bleeding. In the morning, before that bench fills."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:119:Oryn regarded her. Something went over that square brown countenance which Cael had seen there only once, on the edge of a marsh, and it was gone before he could have put a name to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:127:It took the hour, which Brom counted by the shadow of the well's windlass on the flags. Karis sat on the coping with the roll of hide across her knees and did not once open her notebook, and Seln stood where he could see both gates. The lean man had come down off his platform, which Cael had never known him do, and waited a little way from the door with his stave grounded, looking at nothing. Cael watched the back of a healer who was spending, on a foot, what a child at some northern gate might want of her in a week; and he knew that she had weighed that before she pushed up her sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:Oryn stood at the table's end with her sleeves down. She had ordered them, by letter, to bring that thing, and she looked at it for a good while before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:173:"It is," said Karis. "You have seen three of those rings. You walked off the road at a fork before there was a fourth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:The purse had moved by two at the landing. The ferryman had been on his rope-house roof when they walked down the bank, on his knees, with his palm laid on Brom's shingles. It had kept a thaw out, and he said so, and five marks came up through Pike's hand before the supper was on, with a line said twice to go with them. Then he had looked at the brown mule, who was standing at his shed's door as though she had never left it; and he had asked at last. It was three, for the cold term, straw and standing, and he had named it without once lifting his eyes from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:257:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:287:He had begun a cold term in that doorway, with a mule and a frozen trough. The mule was there still, a hundred and thirty-nine days on, with her fee paid. The trough was open water. He went out to the end of the planks before he sat down, and gave the read the ground, as he had on the first morning; and he could feel the shelf going. It was grey, and soft, and full of water, and it took his weight on one side and let it drain away on the other. Out in the channel the river ran black from bank to bank between two rims of rotten ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:295:*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:53:"It is a copy," said Vastin. "I shall say so before I am asked, since she will ask. The line stands in a record that is older than the section it is filed in, in a room where I had the right to read and no right to copy. I copied it. I set down the word and the place exactly as they are written there, letter for letter, and what stands round them, so that nobody need take my account of where the line sits."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:75:"It is a title. It is not a name. A name is written as a name is; it does not take the article before it. A clerk writes *the registrar*, and *the keeper*, and he writes so of an office that somebody holds, and he does not trouble to say who held it." She drew breath. "I have read the Compact's paper from this year's forms back to the oldest charter that a certain house will let a person see. I have dated it by its stocks and by its seals, and I know four strata of it by the feel of their paper. I have never met that word in any stratum: not as a title, or as a trade, or as a thing that anybody was ever called."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:89:"Whatever stood before the first stroke," said Karis. "It is not on this page."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:91:"It is not on the page before it, either," said Vastin. "I read the pages on both sides; the entry begins here, and it ends here, and it goes on directly to a housing on the coast. Whoever wrote that line supposed that his reader would know what was meant. I did not, and I am the reader that he had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:95:Cael looked at the copied page, which lay a hand's width from the ends of his fingers. He had been sent for by paper before, in his life, more than once: by a summons, and by a challenge under a seal, and by an order for a room and an hour; and each of those had carried his name. This one had no name on it but a town's. The line it copied had been written a long while before he was born, by a clerk who was thinking about housings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:109:He laid it before her, under the lamp. Cael had it the wrong way up and a table's length off, and he could see no more than that there were four short lines, and that something black lay across the last of them. It was Lira who read it out, from the meal-sacks, since she was nearest, and since she had never in her life looked at a paper without moving her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:187:It was not a fall. Brom was off the door before it could be one, and had him by the upper arm, left-handed; Oryn's two hands had come up, and stopped in the air, since nobody had asked her for them. Vastin hung in a large man's grip, open-eyed, and as pale in the face as the paper on the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:193:She had got up. She was not half his weight, and she stood before him with her gloved hands at her sides and looked up into his face, as she would have looked at a seal under a glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:195:"You have had one bowl and half a loaf since yesterday. You held a boat this morning. You have stood at this table for the whole of a lamp, since you would not sit, and before that you sat on a stone trough for five hours with nothing to do, which I think was the hardest of them. You have handed over three things in the order that you meant to. I have them. I shall have them in the morning." She did not raise her voice. "There is nothing more for you to do tonight. You are to go up to your bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:199:It was the first thing that the healer had said to him since noon, and she had still not asked him anything. Vastin looked from the one woman to the other, and then down at the small one who stood before him. Cael thought that he had given that order a thousand times, in one form or another, to clerks who stayed too late; he had very likely not been given it since he was a junior with a file under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:271:Karis set a second point on the wick, without looking at it, and the flame stood up again. She had her own copy before her, with the ruled box in it, and she was not reading it: she was writing beneath it, very small, where the sheet itself now lay, and who had laid it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:5:It was a hand taller than she was, and as thick as her wrist. A person could not have swung it with one arm, or even carried it far. She took it up from the wet grass by its middle with her right hand, and then, slowly, as she had set her fingers to a reed the night before, she put her left upon it a yard lower down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:The right-hand end came first, low, at his knee. He took it on his stave and felt it through his elbow. The other end was already coming, over the top, for his left shoulder, and he got back out of its way. Then it was the right again, and the left, and he went round the square before her like a man backing away from a mill. She was quicker than he was, as she had always been. Her feet in that great boot found the ground under the frost as though they had laid it themselves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:He did not understand at first what she had done. She had slid her right hand down to the stave's heavy middle, and tucked the upper half of it along her forearm, as a person carries a pole through a doorway. She had a short stout stick now, in one hand. She came in with it very fast, and low. The thing darted at his thigh and was gone before his own wood got there, and came again at his wrist; and when he covered the wrist she went past him on the mended foot, turning, and rapped him smartly across the back of the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:71:It came out of her between her teeth. He had stopped already, and it was half a breath before he could make himself go on. In that half breath he watched her do the thing that she had come out there to do. The hand was shaking; she looked at it, once, as she might have looked at a dog that had run from a noise. The fingers closed again on the wood. They closed in their slow order, the first and then the next, and when the last was shut she heaved with the whole of that side, and the stave's lower end came round under his guard and thumped against his right ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:97:Wind had him half way to her before the fall was finished, and he told it no, and walked the four strides. He touched the toe of her boot with his stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:113:She did not pick the stave up by its middle this time. She took it as he had seen another person take a pole on that same ground, with her two hands a yard apart about its centre, so that she had as much wood before the leading hand as behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:127:Brom had got up off the bench. Beyond him the tall man lifted his spear's butt a hand's breadth from the turf and brought it down, and sat as he had sat before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:143:*Ephram. You lose because you run it alone. I lost to a seven-foot pole all the cold term, three to none and then three to one, and each time somebody on a bench told me what I'd done before I had wiped the snow off. Find a bench. Tell Rooke the yard out here is quieter too. He'd not say it was worse. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:It rained that night, for the first time since the snow, and in the morning Lira came to the board with her left arm held in against her and her mouth shut. A healer had told her that it would ache in the wet for a year. She did not speak of it. When Brom wanted the new hurdle lifted into its place between the shed's posts, she took one end of it in both hands before anybody else had stirred, and walked it round the drum with her jaw set, and lashed her end to its post herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:157:"The ferryman won't. The broker will. I buy it of the one and I carry it to the other, and then there's a line gone out of your book that's had no figure on it since before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:171:Twelve days before that boat, when the count at Lowmarch stood at two hundred and sixty-five, Vastin had come down to the last ford on the east road with a stick in his hand. He had never heard of the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:173:It was the day on which five people waded a river in flood to reach a healer's gate, a long way to the east of him. He knew nothing of that. He knew that his knee had begun to complain at about the hundredth mile and had left off being civil at the two hundredth. The water before him was cold, and came to the top of his boots, and would have to be walked through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:175:He walked through it. On the near shingle a flat boat lay with its bottom stove in, and two black posts leaned beside it. A board had been left face upward in the grass. The paint had gone out of its letters, but they had been cut before they were painted. *Foot. Horse. Cart, loaded.* At the head of it was the registry's seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:189:His hand went to the corner of the page. A register is turned back as a matter of course; an inspecting officer reads a year of it before he so much as asks for the keys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:211:"I am looking for five people," said Vastin, "who came east by this road before the last cold. Where would a man ask after them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:219:But she had turned the mule already, and was riding it forty yards down the bank before she let it drink. She had not asked him who he was. He stood on the shingle and understood that he had never before in his grown life spoken to a stranger who did not know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:5:He stood just within the post, on the hinge side, where a person coming in out of the light would be three steps into the room before he saw that anybody was there. The long board lay beyond him, by the hearth. Brom had six bowls on it and the ladle in the pot, and the stool stood at its end with nobody on it. Karis sat with her notebook shut under her hand. Oryn was on her feet beside the stool, and Lira on hers at the hearthstone, with her back to the fire and her arms folded, the right over the left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:7:Cael's eyes had given him back the far end of the room by then. He could see the keeper's casks, and the cloth on its nail, with the black still wet on it. The board before him was coming back more slowly, and the faces along it had as yet no more edge than faces seen through steam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:25:Cael turned the four sentences over while the stick came across the floor, since he had never heard that man waste one. A person who arrived in this country with a seal came over the board's head: he sent a clerk to the plank, or a paper, and had himself posted. This one had been told three times to go to twelve feet of oak, and had gone, and had read it from the left-hand post as any carter's boy would have; he had been asked what he had done, and had answered for himself, and had been taken at his word. He had paid a copper and a half for a loaf that cost the meadow a copper. The board had done to him what it did to everybody. Seln had wanted to know, before he stood away from a door, whether the man had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:31:He was looking at the stool. It had a bowl before it, and steam going up from the bowl, and nobody near it but a woman who was plainly not sitting down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:35:He said it with perfect gravity, and nobody at the board laughed. Vastin considered the stool, and the bowl, and the healer; he gave the matter the attention that he would have given a point of procedure which he had not met before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:41:He sat. It took him some time, with the leg, and he did it without putting a hand on anybody's shoulder. The keeper had come to his cask-room door to look at a stranger at the standing crew's board; Vastin lifted a finger to him, and asked what a bowl was, and put a copper down on the wood before Brom had lifted the ladle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:Brom filled the bowl, and set it before him, and looked at the copper. It was a thing that he could weigh, and he seemed to find the weight correct.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:85:"I shall say the short thing first, since everything after it leans upon it. I hold no office." He did not raise his voice, and it had begun to lose its hoarseness. "I left the seal of mine in a tray, some months ago, and came down a stair. I gave no notice of it, and I was given no leave; whether that is a resignation or a man walking out of his place, the office will have settled by now, and I did not stay to be told which. I have no rank that will open a door for you, and no seal that will shut one. Nobody sent me, and nobody has sanctioned a word of what I am going to say; you had better know that before you believe any of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:105:Cael heard the fire tick. He had no way round it either. He had seen that man keep a record honest for a year, and he had heard a quiet voice say in a reading room, before the Line, that a kindness in a lent room is how the third grade is done well. Both were true. He could not have ruled between them on anything that had yet been said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:119:"I didn't say proof. I said what it costs. I'll take a thing that costs that much a long way before I ask to see its seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:125:She did not make anything of it. She walked round the board's end, behind the stool, and sat down on the bench beside Brom, opposite the brown coat; and she put her left hand out on the wood before her, open, and looked across it at the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:145:"Under every station on the continent. I said so at a table before we left Ostrand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:221:"I know that you would, and you are right to. But I have carried it three hundred miles without saying it aloud, and I would have you meet it as it stands on the page before you meet it in my mouth. I am not a witness to what it means. I should only be putting my voice between you and the page."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:245:Cael saw it happen, and it took him a breath to understand what he was seeing. The man had finished. He had come three hundred miles to say perhaps a dozen sentences at a board, and he had said them before the pot was cold; and his right hand had gone out, a little way, across the wood, to the place where a pen would lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:271:The small purse came out on its cord, and a fourth coin went down on the wood. Vastin looked at it for a moment after his fingers had left it, as he had looked at the cut half of the other. It came to Cael that the man had very likely never paid for a bed in his life. An office had sent a clerk before him to every inn on every road that he had travelled, and the reckoning had gone home in a bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:283:She had sat the whole time with her left hand open before her, as she had laid it there when she came from the hearth. She had asked this man a question once before, in a room in Ostrand, with her ankle strapped, and had been answered so exactly that she had nodded. She did not dress this one either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:"She'll not bear a cat before the new moon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:51:He took a count at the start of anything; it was the one habit that he had brought east whole. He had never before come to the start of a thing and found the count made already, with nothing in it that would move for a season, and he did not know what a person did with that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:59:They were in the store behind the chimney at noon, the five of them, which was two more than it had been built to hold. Karis had the table. Lira sat on a meal-sack with the great boot stuck out before her, and Brom filled the doorway, since there was nowhere else that he would go. Seln had the sill of the one small window, and had contrived to take up no room on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:101:"There is a thing that I am counting as well, and I had better say so once. A packet went over that river yesterday in a clerk's bag. I know the road that it takes, and the kind of desk that it stops at, and about how long such a desk sits on a paper before it writes another. Nothing can come back while the ice holds." He looked down at the landing. "On the day that it goes, something can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:Karis took out the purse. She had paid six to the keeper without looking, four days before, with her eyes on a door. Today she opened her notebook first, and did the sum, and did it again beneath; then she counted twenty marks onto the tarred oak in four heaps of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:283:He ruled a line under that, and began a new leaf. He headed it as he had never headed one before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:"He dug every hole before the ground shut," said the factor. "Forty-one of them. Stoned them over, and stuffed them, so the frost shouldn't get down. I never asked him to. He'd have had the posts in as well, only he went off south after somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:31:He looked along forty-one stones. A captain had been called off a job by a feeling in the back of his neck, and before he went he had made certain that whoever came after him would find the ground open; it was the kind of thing that never got said at a plank, and it was a good part of why that man's name stood at the head of a sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:35:Cael had taken them for boulders. They lay along the eastern end of the row of stones, close in under the rock band, where no snow had drifted: a line of long grey backs, keeled, each as broad as a cart and roofed with plates that overlapped like slates. Snow lay in the seams between the plates. He had last seen such backs on a ledge over a bay, on the seventy-eighth day, a breath before a mountain came down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:51:Karis read out her page in the yard on the hundred and fortieth morning, before it was properly light, with her back against the byre wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:She had Seln pace it along the outside of the standing fence. From the yard gate to the nearest grey back it came to three hundred and fifty yards, which was the distance at which she had once lit a fire under a ledge and seen nothing stir. Brom and the mule dragged the factor's hay sledge up the hill to the last of Teague's posts, and Lira sat on it with the bow across her knees. She had seventeen heads before her there, a hundred and fifty yards off, and the whole of the slope beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:They cleared it from the top side, as Lira had said, working east from a place twenty yards beyond the old cow, who lay last in the row. Seln and Cael had the keeper's shovels. Brom had lashed a barn board across the end of a pole, and he drove it before him left-handed, with the pole's butt in the pit of his good arm, so that it took off a yard of snow at a push. Karis came behind with a besom and swept what the shovels left. She had Hesk's clock on a stone, and a peg in the snow at every tenth yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:117:It was an hour before anything on that hill moved but themselves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:235:He stood at the end of Teague's run before they lifted a stone, and looked back along the standing posts with one eye shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:245:It was on the second afternoon that Cael saw Seln at the other end of the fence, with a length of twine stretched from the gatepost to the last of Teague's posts, walking along it with his head bent. Somewhere about the middle he stopped, and stood before two posts for the time that he would have given to a paper. Then he coiled his twine, and came all the way back along the hill, and said something to Brom that took perhaps eight words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:261:Seln was looking at the hill. He had told one person, and he had chosen which. Cael understood why it had not been the one with the book; a thing that went into that book against a man's name would stay there, and Seln had spent fifteen years watching what became of names in books. Teague had dug forty-one holes for somebody else's posts before the frost. Two of his own stood a hand out of true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:265:They pinned the far side on the hundred and forty-fourth, with the herd mowing a grass tongue a furlong above them, and on the next morning the factor walked his line from the gate to the last rail. He kicked six posts. He counted the new ones with his tally stick, twice, as a man counts who has been cheated before; and then he looked up the hill at seventeen grey backs, for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:She showed them how it was done. She was a long time about it, since she had never before had anybody to show. The brass eye went over the peg. The cord was run out to its last knot, which was the fourth, and drawn until it had left the snow along the whole of its length and no tighter. A pebble was set down at the end. Then the peg was lifted, and carried to the pebble, and driven there, and the cord was run out again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:47:She set them. She drove the peg herself. He did no more than run the cord out and hold its end; and when the slip of ash had been laid down once from the fourth knot, there was room for only half of it again before the line cut in the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:57:She had them in the store behind the chimney before the cord was coiled. She cleared the deal with her forearm. The older strip of brass came out of its cloth, and she laid it down on the bare wood, and pressed a needle into the table at each of its two ends, so that there were two bright pricks in the deal exactly as far apart as the strip was long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:103:"I can think of a cause for the long one. Brass that has been in a warm hand is longer than brass that has not. I can think of a cause for the short one, if I try. I cannot think of one cause that will do for both, in the same hand, in a quarter of an hour." She laid the hair down in the fold of her notebook. "And I have a rule about this. I have had it since before any of you knew me, and I said it in that yard an hour ago, to him, as a rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:She had done this once before, on the hundred and sixteenth evening, at a fire, on the day after that cord came up out of the ground. She had the figure in her trials leaf. She did not look at it until she had her new one written down, since a person who knows what she is hoping to find will find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:135:She had not made it for brass. She had made it as a girl, in a hall with a counter in it, on the day when a registrar laid a card before her that said *Iron*, and a numeral, and *certified*. The girl beside her had wept at hers. Karis had read her own twice, and had asked what instrument it had been taken with, and how that instrument was kept true; and the registrar had looked at her as at a child who inquires what the sky is fastened to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:137:Nobody had been able to tell her, and so she had worked it out for herself on the walk home, and had found that it steadied her wonderfully. The card was not a fact about Karis; it was a fact about a plate, and a table of figures, and whoever had last adjusted them. A ruler said what a ruler said. If it said different things of the same length, then somebody had a bad ruler, and the length was exactly what it had been before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:181:She had never in her life wanted an object as she wanted that page. It was not only that it would save her a year. She had been right before, in an annex, with three leaves on a trestle before it was light; she had been right, and it had made no difference, since she had had nothing to show a seat but her own rubbings and her own word. Stone proved that marks existed. It took a text to say what they meant, and it took a text that somebody else had kept to make anybody believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:193:Under it she began a list. The lamp burned down a finger's breadth before she had the fourth line, and she set a second point on the wick without noticing that she had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:223:"It did before I borrowed it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:Teague's three had not spoken, and their captain was not even among them. He stood by the bench with his thumbs in his belt. He had looked at a place on the trodden snow, once, before the word was given. When it was given the spear was standing in that place, and the bow was where the spear had been, and the boy had the sack over the pegs and was waiting to be told whether he might put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:245:Cael was looking past him at what had been a road. The ferryman had pulled his green six days before, bough by bough, and stood the boughs against his rope-house. The ice had gone grey since, and then dull, and a carter who tried it against advice had put a wheel through at the landing's foot and been laughed at. This morning the two shelves stood white along the banks, and between them ran a lane of black water as wide as the scow was long, with cakes of ice turning in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:249:The light had come back before the warmth did. Then the warmth came, by day, and the eaves ran. At night it froze again, so that every morning the meadow was a sheet of glass with last year's grass standing up through it. Lira walked on it with her foot set down whole and her eyes on the ground. She had not fallen since a road in the frost, and she did not mean to begin. Each evening she went down to the gravel bar and stood looking at the saddle to the east, where the snow still lay to the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:271:"I am going to say a thing to you whole, before I say it to anybody else. You are the person whom it costs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:275:"In pieces. I had not the right to say all of it until I had looked in every other place where the answer might lie, and I have looked. It is not in fifty-two sheets. It is not in that case, or in his book." She had a leaf before her with a heading on it and eleven lines. She did not read from it. "Whoever cut those courses did not think in stone. Stone is where a thing is put when it is finished. It was written first; and what is written is kept; and the oldest keeping that I know of is on the far side of that river, inside a wall, under an office of the registry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:289:Lira had been ready since before it was light. The bow was on her back, with its one bolt, and the sling was knotted high at her collar-bone as a healer had left it at a fork. She had not spoken of that arm since a strip of bark was read out at a hearth, and she did not speak of it now. She went up the saddle road first, on the whole of her foot, and the rest of them followed her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:3:Vastin gave Lira the whole of his attention before he answered, and Cael had seen him do that once before, to the same girl, in a room in Ostrand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:"There are three reasons," he said. "I had a long road to put them in order, and I shall give you them so. The first is a thing that you did not do. Five people laid a case in front of me that would have shaken any officer in the Compact, and when I had read it, not one of them asked me for anything. I remarked on it at the time. I have been asked for something by every person who ever set a paper before me; it is what the paper is for. You asked nothing. You let me go down a stair with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:25:Lira thought about it. Her left hand lay open before her, and she shut it, one finger after another, and nodded, as she had nodded in Ostrand. He had not dressed the answer for her, and she seemed to find that she could stand on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:"I should not care to have it thought that I walked here to lay a knee before a healer. It was sound when I set out, and it has carried me, and I have no claim on anybody's purse for it. I offer it to nobody. I had rather that were understood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:45:She said it without any heat. She had a rule, and it was of her own making: she put her question to a person before she put her hands on him, each time, and took the answer. This man had answered a thing that she had not yet put. So nothing had been asked, by her reckoning, and nothing refused; the matter lay exactly where it had lain before she opened her mouth. She drew the sleeve down. Cael saw her eyes go once more to the leg, and stay a breath, and come away; and he thought that the knee had not heard the last of that woman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:47:He carried a piece of her Tide himself, the tenth of the ten, under a rule that he had written down in her hearing: he asked before he read anybody, every time. Two fingers on that knee and a count of ten would have told him how it lay. He had not asked, and he did not mean to. A reading would have mended nothing, and the man had said that he offered the knee to nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:65:"It's why I asked you. A hundred and sixty-three days ago six people stood on a rim, over a place called the Stair, with crossbows. They'd cloths across their faces. No seals, no word, nothing you could have written down. One of them put a bolt in him. They knew which hole we were in before we'd climbed out of it." She leaned across the board. "You say nothing of yours crosses the Line. Something did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:Karis had stopped writing some while before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:103:"You had forty years' standing. You had a seal that every seat in the Compact was bound to receive, and the right to be heard by a convening on any finding that you chose to lay before it. There are councils that sit for exactly such a thing as this. You could have put three papers on a table under your own hand, and made them minute every line." She did not soften it. "You gave up the seal, and you carried them on your back to a board at a ferry. I am not asking why you left. I am asking why you did not use what you had before you left it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:105:"Because a council would file it," Vastin said, "and I spent forty years being the man they sent to make sure the filing held. I know exactly how deep a thing has to be buried before no one goes looking. This is deeper than that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:109:"I helped bury smaller things. I'd like to dig one up before I'm done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:111:Karis did not write it down. She sat with her fingers laced, as still as she sat before a sheet that she had not expected to be able to read; and Cael, who had kept his own question, did not grudge the keeping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:157:Vastin had been on the stone trough by the gate since the board was cleared, with his leg out before him and his hands on the stick's head, looking at nothing that Cael could find; and he had got up at the sight of two men with a load between them. He came across the yard faster than that knee ought to have let him, and put out his raw right hand to the box's nearer becket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:201:"There are three things. I settled their order on the road, and I shall keep to it, since each of them wants the one before. The first is the largest, and I think that it matters least. The second is a single line. The third I do not understand, and I have brought it because I do not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:247:Then she did a thing that was very like her. She went back through the whole row from its first sheet, quickly, looking at nothing but the heads; and each time that she met a group of four signs which she had not met before, she laid one finger of her left glove down upon the deal. She could not read a group. She could tell one from another, as a person with no letters can still tell his own mark from his neighbour's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:263:"There is one sheet more in that flat," said Vastin. "It lies under the sixth row, and it is not the office's. I should prefer that you had counted the rest before you saw it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:15:She went in with a slap, and sat down in the water, and swung to the length of his line with her blunt bow pointing up the river. The ferryman watched how she lay before he walked out along the landing, and made her fast at both ends with his own hands, and stood looking up at his cable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:"Out of yours. I've my own." He pointed with his chin at a cake of ice the size of a barn door, which was coming down the middle of the channel with a whole young alder frozen into it by the roots. "She hangs on that cable by two blocks. One of those takes a block off me, and I've a boat going down to the mill sideways with whoever's in her. I'll not hang her till the river's done sending them. That's four days of ice yet off the top holds, and after the ice there's the water that was under it." He counted on his thumb. "The two hundred and seventy-seventh, at first light, if she drops in the night. Not before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:73:Nobody laughed aloud, since the healer was standing there. But the stool was the mule's from that hour, and it kept its place at the board's end; and Cael noticed that Oryn, having refused it, stood a little nearer to it than she had stood to anything of theirs before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:97:"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:155:From the left-hand post Cael could see the wash-house door, which stood open, as every door of hers did, and through the forenoon he saw what a cold term had sent down to her. A carter went in first, on another man's arm, with one foot bound in sacking; and Cael heard her say to him what she said to each of them, before she had so much as bent: what she could do, and what she could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:161:There was a child from the top hold with a chest that had rattled since the first snow, and Oryn was a long while over her, and came to the door when it was done with one hand on the post. There was the heavy carter with the grey beard. He had been brought off the river ice on a hurdle a hundred and eleven days ago, and he had coughed ever since; he sat down before her as a man sits who expects to be charged. And there was the hide-woman, who held out two hands cracked across every knuckle, like old harness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:175:A lean old woman in a cap came down the bank from the house on the rise, a little before noon, with her staff under her arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:179:She did not look into the wash-house before she turned, and went back up the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:237:"I don't know," he said. "There's a thing that stands before the bearing, and I've not ruled on it, and I'll not rule till that boat's in. It may be days. It may be a good many."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:251:They wove the hurdle that evening on the shed's floor, by the lantern, which hung from its nail by the chain. Cael had driven nine hazel rods into the beaten earth of the horse-walk in a row, a hand apart, to be its bones. Lira sat on her heels at one end and he at the other, and they passed the reeds in and out between the rods, turn about, each of them beating a finished course down with the edge of a hand before the next was begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:265:It was two-handed work, and she had known that when she sat down to it. A reed had to be bent round the end rod and held hard while the next was brought through, or the whole course sprang. She held with the left. He saw her set that hand on the first reed as she would have set it on something hot; the fingers closed, slowly, each a little after the one before, and the arm above them, which was still no thicker than a rail, began to shake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:15:Karis looked at the heap when it was laid, and then put both her hands under her arms and stood back from it. Brom felt in his coat. He brought out a steel and a flint and a twist of charred rag, which Cael had not seen in his fingers since before a small pale woman joined them, and he knelt and struck. It took him four tries.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:25:"She is right," said Seln. He had not sat; he stood at the hedge's end, where he could see the road in both directions, with his hands in his sleeves. "Those two will be at that gate before us in the morning, and a carter talks while he waits. *There was a woman at the three-mile who lit a fire by looking at it.* It is not a crime; it is a line for somebody to write."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:41:He took Hesk's book out of his coat, and then put it back. There was a thing that he wished to do before he wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:Nothing came. It was not a silence, as he had been used to call it, since a silence is a thing that is heard. The tenth simply had no way of going where nothing went. He had the same nothing that he had had at noon, and that he had had three times before on other people's floors; and he knew it at last for what it was. He had laid a foot-rule against a smell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:75:The old man looked at the bank, and he looked at it for longer than a bank deserved: it was two feet of turf, sloping, with a ditch below it on the road's side. Then he looked at his right leg, which stuck out before him in its linen and its two laths like a thing that belonged to somebody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:"I have a matter to put before you," he said. "I find that I will not do it standing over you, since I have said a great many things to people from that height, and you were one of the people. So I am going to sit down. I tell you beforehand, since I shall not be able to get up again without you, and I had rather that you knew it now than learned it afterward."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:85:It was the first time that Cael had seen him lower than his own two feet since a cut on a moor, where he had gone down because nothing was left in him to stand with. This was not that. He had chosen it. A man who had stood for forty years at the upper end of every room had put himself where he could not rise without the arm of a boy whom the registry had no column for; and he had done it before he said a word, so that it could not afterward be taken back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:"I read it for the first time in the week before I came up your bluff, on a road, by the candles of four inns, and for the last time on the morning when I left a seal in a tray. Between those two mornings I read it oftener than I have read anything that I did not write myself. I should like you to hear what was in it, since you have only ever seen it upside down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:101:"It began with a day, and a word in square brackets, and after those two lines it was instruments: a registrar at a market town, with a counter and a pen; wardens, who came and stood in a yard and wrote; a panel; the frames of a house on a hill, at the end of every term, and the officers who watched the frames. Each of them laid upon you whatever he had been issued, and wrote down what it showed him, and what it showed him went under the last sheet and was sent up." The left hand moved on the stick. "There was not a paper in that folder which was not somebody's reading of you. I have handled a great many files, and I had not before met one in which the subject had been asked nothing and measured so often."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:113:"Four feet. I measured it, as I measure everything." His voice had not altered at all, and it was that which made it hard to listen to. "I brought to those three days whatever forty years had given me: I have stood on eleven fields, and made something over nine thousand evaluations, and I can tell a man's rank from the way that he takes a stair, before he has reached the head of it. At the end of the third day I went down your hill with nothing whatever. The plate had lain as flat as a dish; every question had been answered, civilly, and I believe that every answer was true; and I could not have built one finding upon the whole of them. I had never in my life come away from a person without a figure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:139:The old man was not looking at him. He was looking at his own right leg, laid out before him like a plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:165:The light was nearly gone. Cael looked along the bank at a brown coat and a pair of bound hands, and found that there was a thing in his own keeping which he was going to give this man. It surprised him. He had meant it for the book, and after the book for Lira, who read the book. But he had been paid tonight in a coin that he had not handled before, and he owned exactly one piece of the same mint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:171:"It was read again today. Twice. Once behind a hedge at a brook, and once here, against this stone, just before you came along."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:177:He had done the same once before, on a bluff, with a notebook lying shut on a table between them and one more question plainly in reach. Cael had taken it then for a thing that the man had missed, and he knew better now: it had been looked at and left where it lay, and tonight it was being left again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:225:He set the day at the head, since he could not write anything without it, and the place: the three-mile post. Then he put down what he owed, before what he had found, as an old man in Denvash had taught him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:227:*Lira asked whether it was the thing I was told to leave alone. I said beside it. I read a healer's order for the leave I wanted in it. I'd do it again, and she's to be told so at the mill, by me, before anything else is said. Round, both times. Not in. Nothing taken up to it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:243:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Myself, twice: not counted, as before.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:261:"If he does, I'll not mind. I'd sooner somebody had it who could do something with it. I'd only have sat on it." She pulled her knees up, and looked at the fire and not at him. "And the old man had it before the book did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:79:She had the next stone up before they had found their feet. She lifted it with both arms. The left came a little after the right, as it did in everything, and the slope did not care which hand had been late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:97:He did not lift the foot. Cael saw the boot stay where it had been set, on the line, with the whole weight of a beast as heavy as a calf dragging at the leg above it. The bandaged hands went white on the stick. From bank to bank the plane stood as flat and as still as it had stood before, with the frost lying on it. Beyond it a hound that the slide had carried down was running at the bank again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:155:He stood behind another man's wall and could not so much as lift a hand. He had trusted his feet and his skin to a great many things in his life: to a count, to a read of a pace and a half, to his own judgment of a floor. He had never before trusted them to something held up by another person's knee, and a person whom he had known as an officer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:215:"Nobody's helping you to do anything," said Brom. He had lowered himself onto the edge of his stone, which was a thing that he was allowed. He had his right foot out before him with the boot still on it. The upper was torn across from the lacing to the welt. "It had my boot. I said that I was wrapped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:3:Oryn came up the cut on the dry edge, at the pace that she kept for a gate with a bell ringing, and she stopped at her stride and a half and looked the whole of it over before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:She knelt, and slit the frieze with a small knife, from the knee to the boot's top, and laid it back; and she told him what she was going to do before she did it, as she told every person who had ever lain on her plank. There were four holes and a tear, none of them deep, since a stiff leg in a thick coat is a poor mouthful. Those she would close. It would take her a quarter of an hour, and it would feel like cold water poured into the bone. The joint was another matter. It had been wrung like a cloth, and she did not mean to spend on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:49:When she had done, she was grey about the mouth. She had spent on four small holes what a child at some northern gate might want of her within the week, and she had weighed that before ever she knelt, as she weighed every spending; it was why the joint was to have linen and a month, and nothing more. She took linen from the mule's pannier without getting up, and two laths, and bound the knee from above the joint to below it, turn upon turn, until it would bend no more than a post. Then she reached up, and he gave her his wrist, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:101:The twenty was counted out on the top bar of the gate, in four heaps of five, and a thumb was set to the foot of Pike's slip, where it left a mark like a crook. Cael had been paid at a good many gates by then, and he had learned to read a hold by how it paid. The factor at the axe-head had counted twice, like a man who has been cheated before; this one counted once, and did not look at the heaps afterward, like a person who has got exactly what was wanted and means to think no more about the price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:169:He had not known that he was going to say it. It was out before he had weighed it, at a board, to a man who had once sat across a table from him with a file; and nobody at that board looked surprised but himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:"He took every word of that," she said. "About the month. He heard it, and he told me that he couldn't undertake it. And he's not knelt since, or gone faster than a walk, or leaned on anything but that stick." Her spoon went round the bowl. "I've had patients who said *yes* to everything and did none of it. I never had one before who said *I cannot undertake*, and then did it to the letter, as if it were a thing that he'd thought of himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:265:"It is the table that I have been looking for since the night before last. I said that I should count in daylight. I have carried six years of an office's paper for two days up a road and through a fight, and I have not yet turned a sheet of it." She had the flat out from under her arm already. "I want the forenoon. I want pebbles, a great many, about the size of a hen's egg, since there is a wind. And I want every person here who can read to wash his hands in that basin first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:3:Seln had taken the sheet an hour before, from the foot of the second row, while a small pale woman was reading four words aloud from the foot of another; and it had troubled him a little that it was still so easy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:5:He had lifted papers out of heaps under their owners' eyes for the better part of his working life. One did not snatch. One was seen to be handling the heap for an honest reason, as he had been, reading six rows for what they left out. One's left hand squared the sheets while the right went on turning them. The sheet was in his sleeve before the pebble was back on its corner. Nobody at the slab had looked up. They were the only six people living from whom he had never before taken anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:13:It stood at the head of a file. It was the first line of the first sheet, after a name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry sets down the day on which a person was classed. The file had lain open on a good many desks at a house above Ostrand, in the years when he was employed there to carry such things from one desk to another, and to be overlooked. He had copied that line twice with his own hand, for two different officers. A clerk forgets a face in a week. He does not forget a line that he has copied twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:He did not look down at the strap across his chest. He had carried that case for twelve years, shut, and it was shut now. He had found before today that he could not hold two closed things up to the light in one morning. He made no ruling upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:43:He considered it with care, since he distrusted his own distaste. If he said it at the slab, seven people would have it in the same breath. Karis would be obliged to enter it; she entered everything. She would then be obliged to refuse the sentence that followed from it, aloud, with the boy standing there, and he had watched what that refusal cost her once already today. A man who had certified readings for forty years would hear a day named, and his mouth would open on an order before he could prevent it. A healer would hear it from the basin. And the boy would be told one more thing about himself, in public, by a clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:57:That was all. The boy could read a registry date; he had read them upside down on other people's desks for as long as Seln had known him. He knew this one a great deal better than Seln did, since every paper that had ever been served on him began with it. If the sheet lay before him he would see what it was, or he would not look. If he did not look, it was not yet his to carry. If he did, then he might write it or burn it, and tell five people or none. Nobody alive would have the right to ask him what he had made of it. Seln would least of all, having said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:73:He had not thought of it. He had taken one sheet from the foot of one row, to be sure of a thing before he did anything with it. He had forgotten that she never trusted a count until a second person had made it. She had put that row into the one pair of hands in which a cord had read short. He saw those hands now. They lay flat on the rock, on either side of the heap, and they had gone the colour of the paper. The boy was looking at them as a man looks at a tool that has failed him twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:He crossed the turf, and made no more noise than he ever did. He set the sheet down between those two hands, with its head away from the boy, as every paper had lain before him in his life. He squared it with one finger. Then he went back to the water, and stood with his face to the mule. He counted the sound of the spring, since a man who has placed a thing does not watch it found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:Cael saw that before he saw anything else, and for a breath it steadied him. A clerk who set a paper before a person turned it to face him. This one had been turned the other way, with its printed head toward the far side of the rock, as papers had lain on a registrar's counter at Denvash, and on a magister's desk, and on a table on a bluff. It was how he had learned to read. Somebody had noticed that, and had laid the sheet so on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:127:It had Hesk's book half out before he knew what it was about. He had written down everything that had ever happened to him. It was how an old man had taught him to keep from being told what he was: one noted the day, and the count, and the trend. He had the pencil. He had the place, under yesterday's line.

